window.SNDSTATION_DOCS = [{"u":"index.html","t":"sndstation documentation","s":"","h":["sndstation documentation","Two places for docs","For users","For developers","For people who build with coding assistants","Writing a page"],"x":"sndstation documentation sndstation turns small Linux handhelds into music machines. Shops sell these units as cheap game consoles, and many end up in a drawer. Each one holds a capable ARM computer. sndstation is the ecosystem that puts it to work for musicians: an OS image, or a package for a Debian system a unit already runs, with a launcher, an SDK, plugins, and updates. David Viens at Plogue started the project in 2026. Status: private beta. No unit has passed its checks, no image is public, and the API is 0.x. This repository is private until the docs are ready. Two places for docs This repository holds the guides, sorted by who reads them: users, developers, and people who build with coding assistants. Most are stubs today. Each stub says what it will cover and where to look until then. sndstation/sndstation holds the reference that sits next to the code: the SDK, the launcher, and each subsystem. This repository links to those pages and does not copy them. For users Find a supported unit. sndstation runs on RK3326, RK3566, H700, and A133P handhelds. A shop listing names the model, not the chip: supported hardware lists the models, and Identifying your unit says how to tell which one you hold. On an RK3326 unit, read it before you flash. To choose one to buy, the device guide lists the units we have validated and ranks the rest on paper. Get a microSD card of your own. Do not flash the card the unit came with. Put it aside as it is: it boots the stock firmware, so it shows whether a fault is in the hardware or in what you flashed. For which card to buy, and how to check it for a fake, we defer to Handheld Index's guide to SD cards . Flash the image for your unit with balenaEtcher : pick the image, pick the card, flash. For the writing itself we defer to balena's instructions and support. Which image a unit takes is in Getting an image . During the private beta no image is public. An image is not the only way in. The platform also installs as a .deb onto an equivalent \"Lite\" Debian trixie system: the stock Raspberry Pi OS Lite images and Armbian are that. Switchroot's L4T-based Linux for the Switch is on Ubuntu 24.04, which is close enough. See Installing on a handheld . 4. Put the card in and switch on. The unit boots to the launcher. Set up the network in SETTINGS → Network: Wi-Fi where the unit has it, or USB networking to a computer where it does not. 5. Make music. The user guide is a stub; until it is written, the launcher describes every screen. Before buying anything to plug in, Peripherals says what works on your unit: interfaces and MIDI, controllers, drives, Wi-Fi dongles, webcams. For developers A CLAP plugin built for Linux arm64 against Debian trixie's glibc loads on a unit as it is. The launcher finds it, checks it, and rates it ( CLAP plugins ). To go further, pick a route: Write an app. Writing an app takes examples/hello from a copy on your computer to a unit's launcher. The quickstart is the short version, and the headers are in the API reference . Give a plugin the unit's panel and controls. embedded-hardware.md describes the host side of the clap.embedded-hardware extension. A plugin that uses it does not depend on sndstation's code. Publish packages. Publishing a vendor source . Guides: Setting up a unit for development Building on macOS with Docker Optimizing for small ARM chips Building on a Linux desktop, on macOS, and on a handheld is in Installation . For people who build with coding assistants sndstation does nothing special for coding assistants. An app written with one uses the same SDK, the same docs, and the same units as any other. We use these tools ourselves. What we do with their output is question it, and hold it to the standard of any developer on the team: a person reads every line, runs it, and measures it on a unit. Shipping an app with a coding assistant , a stub. AGENTS.md is what an assistant reads before it works in the sndstation repository. A change you send to sndstation itself follows the AI use policy : you are its editor. Writing a page Docs are in American English and follow the rules in sndstation's AI_POLICY.md and .vale.ini : plain sentences, no filler, and facts someone measured. Start a how-to from the guide template . A stub keeps its Stub note until the page is written.","stub":false},{"u":"users/identify-your-unit.html","t":"Identifying your unit","s":"Users","h":["Identifying your unit","Which family","What you have to get right, by family","Device trees and overlays","RK3326: find the device tree before you flash","Why RK3326 is different","Find it from the original card","When it is not an RK3326 at all","Buying an RK3326 unit","H700: match the RAM, then the model","Once sndstation runs"],"x":"Identifying your unit For: someone holding a handheld who does not know which model or chip it is, or which device tree it needs. sndstation picks what to run by the unit's chip , not by the name on the box. A shop names the model, and two units sold under one name can hold different boards. Find the family first, then, for an RK3326 unit, find the exact board. RK3326 takes most of this page, because it is the hard case. Which family Family Sold as RK3326 the RG351 series, R33S, R36S and its clones, R40XX, RGB10, K36, XU10 RK3566 the RG353 series, RGB30, RK2023, X55 H700 the RG35XX series, RG40XX, RG34XX, RG28XX, RGcubeXX A133 TrimUI Smart Pro, Brick, Brick Pro Supported hardware lists the private-beta units and the chip in each, and Peripherals says what each family takes over USB. The label printed on the circuit board is a good second check. On an R36S it is six screws away, with the battery unplugged first. The label names the board, not the screen panel, and it can be faked (see When it is not an RK3326 ). What you have to get right, by family No card works out which board it is in. Every card carries all of its family's device trees in dtbs/ and their overlays in overlays/ , and one file on the card's FAT partition names which to use. What you have to find out before you set that file differs by family: RK3326 H700 What differs between units the board : panel, joypad, audio, keys. One model name can cover many boards the RAM , DDR3 or DDR4. The model name fixes the board What you choose a device tree, plus any overlays it needs the image for the unit's RAM, then the device tree for the model Where it is set config.txt : fdtfile= and overlays= extlinux/extlinux.conf : FDT and FDTOVERLAYS How you find out the device tree on the original card, through DTB Detective the model, then the unit's RAM ( H700 ) What a wrong choice does a dark screen, wrong buttons, or no sound the wrong RAM hangs before the kernel starts RK3566 sits between them: pick the device tree for the model in extlinux/extlinux.conf , like H700, and a few boards (X55, X35S, RG-DS) take a different bootloader. The A133 units have one board each, so there is nothing to pick. Device trees and overlays A device tree ( .dtb ) describes a whole board, and a unit boots from exactly one. An overlay ( .dtbo ) is a small patch applied on top of that device tree: it changes one part, such as the panel, and leaves the rest as it was. An overlay never replaces the device tree. The right device tree with a missing overlay still leaves that one part wrong. Overlays are applied in the order they are listed, after the device tree. RK3326 is the family that needs them most. Two you will meet there: mipi-panel.dtbo , for a unit whose screen is not the one its device tree expects. The USB OTG overlay. The RK3326 device trees taken from stock cards, all but the GKD Pixel 2's, leave the OTG port switched off. Without the overlay, USB networking binds but nothing reaches the cable. RK3326: find the device tree before you flash Why RK3326 is different Hardkernel's ODROID-GO Advance was the first of these. Anbernic cloned it, then Powkiddy, then many more shops, and each one used its own parts: a different panel, joypad, audio chip, or key wiring. By one count there are more than 70 named RK3326 variants, not counting the R36S clones, and new names turn up every week. \"R36S\" alone can mean dozens of boards, each with its own device tree. The handhelds.wiki RK3326 list and its R36S clones page track them. The device tree (a .dtb file) tells the kernel which parts the board has. With the wrong one, the screen stays dark, the buttons are wrong, or there is no sound. The RK3326 image boots on the first try only on an Anbernic RG351V. On any other RK3326 unit, find its device tree first. Find it from the original card Keep the card the unit came with. Do not flash it or wipe it. It holds the stock device tree, and it is the only proof of which board you have. Copy the .dtb files off it. Put the card in a computer and look for files ending in .dtb on its partitions. A card can hold several. Only one is loaded, and which one can depend on the card's start-up scripts, so copy them all. Upload them to DTB Detective . It reads the panel, joypad, audio, and keys out of a device tree and shows the closest known device, with the evidence. It names the device tree to use under ROCKNIX and sndstation, and the overlays to apply on top of it , if any. Treat a match as a strong hint, not a verdict. The site keeps what you upload. Set both on the sndstation card. Before the first boot, open config.txt on the card's FAT partition. Name the device tree on the fdtfile= line, and any overlays on the overlays= line, in the order to apply them: fdtfile=rk3326-gameconsole-r36s.dtb overlays=mipi-panel.dtbo Both must be files the card already holds, in dtbs/ and overlays/ . To move the card to another RK3326 board, edit these two lines; there is nothing to copy. 5. Put the result in your report. Name the device tree and every overlay in the hardware report for the unit. DTB Detective comes from the ROCKNIX community, whose work on these boards made most of this possible. When it is not an RK3326 at all Some cheap \"R36S\" units are not RK3326 inside, even when the chip says so. The best-known is the GA36 fake . Its board is marked GA36-MB V1.0-20250730 , and its chip is marked RK3326, but it is an Allwinner A33: a 32-bit chip that sndstation does not run on. The signs: The stock card runs EmuELEC 4.7 on a Linux 3.4 kernel. The card has an Android-style layout, with a raw U-Boot partition and a boot image, and no .dtb file at all. DTB Detective has nothing to read, since there is no device tree. phaseloop's GA36 notes document the board, its partitions, and a recovery image. The unit cannot boot without its original card, so keep that card. A GA36 is a dead end for sndstation. Its battery and screen still make good spares. For something to run sndstation on today, a Raspberry Pi with Raspberry Pi OS Lite (trixie) takes the .deb as it is and runs full screen ( Installing on a handheld ). Buying an RK3326 unit An R36S is a lottery: the name says little about the board inside. Any other RK3326 model is a better bet. The maintainers' current pick is the K36 : its two USB ports and headphone jack are on the top edge, out of your hands' way, and it has one microSD slot. It is not one of the beta's slots. The device guide ranks the rest. H700: match the RAM, then the model An H700 model has one board, so its name tells you the device tree. The table in Supported hardware names each one. A model sold with a second screen panel has a second device tree, such as sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-h-rev6-panel.dtb : on H700 a panel revision is its own device tree, not an overlay. What varies is the RAM . Units of one model ship with DDR3 or DDR4. The device tree is the same for both, but the bootloader that starts the RAM is not. An H700 image is built for one or the other and says so in its name, as in sndstation-H700-DDR4-….img.xz . The wrong one hangs before the kernel starts, with nothing on screen. To tell which you have, read the vdd-dram regulator on a running unit, for example under the stock firmware: grep . /sys/class/regulator/regulator.*/name # find vdd-dram cat /sys/class/regulator/regulator.N/microvolts # N from the line above 1100000 is DDR4, which most units have. 1200000 is DDR3. A DDR3 card already applies its ddr3 overlay first; list any other overlay after it. Then set the model's device tree on the FDT line of extlinux/extlinux.conf , and any overlays on the FDTOVERLAYS line, separated by spaces: FDT /dtbs/sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg40xx-h.dtb FDTOVERLAYS /overlays/my-panel.dtbo Once sndstation runs The launcher's ABOUT page shows the unit's own identity, read from the chip: the SOC ID row. Two units of one model get different ids. See Which unit this is .","stub":false},{"u":"users/peripherals.html","t":"Peripherals: what works on your unit","s":"Users","h":["Peripherals: what works on your unit","Audio and MIDI","Storage","Controllers","Bluetooth","Wi-Fi","Webcams","If something does not work"],"x":"Peripherals: what works on your unit For: someone about to plug something into a handheld and wanting to know whether it will work before buying it. What a unit supports depends on its chip, not on its brand name. Find your family in the table: the device guide and Identifying your unit say which one you have. RK3326 RK3566 H700 TrimUI (A133) PlayStation Classic Audio interface, MIDI keyboard yes yes yes yes yes USB stick, card reader yes yes yes yes yes exFAT and NTFS drives yes yes yes no no USB-serial adapter yes yes yes yes yes Game controller yes yes yes basic only basic only Bluetooth yes yes built-in only yes no Wi-Fi dongle yes yes yes, 0.15.1-h700 or later not needed required Webcam no no add-on no no Models in each family: RK3326 is the RG351 series, R33S/R36S and RGB10; RK3566 is the RG353 series, RGB30, RK2023 and X55; H700 is the RG35XX series, RG40XX, RG34XX, RG28XX and RGcubeXX; A133 is the TrimUI Smart Pro, Brick and Brick Pro. Audio and MIDI Every unit takes a class-compliant USB audio interface or MIDI controller, with MIDI 2.0. Class-compliant means the device needs no vendor driver, which is most interfaces made in the last decade and nearly every MIDI keyboard. Interfaces that need a vendor driver do not work, and are not planned: Native Instruments, Line 6, and the Edirol and Roland units that use their own protocols. If a device's manual tells you to install a driver on a PC, it will not work here. Storage USB sticks, card readers and USB drives work on every unit. On the TrimUI units and the PlayStation Classic, format the drive FAT32 or ext4 : those two kernels have no exFAT or NTFS support, so a drive formatted the way it came out of the shop may not mount. The other three families read exFAT and NTFS as they are. Controllers Every unit handles a generic USB or Bluetooth gamepad. Console pads that need their own driver are patchier: Xbox pads work on RK3326 and RK3566, PlayStation pads on RK3566 and the H700, and Nintendo and Steam pads only on RK3326. The TrimUI units and the PlayStation Classic have generic support only, so a console pad may show up with its buttons mapped oddly or not at all. Bluetooth RK3326 and RK3566 take a Bluetooth USB dongle as well as using whatever is built in. The H700 can use its own Bluetooth but not a dongle. The TrimUI units have Bluetooth on the same chip as their Wi-Fi. The PlayStation Classic has no Bluetooth at all. Wi-Fi Most units have Wi-Fi of their own and need nothing. Two cases do not: The RG28XX has no Wi-Fi, so a USB dongle is its only way onto a network. Use a Realtek 8188EU, 8188CU/8192CU or 8192EU dongle; these are the cheap ones sold for the Raspberry Pi. The PlayStation Classic likewise needs a dongle, and the supported one is a Realtek RTL8821CU. Other chipsets are not supported, so check the chipset and not the brand before buying. Dongle Wi-Fi also needs the matching firmware, which the images carry. On the H700, the image has to be recent enough. The driver arrived in the 0.15.1-h700 release; the H700 image in 0.15.1 came before it, and a dongle does nothing there. Take the H700 image from 0.15.1-h700 or later. Watch the filename when you do. An image is named for the date it was built, not for the release it came from, so the H700 image in 0.15.1 and the one in 0.15.1-h700 are both called sndstation-H700-DDR4-20261001.img.xz and are not the same image. If you have downloaded both they will collide in one folder: keep them apart, or check each against the .sha256 beside it in the release. Webcams No unit supports a USB webcam out of the box. On the H700 the driver can be installed as an add-on package afterwards, because that kernel can load modules and already has the video framework built in. On the other families it cannot be added after the fact: the TrimUI and PlayStation Classic kernels load no modules at all, and the RK3326 and RK3566 would need a kernel rebuild. Webcams are not in the kernels because no supported unit needs one to do anything it is meant to do. A driver goes in when a unit would otherwise be unable to do something basic, which is why the Wi-Fi dongle drivers above are there. If something does not work Peripheral support in the table is what the kernel offers, not a promise that every device in a class has been tried. If a device in a supported class does not work, say what the unit is and what lsusb reports for the device.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/hardware-report.yml.html","t":"hardware-report.yml","s":"Project","h":["hardware-report.yml"],"x":"hardware-report.yml .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/hardware-report.yml in sndstation, 96 lines · download # Draft for #144. name: Hardware report description: What worked and what did not on a real unit. labels: [\"hardware\"] body: - type: markdown attributes: value: | One report per unit and image. How to measure and what to include: docs/hardware/reporting.md. - type: input id: unit attributes: label: Unit model description: The model on the case, for example Anbernic RG35XX SP. validations: required: true - type: input id: slot attributes: label: Slot description: The unit's slot number in docs/hardware/supported.md. validations: required: true - type: input id: soc attributes: label: SoC description: The CPU row on ABOUT, for example Rockchip RK3326. validations: required: true - type: dropdown id: image attributes: label: Image it runs options: - sndstation rootfs - ROCKNIX - stock BSP - other (say which below) validations: required: true # TODO(#236/#124): how an image states its own version. - type: input id: image-version attributes: label: Image name or date description: The name or date of the image the unit runs. validations: required: true - type: input id: version attributes: label: sndstation version description: From ABOUT, or the release tag. validations: required: true - type: input id: unit-id attributes: label: Unit id description: The SOC ID row on ABOUT. validations: required: true - type: textarea id: rtbench attributes: label: \"`--rt-bench` output\" description: The whole output of each run (docs/hardware/reporting.md says how). If you could not run it, say why. render: shell validations: required: true - type: textarea id: must-show attributes: label: What the slot's \"must show\" says description: One line per item in the unit's private-beta issue, each marked shown, not shown, or could not be tried. validations: required: true - type: textarea id: config attributes: label: Config files description: Paste or attach ~/.config/sndstation/gamepads.json if Map this controller saved it, and the `rings` entry `sndstation --led-slots` printed. - type: textarea id: worked attributes: label: What worked validations: required: true - type: textarea id: failed attributes: label: What did not work description: What you did, what you expected, and what happened instead. validations: required: true","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.html","t":"Code of Conduct","s":"Project","h":["Code of Conduct","In short","Reporting","Enforcement"],"x":"Code of Conduct Draft for #140. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. sndstation adopts the Contributor Covenant, version 2.1 as its code of conduct. It is adopted by reference: the linked text is the code, and this page does not copy it. In short Be respectful. Disagree with an idea without attacking the person. Harassment, discrimination, threats, and publishing someone else's private information are not acceptable. It applies in every space the project runs: issues, pull requests, review comments, and wherever the project's chat lives. It also applies when someone speaks for the project in public. Maintainers may remove or edit contributions, and may ban a person, to uphold it. The Covenant has the full standards and the enforcement guidelines. Where this summary and the Covenant differ, the Covenant wins. Reporting TODO(#236/#191): the reporting contact, named by the people who hold the role. Until it exists, no private address is promised here. Proposal, for the maintainers to accept or change. Reports are read by at least two people, not a single maintainer. A report about one of the readers is handled by the others, and that person takes no part in it. Reports are kept confidential: the people who read them share the details with no one else, except where a decision needs it. Enforcement The people who read reports decide the response, using the Covenant's enforcement guidelines (correction, warning, temporary ban, permanent ban). They tell the reporter what was decided. TODO(#236/#191): who they are and how a report about one of them is handled in practice.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/SECURITY.html","t":"Security policy","s":"Project","h":["Security policy","Supported versions","Reporting a vulnerability","What to expect","Scope"],"x":"Security policy Draft for #141. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. Supported versions sndstation is pre-release. Fixes go to: Version Fixed main yes the latest release yes anything older no Reporting a vulnerability Do not open a public issue, and do not post details in chat or in a pull request. The private beta has no private reporting channel yet. The intended channel is GitHub's private vulnerability reporting: a Report a vulnerability button on this repository's Security tab. That button does not exist until the feature is on: TODO(#236/#150). TODO(#236/#191): an interim contact until then. Include what you found, the version or commit, the unit and image it ran on, and the steps to reproduce it. What to expect Proposal, not a commitment: an acknowledgement within 7 days, and a first assessment within 30 days. Fixes for what we accept go to main and into the next release, and the advisory credits you unless you ask it not to. Scope Areas worth looking at in particular: What the .deb installs on a handheld: the sudo rule for the root helpers, tty1 autologin, the realtime allowance, and the udev rule for the stick lights. sndstation-netctl , sndstation-storage , and sndstation-updates , which run as root. The sudo rule lets the sudo group run them with no password and with any arguments. It also allows poweroff and reboot with no arguments, and tee /sys/power/state , which puts the unit to sleep. The CLAP scanner, which loads third-party plugins (each in a child process). What the .deb runs as root lists the root-run parts and what each is allowed to do. Out of scope: third-party CLAP plugins themselves (report those to their authors), and a unit someone has already given an attacker shell access to.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/SUPPORT.html","t":"Support","s":"Project","h":["Support","Questions","A unit that does not work, or does","A bug","An idea","Security"],"x":"Support Draft for #143. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. Questions Ask where the project's chat or forum lives: TODO(#236/#119): the channel is not chosen yet. Look first in the docs , which cover installing, the launcher, the audio and gamepad setup, and the SDK. A unit that does not work, or does Tested a unit? Report it with the Hardware report issue form. What to measure, and what to put in it, is in Reporting hardware results . The units we know about are in Supported hardware . A bug Open an issue with the Bug report form. Say which version, which unit and which image, and how to reproduce it. An idea Open an issue with the Feature request form. For a large change, read CONTRIBUTING.md first. Security Do not report a vulnerability in an issue or in chat. Follow SECURITY.md .","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/audio-device.html","t":"The audio device layer: the mixer, the test signals, the buffer and the card","s":"Subsystems","h":["The audio device layer: the mixer, the test signals, the buffer and the card","1. The mixer in-process, not through amixer","2. The test signal","2.1 The sequence is a function of the sample clock and holds no state","2.2 A lapse gaps the sound; it does not rewind the sequence","2.3 The fixtures","2.4 MDFOURIER: the measurement signal, for a recorder","3. What can and cannot be set about the buffer","3.0 The stream is run here, on alsa-lib","3.1 The sizes offered are the sizes the device gave","3.2 The rate: Default first, then what the device gave","3.3 Which ALSA device: the card itself, not default","3.4 What the device is handed, and where it clips","3.5 With no device: the app's own clock","3.6 Opening and closing: the fades","4. Where plug detection is read: four doors"],"x":"The audio device layer: the mixer, the test signals, the buffer and the card How sound gets from AudioOut to the card, and what the platform measures on the way: the hardware mixer, read and written in-process; the test signal and MDFourier; the buffer and the rate; which card plays; and where plug detection is read. The code is the SDK's audiodev , mixerio , audioout , alsastream and audiocard , and the test tone testtone . The overview, who opens the device and when, is audio-stack.md . The unit's profile, the set-up by ear and the AUDIO page are audio-page.md ; the volume and the maximums are audio-levels.md . 1. The mixer in-process, not through amixer Every mixer and jack read, and every write, used to run amixer : about 230 ms a call on an RG351V, all of it blocking whoever waited. The page grew mechanisms to hide it — writes coalesced into a 120 ms window, reads on a worker thread, no socket polling while a test sound played, a title frozen meanwhile — and the opening freeze of the AUDIO page and of every app on a headphone plug was three or four of those calls on the UI thread. src/mixerio.cpp does amixer's four verbs through alsa-lib instead. scontents and contents print what amixer prints (alsa-utils' show_selem and the high-level control listing, line for line), sset echoes the control as amixer does, and cset parses the value as amixer does, so the parser established against seventeen units on amixer's output stays the one reader. Checked against amixer itself on an alsa-lib control plugin answering like the handhelds' cards: both listings byte-identical, every write form the code uses landing on the same state. A read is about 0.04 ms on a desktop. Built without alsa-lib, each verb still runs amixer. The reads still run on the poller ( audiodev::poll ) for the page, behind generation counters ( mixerGen() , socketGen() ), and poll::setSynchronous(headless) puts them on the calling thread under --shots . alsa-lib's configuration-parser noise goes through the same filter as SDL's ( rt::hookAlsaErrors ). 2. The test signal AUDIO has a Test sound row, so where sound comes out and how loud can be checked without leaving for an instrument. PINGS 180 ms enveloped sine bursts climbing in octaves, A2 to A8, 120 ms apart. The default, and the one that is safe to leave running. SINE the steady 440 the wizard uses. Still right for \"does this socket carry sound at all\", still the wrong thing to walk away from. BURSTS the pings' notes, sparser: 150 ms in every 600. The same peaks at a quarter of the heat ( TestTone::Signal::Bursts ). MDFOURIER the MDFourier measurement signal, for a recorder: AUDIO's TOOLS opens its own view, which plays it once and exactly (§2.4). Not a sound to listen to, and never on the speaker. A continuous sine at a working level is the one signal that will cook a 1-watt handheld speaker, because it is the only one with no duty cycle to hide behind — hence bursts by default and a warn color on the constant one. Octaves rather than a sweep: the judgment is per note and a listener needs a moment on each. A small driver rolling off, a cabinet buzzing at one note, or an analog stage distorting only at the top all announce themselves in a sequence and hide in a single tone. The steady sine sits at −12 dBFS — the reference MDFourier's sweeps and noise share — and so does everything the set-up by ear plays. The pings and bursts play at full scale where a maximum is judged (SYSTEM LEVELS, past the volume, but not on headphones) and as the main page's test sound (at the volume): an output's maximum is what a full-scale sample produces ( audio-levels.md ). Every constant is in milliseconds converted at the running rate, so the sequence sounds the same on a card that came back at 44100 as on one that gave 48000. 2.1 The sequence is a function of the sample clock and holds no state m_samplePos advances by frames every callback whether the tone plays or not, so which burst is sounding, how far into it, and what the sine is doing there are all derived from the sample's number . Two runs at the same sample position are bit-identical. off == 0 gives sin(0) , so every burst opens at zero by construction rather than by remembering to reset something. The only state in the whole generator is the epoch — one integer, written in one place, when a person picks a signal. That is what a generator with history costs. The earlier one walked a position that advanced only while the tone played and reset when it did not; any hiccup anywhere moved where it thought it was, and it sounded like the pings randomly restarting, because they did. The fix is not to chase the hiccups. It is to have nothing for a hiccup to disturb. 2.2 A lapse gaps the sound; it does not rewind the sequence The tone is a lease : setTone() renews a 0.35 s hold and the RT side drops the tone when it lapses. The renewal is bracketed around the blocking work in the page's update() , so only the draw sits between one renewal and the next. A lapse now leaves a hole, and the sequence carries on wherever the sample clock says when the lease is renewed — partway through a burst, if the gap cut one. A deliberate choice of signal rewinds to the bottom octave; nothing else does. A dropped burst stays dropped rather than being replayed, which is the better answer for a test signal: a dropout you can hear as a missing ping is a dropout you know about. The page and the mixer show a dropout count beside the signal name when it is not zero, which turns \"it stutters\" into a number. 2.3 The fixtures sndstation --tone-wav <pings|sine> out.wav dumps three seconds from the generator the page plays ( TestTone ) — not a second implementation written to check the first, which can agree with it while both are wrong. Measured that way: 7 bursts: 107 217 439 878 1756 3517 7039 Hz, 180 ms, 120 ms gaps peak 0.2510 = -12.01 dBFS, L == R, edges in 0.0000 out 0.0001 sndstation --tone-wav mdfourier[:RATE] out.wav writes the whole MDFourier run instead (48 kHz unless a rate is given), from the run MDFOURIER plays, and checks it against the reference script's checksum: the reference file a recording is analyzed against. sndstation --tone-wav pings-gap puts two deliberate 400 ms holes mid-sequence, because the plain dump could never catch the lapse bug : it renews the lease every block, so the failing path was unreachable. Every surviving burst still lands on its own slot at k × 0.30 s with the frequency that slot calls for: @0.60s slot 2 ~433 Hz 104ms <- cut by the gap slot 3 <- swallowed whole: a HOLE, not a rewind @1.20s slot 4 ~1756 Hz 180ms <- right slot, right note @2.40s slot 8 ~217 Hz 180ms A fixture that cannot fail is not evidence. That is also where the MDFourier sequence gets checked when it arrives. test_testtone checks the same property without anyone listening: a run whose lease lapses twice must be, wherever it plays, sample for sample the run that never lapsed — and fails when the generator is made to rewind. 2.4 MDFOURIER: the measurement signal, for a recorder MDFourier compares a recording of a known signal against the signal itself, so the samples have to reach the DAC exactly as rendered. The signal is pinobatch's mdfourier-dac , as a C++ port ( src/mdfourier/ , zlib) that is bit-exact with the Python original, run under the NumPy 1.x it was written for, at 44.1, 48, 88.2, 96, 176.4 and 192 kHz. Under NumPy 2 the script's pink noise overflows int16 and wraps; the port renders that reading too, on a switch (below), and src/mdfourier/README.md has the detail. MDFourierRun ( include/sndstation/mdfourier.h ) plays it: The signal sync, a sine sweep and a pulse sweep from 20 Hz at 2 s an octave, white and pink noise, all at −12 dBFS ; then a square wave fading from full scale (0 dBFS) , and sync again. 56.4 s at 44.1 and 48 kHz, 60.4 s at 88.2 and 96, 64.4 s at 176.4 and 192. Exact the int16 samples go to the device through AudioOut::setExact : after the app's render, in place of it; no dither, no gain, no clip. S16 is a copy and S32 the sample shifted up 16 bits. F32 is converted again after this program, so it is refused. Once from the first sample to the last, no loop. A starts again from the top. Underruns any one ends the run, in the audio thread, at the next block: the rest is silence and the view says where it stopped and that the recording is not valid. Counted where alsa-lib reports them ( rt::alsaRecoveries ); on another driver the run plays but says it was not checked. Prepared rendered whole on a worker thread when MDFourier is chosen, at the running rate: a few hundred milliseconds and 5 MB at 48 kHz, 22 MB at 192 kHz, given back when the page closes. The audio thread only copies. The view ( launcher/audiomdf.cpp ) shows the rate to record at in large type, with what the device is handed and what the card runs, and refuses to start, saying why, when: the rate is not one of the six (choose one in Sample rate ); the card runs at another rate, i.e. ALSA is resampling; the output format is f32; the sound is going to the speaker (a USB card has none; on a desktop the system decides). The first start each time the page is opened asks once: full scale at the end, line output to a recorder, nobody's headphones. While it plays the page forks nothing (no amixer reads, as for the test tones), and the run ends if the device under it changes (a card plugged in, a rate or size moved): that is a gap the recorder heard. B stops it; leaving the page does too. A run that completes says so, and that it had no underruns; the recording is then the one to analyze, at the rate shown. The reference file. Preparing the run also writes the whole signal as a 16-bit .wav on the unit, for the recording to be compared with, and the view shows where and whether it is there: ~/.local/share/sndstation/mdfourier/mdfourier-dac-48000-numpy1.wav ( $XDG_DATA_HOME/sndstation/mdfourier/ when that is set.) One file per rate and NumPy reading, written once, on the worker, to a temporary name and renamed, so a file under the name is always whole; a later prepare finds it and leaves it. It is the very render the run plays ( test_mdfourier reads one back and checks it against the script's checksum), so scp it off, or pull the card. sndstation --tone-wav mdfourier[:RATE][:numpy2] out.wav writes the same file on a desk. Which NumPy. system.json 's audio.mdfourier_numpy picks how the script is read, which decides the pink noise and nothing else: 1 (default) NumPy 1.x, what the script was written for: its pink mixer's sum promotes to int64 and never overflows. 2 NumPy 2: the sum stays int16 and wraps; a quarter of the pink noise differs. For matching a reference rendered from the script on a current NumPy. Read when the page opens; the view names the one in force on its REFERENCE line, and the file is named for it. The checks: test_mdfourier plays every rate, both readings, through the run in uneven blocks and compares with the reference checksums, writes and reads back a reference file, and checks an underrun's stop; test_mdfourier_ref renders each rate with the Python original ( tests/reference/ , needs numpy) and cmp s the files, for NumPy 1.x's reading and, where NumPy 2 is installed, for NumPy 2's; test_audiosetup opens the view on SDL's disk device and finds the reference file where the view says, for each value of the switch; test_audioout plays a whole run through SDL's disk driver at S16 and at S32, with dither on and the app rendering underneath, and checks what the device was handed. 3. What can and cannot be set about the buffer period size ours, from the page. Buffer size changes it live. On the stream run here (§3.0) it is the period asked of alsa-lib; on SDL's stream it becomes SDL's samples , which becomes ALSA's period. It is saved in the unit's audio.json : one size for the built-in outputs, and its own for a USB or HDMI card ( audio-page.md ). audio.buffer_frames in system.json is only the default a unit has not changed. period count two, or the fewest the card allows: the low-latency answer. Every other value adds latency. start threshold ours (§3.0): the whole buffer, at the first start and after every underrun. sample rate ours, from the page. Sample rate changes it live (§3.2), saved in the unit's audio.json beside the period size; 0 is Default. audio.sample_rate in system.json is only the default a unit has not changed. format, access negotiated. A device may hand back something near what was asked, and nothing says so unless /proc/asound is read. 3.0 The stream is run here, on alsa-lib On Linux nothing is played through SDL: src/alsastream.cpp opens the device with alsa-lib and runs the writer thread itself, whichever device it is — the card this program chose, AUDIODEV or alsa_pcm as given, or default and whatever the system put behind it (a plug , a dmix , a sound server's ALSA plugin, so a server is reached as aplay reaches it). SDL's audio is started on its ALSA driver for that, whatever it would pick first (a sound server's), and only lists the devices for the log: SDL_AUDIODRIVER is set for that one call and taken away again, so nothing the program starts inherits it. SDL's stream is left for where there is no alsa-lib (macOS, Windows, a build without it), and for an SDL_AUDIODRIVER set outside, as the tests' disk driver is. The log says which: \"the stream run here, on alsa-lib\", or why not. The reason is the start threshold. SDL's backend sets it to one frame, so a stream starts, and starts again after an underrun, with one frame queued. At a short period that misses the next deadline at once: one underrun becomes thousands a second, each a stop and restart of the card's DMA. On an H700 under PREEMPT_RT that storm is a kernel panic ( sun6i_dma_interrupt , racing a stop), while aplay , which fills the whole buffer before it starts again, came through 200 forced underruns at 32 frames on the same kernel without a mark. So the stream does what aplay does: start threshold = the buffer : nothing plays until the buffer is full, at the first start and after every underrun; an underrun is recovered with snd_pcm_recover (prepared again, then refilled), which also reports it, so the click count on DIAGNOSTICS is the same; the writer blocks in snd_pcm_writei , as aplay and SDL do: the kernel waits for exactly the frames still to be written. A non-blocking writer that waits with snd_pcm_wait does not work: a write takes the few frames free between two interrupts, the writes stop lining up with the card's periods, and the wait (for a whole period free) wakes an interrupt late to a buffer nearly empty — on an RK3326 at 256 frames, periods in pairs, no safety margin, underruns by the thousand. A card that stops taking anything is the kernel's to notice (an I/O error after its timeout, deviceStopped ); closing drops the stream first, so it never waits on the card. SNDSTATION_AUDIO_STREAM=sdl (or sndstation-audiotest --sdl ) puts SDL's stream back on the same device, to compare: a developer's switch, said in the log at every open. Each underrun on our stream is said in the log, for the first five then every hundred: whether the writer was late (more time since its last write than that write left queued) or the card ran dry with audio still queued. The callback is the same one SDL called: the same conversion, metering, timing and realtime raise; a change the callback reads ( setExact ) is made under the stream's fill lock instead of SDL's device lock. 3.1 The sizes offered are the sizes the device gave How finely the period can be set is a property of the driver and the hardware, and none of it can be looked up: SDL's ALSA backend asks for the period nearest the request, and the card decides: a codec can round to multiples of 16 or 64, and a dmix default device runs at its own fixed size whatever is asked. PipeWire has a floor (32 frames at 48 kHz); PulseAudio takes anything. Every other SDL2 backend (dummy, jack, sndio, OSS) takes powers of two only, and asking one of them for anything else is not merely rounded: SDL 2.26–2.32 computes the buffer size before rounding and never recomputes it, so the callback is handed a buffer sized for 48 frames and told it holds 64. A heap overflow — reproduced with plain SDL on the dummy backend, free(): invalid pointer on close. Only alsa , pulseaudio , pipewire and disk set SupportsNonPow2Samples ; on anything else sndstation asks for powers of two only, at startup as well (a buffer_frames of 1000 in system.json used to reach that bug). So the first press on Buffer size asks : the running device is closed, fifteen sizes — the powers of two from 32 to 4096 and the halfway points between them, or just the powers of two where those are all it is safe to ask — are each opened paused and closed again, and the distinct sizes that came back become the choices. A device that rounds to multiples of 64 offers those; a dmix that only runs at 1024 offers one size and says so. The help box lists them. It is a moment of silence, so the page says \"Asking the device…\" in the frame before it happens, and it is done once a session. Each change after that is a reopen at the new size, at the rate already running ( SDL_AUDIO_ALLOW_FREQUENCY_CHANGE is not passed, so a device that comes back at another rate is resampled rather than detuning the engine and the plugin). Steps are applied after 0.45 s without a press, so holding the d-pad costs one gap, not one per step. The callback takes whatever frame count it is handed and the plugin path slices to the block the plugin was activated with, so nothing downstream has to be rebuilt. The timing counters reset on a reopen, which makes the row's \"no clicks / N clicks\" a verdict on the size on screen. 3.2 The rate: Default first, then what the device gave SDL2 has no call that lists a device's rates, so they are found the same way as the buffer sizes: on the first press the running device is closed, the common rates (22.05, 32, 44.1, 48, 88.2, 96 and 192 kHz, plus whatever Default resolves to) are each opened with the rate allowed to move , and the distinct rates that came back are the choices. ALSA's plug layer and the sound servers resample and so take all of them; a raw hw: device offers its own; a dmix offers one. Default comes first, and is what ships ( sample_rate: 0 ). It is not SDL's default — asked for a rate of zero, SDL picks 22050 — but the device's own rate where SDL can report one, which only the PipeWire and PulseAudio backends do ( SDL_GetDefaultAudioInfo ), so nothing is converted between the program and the server. Everywhere else, ALSA included, it is 48 kHz: the rate the program always asked for before. It stays a separate choice even when it resolves to a number also in the list, because it is a different promise — \"whatever this device prefers\" follows the device; 48 kHz picked by number stays 48 kHz. A change of rate is a reopen at the new rate with the buffer size kept . On the page that is the whole of it — it plays nothing but its tone. An app that changes rate while running ( AudioOut::setRate ) hears about it in its rate callback while the device is still paused, and re-prepares everything that turned the old rate into a constant: clocks, synths, a hosted plugin (deactivated and activated again at the new rate, which keeps its patch where a reload would lose it). Voices already sounding are best released, having been started at the old rate. AudioOut::sampleRate() returns the running rate, not the one asked for, which a device allowed to move it could make wrong. Measured through the real callback on SDL's disk backend: the test sine reads 440.00 Hz at 22.05, 44.1, 48 and 96 kHz after every sequence of changes tried, at -12 dBFS throughout. Which is why DIAGNOSTICS prints ASKED FOR and GOT on adjacent lines. The numbers come from /proc/asound/cardN/pcmNp/subN/hw_params and status — no device is opened to ask , so nothing is disturbed, and the stream is found by looking for the one that is not closed , on whichever card it turned out to be. The row worth watching is WORST MARGIN : buffer_size - avail_max . avail_max is the high-water mark of how empty the buffer ever got, so this is the distance from an xrun the run has actually survived , as opposed to the nominal figure. It turns warn-colored below one period, which is one late wakeup from a dropout. DIAGNOSTICS also measures the callback itself — gap, render time, and a count of late arrivals (>1.5 periods) — with SDL_GetPerformanceCounter , which is a vDSO read and safe from a callback. What it calls a click , there and on the Buffer size row, is an underrun as alsa-lib reports it: the stream (§3.0, or SDL's ALSA backend) recovers every one with snd_pcm_recover , which reports it, and the program counts those reports for the whole run ( rt::alsaRecoveries ). Late callbacks are kept as their own row and are not the same thing: a Powkiddy RGB30 (RK3566) delivers its callbacks in pairs — two together, then a double gap — and scored 1100 \"late\" in a ping test that underran about 10 times. Where alsa-lib cannot say (not ALSA), late callbacks are the only stand-in, and the page says \"likely\". The same handler is installed before SDL starts its audio (so it also catches SDL's first device listing), and it hides alsa-lib's configuration-parser messages: everything from its conf.c and confmisc.c . SDL lists every device at startup and again every five seconds, and alsa-lib tries each standard definition (hdmi, surround, iec958…) against every card, complaining about each that does not fit: a two-card RGB30 printed dozens of Unknown parameter CARD a scan, none about anything playing. One line says they are hidden; SNDSTATION_ALSA_VERBOSE=1 shows them, for debugging an asound.conf of one's own. Everything else alsa-lib says, underrun occurred included, prints as before. Raise Buffer size first if it reports underruns. 512 doubles the margin and costs 5 ms; on a box where the UI, Mesa and a forked amixer all compete for four cores, that is usually the difference between clicking and not. The audio thread runs SCHED_FIFO 70 ( audio.realtime_priority ; 0 = an ordinary thread), raised by the thread itself on its first callback, and may be pinned to a core ( audio.cpu ). It needs an rtprio allowance, which the package grants the audio group on a handheld; without one the raise is refused and DIAGNOSTICS shows the thread as ordinary, in the warning color. What it changes was measured, not assumed: see rt-bench.md . 3.3 Which ALSA device: the card itself, not default sndstation opens hw:<card>,0 rather than default . The stream run here (§3.0) opens the device it is named; for SDL, which reads AUDIODEV at every open, the same name is set there after the audio subsystem is up and before the first open, so a probe or a reopen through SDL uses the same device. The variable belongs to the process, and every program it starts inherits it. So the last AudioOut to close puts it back as the process found it: unset, or what was set outside. An app the launcher starts afterward chooses its card by the rule below, as it would started from a shell, rather than taking the launcher's choice for a user's (rule 1), and playing through SDL's own ALSA stream instead of its own. default on a system with no ALSA configuration is alsa-lib's plug over that same card. It offers nothing to share — there is no dmix in it — and plug drives the card through MMAP . On an RG351V (RK3326, mainline 6.12 and 7.1) that path underran steadily at 48 kHz / 256 frames with the built-in synth alone, at a few percent CPU, under SCHED_FIFO 80 as well. Opened as hw:0,0 the same program was clean. hw_params under both showed the same rate, format ( S32_LE ), period (256) and buffer (512); the only difference was access : MMAP_INTERLEAVED through plug , RW_INTERLEAVED from SDL's own snd_pcm_writei on hw . The samples arrive already in an integer format (§3.4), so nothing is lost by skipping plug , and the AUDIO page's rates and sizes become the card's real ones. The rule, in order: AUDIODEV set in the environment — used as given. audio.alsa_pcm in system.json , if not empty — used as given, \"default\" included. Plays on names a card (USB or HDMI) that is plugged in — that card. Not plugged in, Automatic until it is. Plays on one of the built-in card's outputs takes the built-in card (rule 6) whatever is plugged in. Plays on is Automatic (the default) and a USB card that plays is plugged in — that card. Someone plugged it in to hear it. This system configures ALSA ( /etc/asound.conf , ~/.asoundrc , $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/alsa/asoundrc , a non-empty /etc/alsa/conf.d or /usr/share/alsa/alsa.conf.d ) — default . Somebody may have put dmix, softvol or a sound server behind it on purpose. Otherwise hw:CARD=<id>,DEV=0 : the card named by ALSA_PCM_CARD or ALSA_CARD if one is set, else the first card that is not a display output (HDMI, DisplayPort, S/PDIF), named by its id so a change in probe order cannot move it. Not card 0: on a Powkiddy RGB30 (RK3566) card 0 is HDMI and the speaker codec is card 1, and \"card 0\" played to a cable nobody had plugged in. Nor a virtual card: on an H700 image that loads snd-aloop , card 0 is ALSA's Loopback, which plays into nothing. ALSA has no word for \"real hardware\"; sysfs does. A real card's device ( /sys/class/sound/cardN/device ) hangs off a bus (USB, PCI, an SoC's) or has a firmware node ( of_node from the device tree, firmware_node from ACPI); a made-up one is a platform device straight under /sys/devices/platform with neither ( snd_aloop.0 , snd_dummy.0 , snd_virmidi.0 ). Where sysfs cannot say, the driver's name does (Loopback, Dummy, VirMIDI). A virtual card is taken only when named, or when there is no hardware at all. With nothing but display outputs, the first of them. A card picked by rule 3 or 4 that will not open (busy, or a USB card whose /dev/snd node udev has not finished with) falls back to the built-in card, and is tried again two seconds later. A device picked by rule 2, 3, 4 or 6 that still will not open gets one retry on default , so a wrong guess costs the log a line, not the program its sound. The log says which device was used and why. USB cards say what they take. /proc/asound/cardN/stream0 lists every format and rate of a USB card's endpoints, and src/audiocard.cpp reads it (nothing is opened to ask). Two things follow from it: Format. SDL2 has no 24-bit packed format. A card that offers none SDL knows — a Dirtywave M8 takes S24_3LE and nothing else — cannot be opened on hw at all (\"Couldn't find any hardware audio formats\"), so it is opened as plughw:CARD=<id>,DEV=0 and handed s32 (an s16 setting would drop eight bits that plug then pads back). Plug's conversion from S32 to S24_3LE is a truncation of bits the DAC never had. A card that takes any SDL format stays on hw . Rate. \"Default\" is 48000 when the card takes it, else the card's own (the M8's 44100), and a pinned rate the card does not take is replaced the same way. Through plughw anything would open — resampled by alsa-lib's linear converter — so setRate and the AUDIO page's rate list are held to what the card lists. The log then says what the card actually runs at, from its hw_params : audio: card M8 runs S24_3LE, 2 ch, 44100 Hz (plughw converts from S32) . AudioOut::cardFormat() and cardRate() carry the same. Hotplug. An app calls AudioOut::service() once a frame. A device SDL marks stopped (a USB card pulled mid-period: the write returns ENODEV and SDL gives the device up) is noticed at once, and /proc/asound/cards is read twice a second. When the card rule 3 or 4 would choose differs from the one playing, the device is closed and opened on the new one; a rate that moved goes to the app's RateFn with the device paused, the same as a rate change from the AUDIO page. Only a PCM this program chose is moved: rules 1 and 2 are left alone. A USB card's own volume. Many come up with their playback level at the bottom, so the first time a USB card is played to, a PCM or Master playback level at 0% is raised to 50%, once . (On a real M8 set to 0% this raised PCM to 128 of 255; the M8 then kept 50% across a cable pull and replug. The M8 has two controls both named PCM , and only the first, 'PCM',0 , is reached.) The unit's audio.json records it under the card's endpoint ( usb:<vendor>:<product> , \"raised\": true ), and a card with that record is never touched again on this unit: a level turned down afterward stays down. No other level is touched; a USB interface's other playback levels are often input monitoring. More than two channels. The stream file lists each endpoint's channel count ( Channels: 24 ). audiocard keeps them per direction ( Card::channels , Card::capChannels ), and CARDS shows them: Plays S24_3LE 2 ch 44.1 kHz · Records S24_3LE 24 ch 44.1 kHz . AudioOut plays stereo and asks for 2 channels, so a card whose playback is 2 channels is used exactly as before. sndstation does not record; a card's capture channels are listed for the person choosing, and an app that records opens them itself ( hw:CARD=M8,DEV=0 , all channels at once). A Dirtywave M8 in its multichannel USB mode (SYSTEM → USB AUDIO MODE, firmware 6.5.0 and later) is that case. It still plays 2 channels, and records 24. Dirtywave's channel order is: Channels What 1–2 main mix 3–18 tracks 1–8, a stereo pair each 19–20 mod FX 21–22 delay 23–24 reverb Switch the mode, then unplug and replug the M8 so the kernel reads the new endpoints. In this mode the M8 is a different USB product, 16c0:048b (stereo mode is 16c0:048a ), so audio.json keeps a separate first-sight record for it. Its 'PCM',0 then has eight channels: front left and right at 50%, the other six at 0%. The volume raise looks at the first channel, so it leaves them alone. Measured on the SP (2026-10-17), with a song playing on the M8 and AudioOut playing a 440 Hz tone to it at about -14 dBFS: Playback: plughw, S24_3LE, 44.1 kHz, 0 xruns. Recording: all 24 channels carry the song, and each track's pair reads the same on both sides. main: -7 dBFS peak tracks: -15 to -27 mod FX, delay, reverb: -38 to -42 InputMeter : reading the same channels, it agreed to within a dB. Our tone: it was not in the recorded main mix (440 Hz at -66 dB): what the M8 receives over USB does not come back in its multichannel capture, or its USB-input level was down. USB I/O view: on the SP with the M8 plugged in, it names the card and its formats and labels the meters MAIN, T1–T8, MOD, DLY, REV. --fake-audio m8multi answers as the SP with the M8 in this mode. A card that plays more than 2 channels and not 2 (an 8-out interface with no stereo altset) has not been tried: hw would refuse SDL's 2 channels, and plughw's channel routing has not been measured. 3.4 What the device is handed, and where it clips Everything up to the device is float: the engine, the internal synths, a hosted plugin, the host hooks. Full scale is ±1.0, and something has to decide what happens to a sample past it. The internal synths never get there — their limiter ( limiter.h ) bends to a ceiling of 1.0 — but a plugin, the hooks and the click are summed in unlimited, on purpose. audio.output_format ( --audio-format for one run) says who converts: converted by an over s16 (shipped) this program, AudioOut::toDevice (SDK) clipped flat here s32 this program clipped flat here f32 alsa-lib's plug , or a sound server (SDL, on SDL's stream) the converter clips it; a server may turn it down first With s16 / s32 the clip happens in the loop that counts it, so the count means the same on every driver: through PipeWire or PulseAudio an over is no longer quietly rescued by the server's own volume. SDL 2.32's own F32→S16/S32 converters saturate too ( SDL_audiotypecvt.c ), so on ALSA hw f32 clips at the same place — just out of sight. The device is opened without SDL_AUDIO_ALLOW_FORMAT_CHANGE , so SDL delivers exactly the asked format; on a card that wants another integer width it widens S16 losslessly. s16 is dithered ( audio.dither ): two uniform draws a sample, differenced — one step of triangular noise, from a xorshift32 — which turns 16-bit rounding's low-level distortion into a steady hiss about 90 dB down. s32 is not; its step is far below any DAC's. The same loop measures the peak for the HUD ladder and counts the overs: the square at the top right of the ladder lights red for two seconds after any block went over, and AUDIO / DIAGNOSTICS says how many and how far ( Clipped: 37 blocks · worst +1.8 dB over full scale ), beside what the device is handed ( Samples ). NaN is not looked for: a plugin that outputs it is broken, and the check would cost every sample. Checked through SDL's disk driver, which writes what the device is handed: the same chord in all three formats measured peak 0.3212 and RMS 0.0763-0.0764 once scaled back. 3.5 With no device: the app's own clock A headless run, or one where no device would open, has no callback. The app calls AudioOut::pumpManual(dt) from its own loop instead, with the seconds since the last call, and the render function runs as the callback would have run it: the volume, setExact 's samples and the peak meter included. With a device running it does nothing. dt seconds is rarely a whole number of samples. At 48 kHz a 60 Hz loop is 800 a frame, but a 144 Hz one is 333.33…, and a loop pumped in small steps may be owed less than one sample a call. So the fraction left over is carried to the next call rather than dropped. Dropped, the 144 Hz clock would run a third of a sample slow every frame, and a small enough step would never render anything at all. Carried, the samples rendered over a run match the time that passed, to within one. A call renders at most 4096 frames. What a long stall is owed beyond that is dropped, not carried: the run's clock loses that time rather than catching up in a burst. A dt that is zero, negative or not finite is ignored. The carry starts again at each open() . 3.6 Opening and closing: the fades A stream started at full level, or cut there, is a step, and a speaker plays a step as a click. So the sound comes in from silence over AudioOut::FADE_MS (5 ms, 240 frames at 48 kHz) after every start, and goes out over the same before every close, reopen() , setRate() , rate or size probe, and card move. The gain is applied in the callback after the volume and before the conversion, so the meter and the over count see what the device gets. A fade out is played, not just computed: close() and the rest wait until the gain is at 0 and three whole callbacks have gone out silent, by when whatever the device still held of the slope has played too (two periods of buffer, and one more). That costs a close about three periods and the fade; it waits at most 100 ms past that for a callback that never comes. A device not started, stopped or playing the exact path ( setExact , MDFourier) is closed at once: there is nothing to fade, and the exact path's samples are never touched. The destructor does not wait either: the app the render function belongs to may already be going, so an app that wants the fade calls close() . What a fade cannot remove is what the hardware does when a device opens or closes: a codec that powers its output down some seconds after the close (ASoC's pmdown_time , 5 s by default; a late pop on some H700 units) still clicks then. Keeping the device open is the remedy for that, not the fade. test_audioout reads what the device was handed (SDL's disk driver): the slope in and out, each about 240 frames, no step larger than a slope's, and the silence before the close. 4. Where plug detection is read: four doors audiodev::readSocket() answers \"is something in the headphone socket?\" There is no one place a Linux handheld says so, so it tries every door, keeps what each one said in Socket::scanned , and takes the first usable one in this order of confidence: kind door why it is where it is in the order alsa the card's read-only BOOLEAN jack kcontrols ( amixer contents ) set by the same driver that decides where the audio goes input evdev nodes with SW_HEADPHONE_INSERT , SW_LINEOUT_INSERT , SW_MICROPHONE_INSERT or SW_JACK_PHYSICAL_INSERT a copy of the codec's state, and on some clones a copy that was never wired up extcon /sys/class/extcon older vendor kernels switch /sys/class/switch (Android's switch class) older vendor kernels still The input door is read through device events : readSocket() opens a one-shot DeviceEvents scan of /dev/input , with no hotplug watch and no hallkey door, and audiodev::inputSockets() turns its rows into SocketSource s. That leaves one evdev switch reader in the SDK. Each node that carries a jack switch becomes a row like HEADPHONE=1 MIC=0 , listing every jack switch it has with its state. A node whose HEADPHONE bit never moves while its LINEOUT bit does is a diagnosis, and only the full list shows it. A node with only LINEOUT or JACK is still offered. A node that couldn't be opened is kept with its error, so \"denied: join group 'input'\" can be said instead of \"no jack found\". Nodes with no jack switch are left out. --fake-audio snapshots answer from the ALSA door alone, so they never reach this one. inputSockets() is public so it can be tested on rows instead of a unit, in tests/test_deviceevents.cpp .","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/audio-levels.html","t":"Audio levels: the system volume and the maximums","s":"Subsystems","h":["Audio levels: the system volume and the maximums","The layers","Outputs (endpoints)","The value","Who applies it","The API","Maximums","Who uses which","Calibration signals: what is safe where","Power","Open questions"],"x":"Audio levels: the system volume and the maximums How loud the unit plays, and how loud it can never play. Two layers: Maximums , per output: the levels the hardware gain stages are put at while that output plays. The loudest the output is meant to go, set by a person (SYSTEM LEVELS, on the AUDIO page) and applied by the platform. The system volume , per output: a software volume in dB that every program shares. 0 dB is the maximum, so the volume only ever turns down. Protecting the speaker is the maximum: nothing in the SDK can make an output louder than the levels kept for it. There is no filter and no limiter. Where they are kept and who decides which output plays is audio-page.md ; the rest of the audio path is audio-device.md . The layers Layer Set by Kept in the unit's audio.json Moves while playing? Maximum , per output: each playback level's value, with its range a person, in SYSTEM LEVELS and nowhere else endpoints.<output>.maximum no: applied when the output is chosen System volume , per output, in dB Vol± anywhere, the AUDIO page, an app's own control endpoints.<output>.volume_db yes, ramped The app's own mix the app yes At the maximum, the samples reach the hardware unchanged and the hardware is at the loudest level that output is meant to play. So 0 dBFS out of AudioOut at 0 dB of volume is the loudest the output goes. An app's mix past full scale is hard-clipped at the conversion: the maximum is what full scale means. Outputs (endpoints) Each endpoint keeps its own volume and maximum: speaker , headphones , speaker+headphones , out (a unit that cannot tell which plays), builtin (a unit not set up), usb:<vid:pid> , card:<id> , default . Which one plays is decided by audiounit::endpointOf from the card playing, the unit's set-up, its Plays on and the socket ( audio-page.md ). All of them belong to the unit: a USB interface used on two units keeps a volume on each. The value dB, −60 to 0 , then mute one press below −60. Up from mute is −60. 1.5 dB a press. Steps in dB are the same size to the ear anywhere. First time : 0 dB, except an output with headphones on it (headphones, or speaker + headphones), which starts at −18 dB . A first plug never delivers the speaker's level to someone's ears. Kept to 0.1 dB, \"muted\" for mute. Who applies it The SDK (default) The app ( Settings::appVolume ) The app's mix nominal, at 0 dB already has volumeGain() in it Where AudioOut , after the render function and before the conversion, so the meter and the over count see what the device gets wherever the app puts it A change of volume or endpoint ramped across one block the app's to smooth The exact path ( setExact , MDFourier) not applied: a measurement signal is not turned down not applied Exactly one of the two applies it. The log says which, once, when the endpoint is found: audio: volume of Speaker (RK3326-…/speaker): -6 dB, applied here (opened) The API include/sndstation/volume.h : Endpoint (the unit's key, the endpoint's name, a label), of() , current() (the endpoint a program would play on now, with no stream open), load() , save() , step() , gain() , position() , text() , and the constants. On AudioOut , for the endpoint its stream plays on: const volume::Endpoint& endpoint() const; // .name: \"speaker\", \"usb:16c0:048a\", … float volumeDb() const; // volume::MUTED when muted float volumeGain() const; // linear, 0 when muted; RT-safe (an atomic) float stepVolume(int dir); // Vol+ / Vol-: one step, saved at once void setVolumeDb(float db); // clamped, saved bool refreshEndpoint(); // decide again and apply (a plug, a change) open() finds the endpoint and reads its volume before the first callback, and on its card applies what playing it writes: the built-in card's route and speaker switch, then the endpoint's maximum. Only what differs is written. service() (or followJack() alone), every frame, follows a card coming and going, and a plug: that swaps the route and the volume only. Maximums Set in SYSTEM LEVELS (AUDIO, under LEVELS), by ear: with the test sound on, pings at full scale , 0 dBFS, play past the volume there; the card's playback levels are listed first, each with a bar and its dB. Raise them until it distorts, rattles or is too loud, and back off. A playback level moved is kept at once, all of them together, as the playing output's maximum. On headphones the pings stay at the volume. Nothing else sets a maximum: not an app, not the SDK, not the volume. A board's preset carries the maximum the team set for its speaker this same way, and the unit's own, once set, replaces it ( audio-page.md ). Why full scale: the maximum is what a full-scale sample produces, the loudest any app can make the output. Judged at −12 dBFS, as the first version did, a full-scale app plays 12 dB louder than anything heard while setting it. Kept per level as { \"control\", \"value\", \"range\": [lo, hi] } , the value in the card's own units. A maximum level is refused, and said, where the card no longer has the control, or reports another range than the one it was kept in: set it again. Applied by AudioOut when its stream opens, when the card changes and on refreshEndpoint , and at startup by applyStartupMixer() , after the levels kept by hand, so the maximum wins. Not on a headphone plug: that swaps the route and the volume, nothing else. An output with no maximum, its own or its board's preset's, has none, and nothing is written on its behalf: its levels stay where the card has them. Who uses which Launcher Vol± on every page but CONTROLS step the volume of the output a program would play on ( volume::current() ), with an overlay saying which and how loud. With the launcher's stream playing (every page, launcher/audio.h ), they step that stream's volume, heard at once. AUDIO page the playing output's volume (User level) on the main list, and every output's on its own page; its maximum in SYSTEM LEVELS. Its test sound plays at the volume (as loud as an app at this volume); SYSTEM LEVELS (but not on headphones) and the set-up by ear play past it. An app with a volume of its own applies volumeGain() itself ( appVolume ) where its own gain was, if it must come before something of its own, else leaves it to AudioOut ; steps it with stepVolume() . Calibration signals: what is safe where Signal Speaker Headphones, line out Pings at full scale, with gaps (the test sound, at the volume; past it in SYSTEM LEVELS) raise the levels with care at the volume only; not on anyone's ears until it is down Sparse bursts at full scale a quarter of the heat as above Beeps at −18 dBFS, 20% of the time (the set-up by ear) safe to leave running at a working gain safe Steady sine at −12 dBFS (the steady tone) warms the voice coil; not to be left on safe at modest gain MDFourier (AUDIO's TOOLS) refused the measurement: one recording, analyzed on a desk ( audio-device.md §2.4 ) Power The split of gain between hardware and software does not change the battery: the same loudness is the same power, whichever stage made it. What does: closing the stream when nothing sounds, larger buffers when not performing, and powering the unused output down (the route and the speaker switch). Expectations, not measurements. Open questions Hiss at the lowest volumes on sensitive headphones, with the hardware at the maximum: measure before putting hardware steps under the software volume while silent. A calibration mode that steps a level between MDFourier runs and logs which step was where, to find a maximum by measurement. USB interfaces : their own playback level kept as a maximum, in place of the raise from 0% ( raiseUsbVolumeOnce )? A quiet timeout , closing the stream after a while of silence. The overlay : drawn by the SDK, so every app looks the same? Programs outside the SDK get the maximum (it is in the mixer) but not the software volume. Deferred: they follow the SDK for now.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/audio-page.html","t":"Audio: one model, one decision-maker, a page that shows it","s":"Subsystems","h":["Audio: one model, one decision-maker, a page that shows it","The rule","Why it is built this way","Why each unit has a profile of its own","Outputs","Set up by ear","The pages","What holds across the views","Plays on and Cards: which card plays","USB I/O: is it there, and does each output and input work","Editing the mixer","Levels are discrete, and shown that way","Two ALSA details that bite on real cards","The maximum, and the signal that sets it","System defaults: a calibrated board's preset","Kept: audio.json, version 3","What ALSA does and does not promise","Not yet tried on a unit"],"x":"Audio: one model, one decision-maker, a page that shows it How the unit's audio is set, kept and decided, and why the AUDIO page is built the way it is: launcher/audiosetup.cpp and the files beside it, on the SDK's audiounit , audiodev and mixerio . The overview, who opens the device and when, is audio-stack.md . The device layer underneath (the card, the buffer, the test signals, MDFourier) is audio-device.md ; the volume and the maximum are audio-levels.md . The rule Audio is set and tested in the launcher . Every app opens its sound through the SDK ( AudioOut ) and follows the same decisions without working anything out itself : which card plays, which output on it, the route, the maximum, the volume. Programs outside the SDK get what the launcher's startup apply puts on the card, and nothing more. Why it is built this way The page before this one worked the same facts out in several places from several copies — the route in the page (from its unsaved profile), in AudioOut (from the file), at startup (from a third lookup) and in the wizard; which output played in three functions; which card in four — and more than one of them wrote to the card. Nearly every regression was two of them disagreeing. It also ran amixer for every read and write, about 230 ms a call on an RG351V, and most of its modes (not watching the socket during a test, a frozen title, delayed writes) existed to hide that. So now: One set of functions decides ( audiounit.h ): from the cards present, the socket, the unit's set-up and its choices, which endpoint plays and what playing it writes. They read nothing, so they are tested as a table ( tests/test_audiounit.cpp ), and AudioOut , the startup apply, the volume keys and the page all call them. One writer : AudioOut applies routes and maximums when it opens, when the card changes, and when asked ( refreshEndpoint ). A headphone plug only swaps the route (where the set-up says software must: the rk817's mux, the H700's amp) and the volume to that output's: no maximum written, no card looked for, nothing reopened. Every program that plays follows the plug, not only the AUDIO page. The page saves a change and asks its own AudioOut to apply it, and shows what that output reports. While the set-up by ear runs, the output is told to keep its hands off the route ( holdRoute ). The mixer in-process ( src/mixerio.cpp ): amixer's four verbs through alsa-lib, printing exactly what amixer prints so the parser proven on seventeen units stays the reader. Under a millisecond a read. Why each unit has a profile of its own Nothing about a handheld's audio can be read off its device tree. Of seventeen RK3326 units surveyed, seven report the same board model and the same compatible — and they do not behave the same: five route audio cleanly, two play out of both outputs at once. An image builder patched a dts somewhere and the string stayed put. Whether the headphone detect bit means what it says, and which mux item reaches which physical output, are facts you establish by plugging something in and listening. So nothing is guessed : one unit's answer given to another would take a working box and silence it. A board the team has calibrated itself, on our own image, has a preset ( below ); every other unit has only what was heard on it. The user's audio.json is keyed by the unit — the SOC ID row on the ABOUT page — else the board model, else the built-in card, and only that key is read ( below ). The wizard is offered once, never forced. A box whose audio already works — every desktop, and any handheld whose dts happens to be right — has nothing to calibrate. So on its first run the launcher puts up a card only on a handheld (a device tree, and a card with a speaker/headphone route) that is not set up, after the gamepad's own first run: A starts the wizard, B skips it, and the skip is remembered for the unit ( ~/.config/sndstation/setup.json , audio_skipped ). A desktop is never asked, nor a unit its board's preset applies to: it is set up. Outputs An endpoint is a place sound comes out: Endpoint What it is speaker , headphones the built-in card's two outputs speaker+headphones both at once, where the board can make it out a unit that cannot tell which of the two plays (no usable detect bit): Automatic plays the speaker's route, the socket does the rest builtin the built-in card of a unit nobody has set up: nothing is routed usb:<vid:pid> a USB interface card:<id> any other card: HDMI default the system's own device: no card of ours Plays on is one choice among them, each a complete answer to where sound comes out: Automatic USB if plugged in, else the jack decides Speaker + headphones where the board can Speaker Headphones M8 while plugged in HDMI while connected Automatic : a USB interface when one is plugged in; else the headphones when they are in and the unit can tell; else the speaker. HDMI is never chosen automatically. There is no \"off\": \"neither speaker nor headphones\" is choosing another card, and something always plays. A chosen card that goes away falls back to Automatic until it is back. Only what this unit can make is offered. The set-up by ear finds it out: Board Speaker + headphones Speaker alone, headphones in H700 (the amp's switch is the route, the headphones always fed) yes no: the speaker asked for with headphones in plays both rk817 with a speaker switch yes as heard rk817 with a plain mux where one item plays both as heard a socket that cuts the speaker itself no no: only Automatic is offered A unit nobody has set up offers Automatic, Built in and any other card, and leaves the built-in card's routing as the system set it. Built-in and another card at once is not offered: two clocks need a second stream and drift correction. Set up by ear AUDIO / Set up by ear derives the whole profile by asking, at each step, the one question a person holding the box can answer: where do you hear it — speaker, headphones, both, or nothing. Short beeps play throughout: 440 Hz, 120 ms in every 600, at −18 dBFS ( TestTone::Beeps , BEEP_LEVEL ), never a held tone. The page's volume is not applied to them, so a turned-down volume cannot make \"nothing\" a wrong answer; their level and their gaps are what keep a small speaker safe meanwhile. What it concludes goes in the unit's set_up ( detect , route_control , speaker_switch , and a route for each output it can make: speaker, headphones, speaker + headphones where it heard both, and out , what Automatic plays where it cannot tell). While it runs, the page's sound output keeps its hands off the route. What it observed is kept beside it , in survey : the raw detect-bit readings plugged and unplugged, what was heard in each state, whether the jack switches mechanically, whether the speaker bleeds, whether the channels are swapped — plus the card's whole control list and the subset of it that could move sound around and that the profile never touches. That last one is written even when it is empty: \"all of them accounted for\" is a finding once it comes from twenty boards. The point of keeping the observations is that the conclusions can be re-derived later, from a different theory, without going back to the hardware. Two things the surveys settled that are not obvious: detect: \"mechanical\" — some sockets have no detect bit at all and switch the speaker themselves. That is a fourth state, not a broken version of one of the other three. speaker_switch — on rk817_ext the speaker mute is factored out into a second control ( Internal Speakers ), so setting the mux alone leaves both outputs playing. Empty where the mux suffices. It is also what makes \"speaker + headphones\" a choice there: the headphones' route with the speaker left on. The pages AUDIO ● Playing on Speaker · nothing in the socket AUDIO SYSTEM Device ◂ Automatic · Speaker now ▸ Sample rate Automatic · 48 kHz Buffer size ───|────── 256 frames / 5.3 ms no clicks OUTPUTS Speaker playing -6 dB Headphones not plugged in -21 dB LEVELS User level ────────|─ -6 dB software System levels Master -6.0 dB · DAC 0.0 dB hardware Test sound Off TOOLS Diagnostics device, timing, startup MDFourier measurement, for a recorder Device : where sound comes out (it was Plays on). ◂▸ steps through the choices; A lists every card (CARDS). Sample rate and Buffer size : the playing card's, live, offering only what the device answered when asked; kept per card. An output (A on its row): its user level, Play on it; for the built-in card, Set up by ear and, once set up, Corrections (plug detection, swap, the speaker switch). User level : a gain on the samples, in software, before the chip; every app plays through it ( audio-levels.md ). System levels : the chip's own controls. Its playback levels are the playing output's ceiling, and nothing else sets it: see below. TOOLS : USB interface (while one is plugged in), Diagnostics, MDFourier, each a view of its own. The test sound is pings peaking at full scale, played at the volume: what a loud app does at this volume. The steady sine stays at -12 dBFS (a continuous sine is the signal that cooks a small speaker), and so does the set-up by ear, which runs before any maximum exists. The page plays into the launcher's stream ( launcher/audio.h ), opened when the launcher starts: its test sounds are a source on that stream's mixer from the page's open to its close, at the user level, or past it in System levels, where an output's ceiling is judged (on headphones, at the user level there too). It is also the one place that reopens that stream: a card, a rate, a buffer size ( audio-stack.md ). Built alone ( --shots , the tests), it has a stream of its own, opened and closed with the page. What holds across the views The views are also listed in launcher.md . What holds across them: The title line answers the question people open an audio page with : where is the sound going, and is anything plugged in. It shows what the page's own sound output reports, so it is never stale. The bottom bar says what the selected row does (its tooltip), and for a choice, what that choice does. A message takes its place while it lasts. There is no help box: the list has the room. Nothing waits to be saved. Every change is written as it is made, and \"✓ Saved\" shows in the title bar for a moment. --shots writes the caption as 09a-audio-saved . Rows that cannot mean anything are not offered : a unit not set up has no Corrections. Choices take ◂▸ and show arrows when selected; rows that open a view take A and say so. Every reading is in DIAGNOSTICS , with a one-sentence verdict at the top and the figures underneath. Every control is in SYSTEM LEVELS . Plays on and Cards: which card plays Device chooses where sound comes out: Automatic, an output of the built-in card, or any other card that plays, by its id ( Outputs ). The sound moves there at once ( AudioOut::setDevice , then refreshEndpoint ). Each card keeps its own rate and buffer size. Cards (A on Device) lists every card, built in and USB, playback and capture alike, by name, with its direction and, where the card says them, the format and rate of each direction. A plays to the card under the cursor, and so does Automatic at the top. The list follows plugging. The page reads /proc/asound twice a second, so a card plugged in or pulled out appears or goes while the page is open, and the page's sound output follows it ( audio-device.md §3.3, Hotplug). An HDMI card plays through ALSA's hdmi device where ALSA's own file for its driver defines one ( /usr/share/alsa/cards/<driver>.conf ): hdmi:CARD=vc4hdmi,DEV=0 on a Raspberry Pi. The Pi's HDMI hardware ( vc4-hdmi ) takes IEC958 frames and nothing else, so hw and plughw refuse every format SDL has and nothing sounds; ALSA's device encodes them. Automatic never picks a display output: on a Pi it takes the headphone socket, and Plays on picks vc4hdmi for a screen with speakers. --fake-audio m8 answers as an RG35XX SP with a Dirtywave M8 plugged in ( Codec plus an S24_3LE-only, 44.1 kHz M8 ). --shots writes the list as 11b-audio-cards . USB I/O: is it there, and does each output and input work While a USB card is plugged in, MORE has a USB interface row: its id, and how many channels it plays and records ( M8 · 2 ch out · 24 ch in ). The row appears and goes with the card, and the page says so in its help box wherever it is (\"USB interface M8 plugged in: 2 ch out, 24 ch in.\"). A on the row opens USB I/O : USB I/O M8 · plugged in Dirtywave M8 at usb-5101000.usb-1, high speed OUT S24_3LE · 2 ch · 44.1 kHz IN S24_3LE · 24 ch · 44.1 kHz Play through it Yes · playing Test output ◂ Out 1 (left) ▸ INPUTS · S24_3LE · 24 ch · 44.1 kHz ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ ▮▮ MAIN T1 T2 T3 T4 T5 T6 T7 T8 MOD DLY REV What it is: name, and the format, channel counts and rates of each direction, from the card's own stream file ( audio-device.md §3.3), as AudioOut's choice reads them. Play through it: A makes the card what Plays on chooses. Left goes back to the built-in card, since Automatic would land on the USB card again. Test output: Off, Out 1 (left), Out 2 (right), Outs 1 + 2. The tone is the page's test tone ( testtone.h ): a steady 440 Hz at -12 dBFS, held by a lease, so leaving the view, the page or the launcher silences it. Testing moves the sound to the card first, because the tone plays where the sound goes. AudioOut plays stereo, so a card's first two outputs are the ones offered. Inputs: a meter on every capture channel, 0 to -60 dBFS, falling at 20 dB a second, teal, then amber from -6 dBFS, and orange at full scale. A mark over a meter counts samples at full scale; X clears them. Channels that come in pairs are grouped, named under the pair. An M8 recording 24 channels is named in Dirtywave's order (MAIN, T1–T8, MOD, DLY, REV); other cards by number. The meters are InputMeter ( audioin.h ). It opens the card's capture on alsa-lib, not SDL, because SDL2 opens only 1, 2, 4, 6 or 8 channels. It opens every channel at once, in the card's own format (S24_3LE, S32_LE, S24_LE or S16_LE), on hw , so a level read is the level the card sent. It is open only while USB I/O is on screen. Pulled out, the view says nothing is plugged in and the inputs close; plugged in again, they reopen. --fake-audio m8multi and the tests make up levels instead of opening a device. --shots writes the view as 11c-audio-usb . Editing the mixer Every control the card publishes is editable in place, live, and nothing about which controls exist is hardcoded : a level steps in the card's own discrete units, a switch toggles, an enum cycles its own published items. Anything moved joins the unit's built_in.mixer list at its new value (a level with its range) and is put back at startup from then on (a dot marks those), saved as soon as it settles; X drops a control back out of the list. A playback level is the playing output's ceiling instead: moved, every playback level is kept at once as that output's maximum, and put back whenever it plays ( audio-levels.md ). The view shows PLAYBACK and ROUTING; the switches, and capture and the rest, are each behind a row of their own that A opens. A level is drawn as a bar and its dB (or percent); the bottom bar gives its number and range, for the reason below. There is no per-card table of \"the controls that matter\", and there cannot be one. Seventeen rk817 units with the same codec and the same board string behaved three different ways; any curated list would be wrong on some unit within a week, and nothing about a board predicts which. What ALSA does say on every card is what KIND each control is — pvolume , pswitch , penum — and that is all an editor needs. Which ones matter is a per-unit fact, established the way every other per-unit fact here is: a person listens, and the file remembers. That is what makes an H700, a USB DAC or the next clone work with no new code. The list stays in the card's own order rather than being sorted by likely relevance: its other job is to be what you cross-check against amixer scontents , and a page that agrees with the tool is worth more than one that puts the interesting row first. Levels are discrete, and shown that way 201 (79%) 0:255 , not 79% . The value and its range are the granularity — a 0:255 control has 256 positions and a 0:15 one has 16 — and which you are on decides whether the next press is a nudge or a jump. The fine step is 1 : the device's own granularity, not a fraction of a range invented here. Y / A take a coarse step (a sixteenth of the range, at least 2) as a concession to a d-pad rather than to the device. Y is west and A is east, so the face pair points the same way left / right does. The bottom bar names both for the control under the cursor (\"◂▸ moves it 1, Y/A 15 (now 201 of 0:255).\"), because how fine the control is and how fine you are being with it are two different questions. A percentage step was worse than imprecise: 5% of a 0 - 15 control is 0.75 units, so it landed back on the integer it started from about half the time and the press did nothing — invisible to anyone watching a percentage, obvious to anyone watching the number. Percent survives in parentheses, and as the fallback for a card that publishes no Limits: line, where there is no unit to step in. Two ALSA details that bite on real cards A control with both pvolume and cvolume carries two independent values , and amixer sset 'Master' 84% sets both — so adjusting output would drag the capture gain along with it (on rk817_ext, playback 79% and capture 100% collapsing onto one number). Writes to those name playback . amixer itself applies a value before it reads a direction word after it, so sset 'Master' 84% playback still set both; the in-process sset ( audio-device.md §1) honors it. An undirected volume is one value serving both, and is written undirected. amixer cost about 230 ms per call on an RG351V : a fork, an exec, and an ALSA config parse off slow storage. See audio-device.md §1. The maximum, and the signal that sets it An output's maximum is the set of hardware levels it plays at: its 0 dB. The volume only turns down from it, so nothing an app does goes louder. It is defined as what a full-scale sample produces, so it is set with one. Judged with pings at -12 dBFS (as the first ceilings were), an app reaching full scale plays 12 dB louder than anything heard while setting it: four times the amplitude, sixteen times the power, enough to clip the amplifier or rattle the speaker. The SDK clips anything past full scale, so full scale is the most the analog stages ever see. System levels is where it is set, by ear: turn on the test sound ( pings at 0 dBFS ), open System levels, and the pings play past the user level, so what is heard is the ceiling itself. Raise the playback levels until it distorts, rattles, or is too loud, and back off; each move is kept as it is made. On headphones the pings stay at the user level. MDFourier finds it by measurement instead: record it on the line output at each setting, the highest that does not clip. It measures these levels, as it plays past the user level. System defaults: a calibrated board's preset config/audiopresets.json , shipped, holds one entry for each board the team has calibrated on our own image: what the set-up by ear concluded there, the speaker's maximum from SYSTEM LEVELS, the card's rate and buffer size, and the card as it was. A unit an entry applies to starts set up, at those levels. It is not offered the set-up by ear, and the first start's bring-up never runs on it. The match is all or nothing ( audiounit::presetApplies ): the board's model ( /proc/device-tree/model ); the built-in card's ALSA name: A100 Audio Codec , not its id, which the A100 and H616 codecs share; every control of the card: the same ones, each with the same capabilities, range and items. A control the card has and the entry does not is a miss; the control the socket is read from, where the set-up follows the plug. Any miss, and the unit is as it would be with no file: the log says what differed ( audio/preset: … does not apply: … ). A kernel that renames a control or changes its range sends the unit back to the set-up by ear, not to a wrong route. The unit's own wins, and nothing is copied. audiounit::load fills in from the preset what the unit does not keep for itself: its set-up, the built-in card's rate and buffer size, an output's maximum. save writes none of it back: a part that equals the preset's is not the unit's own. So an entry corrected in a later release reaches every unit that has not changed that part. A set-up made by ear on the unit is its own, whatever it concluded. At every start ( applyStartupMixer ), before what the unit keeps by hand and the playing output's route and maximum, every level and switch the entry holds is put back, only where it differs. A control moved behind the page, with amixer or by another program, does not stay moved. An entry has no maximum for headphones: a speaker is the board's, headphones are not, and the first plug's volume of -18 dB ( audio-levels.md ) is what covers them. Making an entry. Calibrate the unit: Set up by ear, then SYSTEM LEVELS. sndstation --audio-preset prints its entry, from its audio.json and the card as read. A person reads it over, fills in who calibrated it and on which image, and adds it to the file. The file's own comment has the format. The match cannot see a driver that changes what a control does without renaming it: the entry still applies, and is wrong. When a card's driver changes, its boards are calibrated again. Not built yet: the page saying whether the unit is on its board's defaults, and going back to them (#268). Until then, removing the unit's entry from audio.json does it. Kept: audio.json, version 3 The user's ~/.config/sndstation/audio.json , one entry per machine, under its key: the chip's ID (the SOC ID on ABOUT), else the board model, else card: and the built-in card's id. Only that key is read. { \"version\": 3, \"RK3326-URKC08010-A599D7394C5B58B6\": { \"set_up\": { \"when\": \"2026-11-05 13:22\", \"detect\": \"inverted\", // normal | inverted | none | mechanical \"route_control\": \"Playback Mux\", \"speaker_switch\": \"Internal Speakers\", // a second control, where there is one \"speaker_alone_with_headphones\": false, \"routes\": { \"speaker\": { \"item\": \"HP\", \"speaker_switch\": \"on\", \"heard\": true }, \"headphones\": { \"item\": \"SPK\", \"speaker_switch\": \"off\", \"heard\": true }, \"speaker+headphones\": { \"item\": \"HP\", \"speaker_switch\": \"on\", \"heard\": true }, \"out\": { \"item\": \"HP\", \"speaker_switch\": \"on\", \"heard\": true } }, \"survey\": { ... } // what was observed, kept beside it }, \"plays_on\": \"auto\", \"built_in\": { // one stream: shared by its outputs \"rate\": 0, \"buffer\": 256, \"mixer\": [ { \"control\": \"Mic Capture Gain\", \"value\": 3, \"range\": [0, 15] } ], \"brought_up\": true }, \"endpoints\": { \"speaker\": { \"volume_db\": -6, \"maximum\": [ { \"control\": \"Master\", \"value\": 180, \"range\": [0, 255] } ] }, \"headphones\": { \"volume_db\": -21 }, \"usb:16c0:048a\": { \"volume_db\": 0, \"rate\": 44100, \"buffer\": 256, \"raised\": true } } } } Hardware values are native units : a level's raw number with the range it was read in, a switch's on/off, an enum's item. A level kept in a range the driver no longer reports (a kernel that turned 0:255 into 0:63) is not applied, and says so. The driver's dB is only ever shown, never kept. volume_db is the software gain, below the maximum: ours, exact. Only the set-up by ear writes set_up (and Corrections, by hand). A unit without one is not set up, whatever else it keeps: a mixer edit or a maximum never makes it look set up. Its board's preset, where one applies, sets it up without writing here ( above ). Speaker, headphones and both share one rate and buffer ( built_in ): one stream, and separate values would reopen it on every plug. A USB or HDMI card keeps its own. A file of any other version is deleted when found , with the device , sample_rate and buffer_frames the old page wrote into the user's system.json , and the unit starts again. Nothing is converted. Every save is a patch of the file as it is ( audiounit::patch ), so two holders of the unit cannot undo each other. system.json keeps what is the platform's: the output format, dither, realtime priority, core, alsa_pcm , and the default rate and buffer size a unit has not changed. What ALSA does and does not promise The raw integer is the firm part : writing 180 gets the same result every time on the same driver. It need not be the register value. dB is metadata the driver author wrote (TLV): missing on many controls, wrong on some. amixer's percent is a third mapping , linear in the raw range. A range can change with the kernel : hence the range kept beside each level. amixer applies a value before it reads a direction word after it, so amixer sset 'PCM' 50 playback set the capture level too. mixerio honors the direction ( Two ALSA details , above). Not yet tried on a unit Built and tested on the fakes ( --fake-audio ), SDL's disk driver, and an alsa-lib control plugin made to answer like the handhelds' cards (mixerio against amixer, byte for byte). Still to try on hardware: the in-process mixer on each family, \"speaker + headphones\" on an rk817_ext and an H700, SYSTEM LEVELS' full-scale pings on a speaker, the startup apply on a unit with a chip ID, and a board's preset on a unit ( test_audiopreset checks it on the H616 fake).","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/audio-stack.html","t":"The audio stack","s":"Subsystems","h":["The audio stack","The rules","The layers","Opening","While it runs","Playing into the stream","MIDI","The launcher's life, and the hand-over","When the device is closed","Handing over to an app","Coming back","Writing an app","Rules for SDK contributors","Diagnostics","What is built, and what is coming"],"x":"The audio stack How sound gets from a program to the speaker on a sndstation unit: who opens the device, who keeps it, and how it passes from the launcher to an app and back. This is the overview. The details are in audio-device.md (choosing the card, formats, timing), audio-levels.md (volume and maximums) and audio-page.md (the AUDIO page). Parts of this are planned and not built yet, first written up as sndstation#251 . Each section says which parts: Now is what the code does today, and #251 is what it is moving to. The order they are built in is the table at the end. The rules One owner per process. Exactly one place in a process opens the audio device and the MIDI ports, and keeps them. In the launcher, that is the launcher. In an app, it is the app. Everything else plays into the owner's stream and never opens, closes or reopens a device. ALSA directly, on a unit. Playback goes from AudioOut to alsa-lib, through the SDK's own stream ( alsastream ). SDL may start its audio subsystem and list devices, but on Linux it never plays. SDL's own stream is for macOS and Windows development builds, and for tests that use its disk driver. Open once, close rarely. Opening and closing a device is what pops. The owner opens at start and closes only when it must (see \"When the device is closed\" below). Every open, close and reopen fades. Leave nothing behind. A process that started with the device free hands it on free, and hands on its environment as it found it ( AUDIODEV , SDL_AUDIODRIVER ). The layers your sound a synth, a sampler, a plugin, a test tone │ render(out, frames) on the audio thread ▼ audiomix::Mixer sums the sources, fades each one in and out │ the owner's RenderFn ▼ AudioOut which card, the conversion to its format, dither, │ the system volume, meters, health, card changes ▼ alsastream the SDK's alsa-lib stream: snd_pcm_writei on the card │ ▼ ALSA → the card → speaker / headphones / USB interface A source knows nothing about devices. It fills interleaved float stereo when asked, at the rate it is told ( rateChanged() ). AudioOut is the device. It picks the card the way handhelds need: the card itself ( hw:CARD=<id>,DEV=0 ), not default , skipping display outputs and virtual cards ( audio-device.md §3.3 ). It converts to the card's format in one loop that also counts clips and dithers. It applies the system volume, ramped. It keeps the callback's health: gaps, underruns and CPU. It follows a USB card that is plugged in or pulled. alsastream is what plays. An AudioOut is handed the unit's settings (rate, period and format, from the AUDIO page) and its realtime priority ( rt.h ). SDL is not on that path. Now: The launcher has one stream and a mixer ( launcher/audio.h ). An app's AudioOut has its own render function, which is the app's mix. On Linux, AudioOut plays every device through alsastream : the card it chose, AUDIODEV or audio.alsa_pcm as given, and default with whatever the system put behind it (a plug , a dmix , a sound server's ALSA plugin). SDL's audio is started on its ALSA driver for the log's device list, whatever it would pick first ( audio-device.md §3.0). Not yet measured on a unit with a sound server (KNULLI) or a dmix : alsastream starts with the whole buffer queued and blocks in snd_pcm_writei , as aplay does. Underruns (AUDIO → Diagnostics) and latency through default against SNDSTATION_AUDIO_STREAM=sdl on the same unit settle it; if a server needs it, default alone gets a smaller start threshold. Opening The owner does this once, when it starts: #include <sndstation/audioout.h> #include <sndstation/rt.h> AudioOut::Settings set = AudioOut::loadSettings(); // the unit's: card, rate, period, format rt::applySettings(set.realtimePriority, set.cpu); // the audio thread's priority and core AudioOut out; out.open(set, &render, &rateChanged, this); // opened paused out.start(); // fades in loadSettings() reads the platform's system.json and the unit's audio.json. The AUDIO page writes the latter, and every program on the unit opens with the same settings, so an app plays on the card, at the rate and period the user set up. With no device (a headless run with --shots , or nothing that opens), open() still succeeds, and the owner calls out.pumpManual(dt) from its own loop. Everything above the device keeps running and stays testable. While it runs Once a frame, on the main thread, the owner calls: Call What it does out.service() a USB card plugged in or pulled: the stream moves to the card the rules choose now, and the sources are told the new rate out.followJack() the headphone socket: the right output's volume and maximum out.pumpManual(dt) only with no device: renders the time that passed Only the owner changes the device. In the launcher, the AUDIO page is the only place that reopens it: a new period ( reopen() ), rate ( setRate() ) or card ( setDevice() ). Each of those fades out, reopens and fades in ( audio-device.md §3.6). The system volume is not the owner's. Vol± and the AUDIO page set it per output, every program shares it, and AudioOut applies it after the render function ( audio-levels.md ). Playing into the stream A page, the game or a plugin is a source attached to the owner's mixer ( audiomix.h ): struct Tone : audiomix::Source { void render(float* out, int frames) override { /* add into out */ } void rateChanged(int rate) override { /* re-tune */ } }; Tone tone; launcherAudio.mixer().attach(&tone); // fades in from the next block // ... launcherAudio.mixer().detach(&tone); // fades out; returns once the audio thread let go The mixer applies the system volume to each source, unless the source says it applies its own ( ownVolume() ): the AUDIO page plays past it while an output's maximum is judged. A source fades in when attached and out when detached, and detach() returns once the audio thread has let go, so the source may then be destroyed. Now: the AUDIO page and the game are sources on the launcher's stream. Built alone ( --shots , the tests), each has a stream of its own, opened and closed with it, as before. A CLAP plugin is played the same way. ClapPlayer opens no device and no MIDI ports: In the launcher , it is a source on the launcher's mixer, activated at the stream's rate ( rateChanged() activates it again at a new one), and the launcher's MIDI is pointed at it. In an app with an engine of its own , the app activates it ( activate(rate, maxFrames) ), its audio thread calls ClapPlayer::process() with its notes and parameter changes, timed within the block, and mixes the result itself. The app lists and sets its parameters ( params() , setParam() ) and keeps its state as bytes in its own files ( saveState() , loadState() ). A restart the plugin asks for is answered at its next block: silence while it is activated again on the main thread ( update() ). MIDI MIDI follows the same rule. The owner starts it ( midi::start() ), listens to the unit's inputs ( midi::listen() ), and stops it ( midi::stop() ) at the same moments it closes the audio device. A page or a plugin never starts or stops MIDI itself. In the launcher that is LauncherMidi ( launcher/launchermidi.h ): started with the launcher, it hands what arrives to the page in front that takes it (a played plugin) or to nobody, and a page's sink is taken away only once a message on its way to it has been delivered. Closed for the hand-over and every way out, let go of while the screensaver is up and while the unit sleeps. The MIDI monitor opens every port itself, each with its own sink, so while it shows the listening is paused and the ports are its; the launcher listens again after. The launcher's life, and the hand-over The launcher is one turn of a loop. sndstation-session starts it, the launcher replaces itself with the app you pick ( exec ), and when the app exits the session starts the launcher again. There is never a launcher and an app at once, so the device always has exactly one owner. sndstation-session │ ▼ ┌─ LAUNCHER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ start: window → audio OPEN, fade in → MIDI start │ │ │ │ pages, the game, a plugin ── play into the stream (sources) │ │ AUDIO page ── may REOPEN (period, rate, card), with fades │ │ │ │ screensaver / sleep ── fade out, CLOSE, MIDI stop (device free) │ │ a button / waking ── OPEN, fade in, MIDI start │ │ │ │ app picked: fade out → CLOSE → MIDI stop → AUDIODEV as found │ │ → SDL shut down → exec(app) │ └──────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘ │ the same process, now the app ▼ ┌─ APP ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ start: AudioOut OPEN with loadSettings() (the same card and timing) │ │ runs: service() / followJack() each frame │ │ exit: CLOSE (AUDIODEV put back), MIDI stop, exit │ └──────────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘ │ the app's process ends ▼ sndstation-session starts the launcher again → LAUNCHER start (above) When the device is closed The launcher closes the device only: when it hands over to an app; when it shuts down, restarts or exits, whatever the path; when the screensaver starts, or the unit goes to sleep; when the AUDIO page reopens it for a new period, rate or card. Nothing else closes it, and it is opened again at the end of each of these but the first two. Handing over to an app Fade out, then close , as every close does. The app opens a free card. Two processes can't share a card without a sound server, so the hand-over is a short fade rather than nothing. MIDI stopped. The app opens the ports it wants. AUDIODEV as the launcher found it. An AudioOut writes AUDIODEV while it plays and puts it back on its last close, and launch() sets it once more to what the launcher was started with. The app chooses its card by the same rules as when it is started from a shell. (Before this was fixed, an app could inherit the launcher's choice, take it for a user's, and play through SDL's stream instead: see the changelog .) SDL shut down, then exec . The app gets the display, the pads and the card, in the same process, with SNDSTATION_SESSION=1 in its environment. Coming back The app exits, its AudioOut closes (the destructor does it too), and the process ends. The session starts the launcher afresh, and the launcher opens the device as at any start. Nothing is carried over, so a card that was plugged in while the app ran is the one the launcher opens. Writing an app Open one AudioOut at start, with AudioOut::loadSettings() , and close it at exit. Don't open a second one, and don't close and reopen it to change what plays: mix inside your own render function. Call service() and followJack() once a frame. Leave the volume to the system unless you set appVolume and apply volumeGain() yourself. Don't set AUDIODEV , and don't open devices through SDL. A user who sets AUDIODEV is making a choice, and the SDK honors it. With SNDSTATION_SESSION=1 , quitting is going back. Close what you opened, and exit. The session brings the launcher back. Hosting plugins: load them with ClapPlayer , activate them at your stream's rate, and call process() from your own audio thread, or attach them to an audiomix::Mixer . Point your MIDI at them (they are midi::Sink s); never let them open a device. Rules for SDK contributors Only the owner calls open() , close() , reopen() , setRate() or setDevice() . A new piece that makes sound is a source. No SDL_OpenAudioDevice on Linux. SDL's audio subsystem may list devices and pick its driver; playback goes through alsastream . No setenv(\"AUDIODEV\") outside AudioOut 's own writer , which puts it back. Every change to a running stream fades. test_audioout checks for steps. Diagnostics The log, in order, says what happened: audio: driver 'alsa', N playback device(s) : SDL's list, for diagnosis only. audio: ALSA device '<pcm>' (<why>) : which device, and which rule chose it. (AUDIODEV, set outside) in an app started from the launcher means something leaked : report it. audio: <rate> Hz, <frames> frames, handed <format> : what the device accepted. The stream's health (gaps, underruns, clips, CPU) is on AUDIO → Diagnostics. To check that nothing holds the card during the screensaver, run fuser -v /dev/snd/* . SNDSTATION_AUDIO_STREAM=sdl plays through SDL's stream on the same device, to compare the two: a developer's switch, and each open through it says so in the log, as does a build that plays through SDL's stream for any other reason ( audio: SDL's stream, not alsa-lib's: ... ). What is built, and what is coming In this order, each its own pull request: Now Coming The device picked by the SDK (card itself, not default ) yes — AUDIODEV put back on the last close; the launcher hands it on as found yes — Playback through alsastream , every device, on Linux yes (not yet measured behind a sound server) — Fades at open, close and reopen yes — One stream in the launcher, with a mixer of sources yes: AUDIO, the game, a played plugin — The screensaver and sleep free the audio device yes — ClapPlayer without a device, playable in an app's engine yes — The launcher owns MIDI the same way, freed by the screensaver and sleep yes — SETTINGS → POWER: the screensaver's kind and wait, Sleep after no 1 No pop at the hand-over to an app — not planned: two processes need a sound server","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/device-events.html","t":"Device events","s":"Subsystems","h":["Device events","The usual app","Where a switch can come from","evdev","Hotplug","hallkey: profiled, never guessed","Motion: the accelerometer, the gyro, and which edge is up","The lid policy: what closing the lid does","Who else reads it","Proposal: events, not polling (for discussion)","Open questions","Other operating systems","Testing without a unit"],"x":"Device events <sndstation/deviceevents.h> reports what the unit's body says about itself: a clamshell's lid, the headphone and line-out sockets, a tablet-mode or rotate-lock switch, and an accelerometer or gyro if it has one. SDL's gamepad API never sees any of it, so the SDK reads it from the kernel directly. It uses no SDL and no threads, and it never grabs a device. The app calls poll() once a frame from its own loop, and logind and everything else watching the same nodes keep seeing them. The usual app sndstation::DeviceEvents dev; dev.open(); // /dev/input, and the hallkey door if profiled if (dev.has(sndstation::Switch::Lid)) { /* offer the LID input */ } // once a frame for (const sndstation::DeviceEvent& e : dev.poll()) { if (e.sw == sndstation::Switch::Lid) lidClosed(e.on); // on = closed } poll() doesn't block. It drains every node, then returns each switch whose state differs from the last call. A switch that flips and flips back within one call is no change: the app only learns where it ended up. on(Switch) is the state now. A switch carried by two nodes counts as on while either holds it. timestamp_ns is CLOCK_MONOTONIC, which does not advance across a system suspend. The open itself is not news: poll() reports changes from then on. A node that arrives later by hotplug is news, and its switches that are on are reported. Switch 's values are the kernel's own SW_* codes, so the mapping is a cast. switchName() gives \"lid\" , \"headphones\" and so on for logs. Where a switch can come from Every door that was tried is kept in scanned() , the way the audio socket's scan is ( SocketSource ). A unit that reports nothing can still say where it looked. kind where what it said evdev /dev/input/event3 lid , headphones (on: headphones) , no switches , an errno hotplug /dev/input watching for nodes coming and going (inotify) , or why not hallkey a sysfs path reads 1, closed reads 0 , unreadable , not configured evdev Every /dev/input/event* that advertises any EV_SW bit is kept open. Nodes are found by capability, never by number, since the numbers move with probe order, and are scanned in event-number order, so the report is repeatable. Initial state comes from EVIOCGSW , not from waiting for an event: a switch that nobody is moving emits nothing. SYN_DROPPED means the kernel's buffer overflowed. As the kernel documents, everything up to the next SYN_REPORT is skipped, and then the state is asked for outright. Permission: /dev/input/event* is root:input 0660 once udev has run, so the user has to be in input . A refused node is a row with Permission denied (is the user in the input group?) . Hotplug open() starts an inotify watch on the input directory. poll() drains it before reading anything. A new node with switches is opened and added. The watch includes IN_ATTRIB , because udev creates a node root-only and opens it up a moment later. A first refusal is followed by a second try that works. A removed node , or one whose read fails with ENODEV , is dropped, and any switch it was holding on reports off. Both are recorded in scanned() , one row per node saying what happened last, so a dock that comes and goes doesn't grow the list. Units with only built-in switches behave the same as with a single scan at open() . hallkey: profiled, never guessed The Allwinner BSP kernels that the stock Anbernic images ship report the RG35XX SP's lid as a hallkey attribute under /sys/class/power_supply , not through evdev. The door's code lives here. Which node to read and which value means closed come from the unit's entry in the platform's deviceevents.json , never from code: \"Anbernic RG35XX SP\": { \"hallkey\": { \"node\": \"axp2202-battery\", \"closed\": 0 } } The file is keyed and merged like leds.json : \"*\" , then the board model, then the unit id, widest first. node is a power_supply node's name or an absolute path. Both fields are needed. A unit with no entry never tries the door , so the polarity is never guessed on hardware nobody has measured, and scanned() lists it as not configured . The file ships empty: the SP on the sndstation rootfs runs a mainline kernel, and its lid is evdev gpio-keys-lid (value 1 = closed), with no hallkey at all. hallkeyFromProfile() is that lookup through appcfg::platform . hallkeyFor(json, board, unit) is the same thing on a file already read. Motion: the accelerometer, the gyro, and which edge is up Motion is the second kind of source. It is read as state, not reported as events: an accelerometer changes every frame, and the app wants the latest reading. if (dev.hasMotion(sndstation::Motion::Accelerometer)) { sndstation::MotionSample a = dev.motion(sndstation::Motion::Accelerometer); // a.x, a.y, a.z in m/s^2; dev.motion(Motion::Gyroscope) in rad/s if (dev.orientation() != lastOrientation) { /* turned */ } } open() scans /sys/bus/iio/devices . Every iio:device* with in_accel_{x,y,z}_raw or in_anglvel_{x,y,z}_raw is a sensor, and every poll() takes one reading per kind, from the first sensor of that kind that reads. Units: each axis is (raw + offset) * scale . The per-axis _x_scale / _x_offset is used before the shared _scale / _offset . The IIO ABI makes this m/s² for accelerometers and rad/s for gyros. The unit's own axes: x to the right, y up the panel, z out of it. The sensor's in_accel_mount_matrix (or mount_matrix ) turns its readings into them, so a chip soldered on a quarter turn round reads the same as one that isn't. Lying flat and face up, a unit reads z ≈ +9.8. orientation() : which edge is up ( Up , Left , Right , Down ), or Flat , worked out from the accelerometer. An edge counts once it is within 35° of vertical, and Flat within 35° of face up or down. Between those, the previous answer stands, so a unit held at 45° doesn't flicker. A reading too weak to show gravity (falling, shaken) changes nothing. orientationOf(x, y, z, previous) is the same rule as a pure function. Each IIO device is a row in scanned() (kind iio ), with its name, scale and mount matrix. A device without motion channels, such as a battery ADC, says no motion channels . Not yet: nothing drives display.rotate from orientation, and how the RG Rotate reports its rotation hasn't been looked at. The test hook is addIio(dir) , which the test points at a fake IIO tree. The lid policy: what closing the lid does What a shut lid does is the platform's setting, shared by every app on the unit, in system.json : \"system\": { \"lid\": \"suspend\" } value the unit the app \"suspend\" (shipped) sleeps when the lid shuts and wakes when it opens. This is logind's own default. gets the Lid event a moment before the freeze. On the SP that window was about 3.6 s, measured, so it should only get ready (stop the audio, darken the lights). \"keep-playing\" stays awake keeps playing, and may blank the panel \"off\" stays awake takes no notice of the lid Any other value reads as \"suspend\" , with a warning on stderr, so a typo can't leave a unit awake in a bag. An app never suspends the unit itself. logind does the suspending, and the platform tells logind what to do: At boot, sndstation-lid.service runs before systemd-logind . It's a oneshot, linked into /etc/systemd/system on a handheld by the package. It runs sndstation-lid-apply . That runs sndstation --logind-lid as the user tty1 logs in, so the user's ~/.config/sndstation/system.json is merged over the shipped one. With no such user, it uses the shipped policy alone. The launcher prints a logind drop-in and exits before touching the display. sndstation-lid-apply writes it to /etc/systemd/logind.conf.d/sndstation-lid.conf : ini [Login] HandleLidSwitch=suspend # ignore for keep-playing and off HandleLidSwitchExternalPower=suspend # likewise HandleLidSwitchDocked=ignore # logind's default, whatever the policy It replaces only a drop-in that carries its own marker line. One that someone else wrote is left alone, and the script says so. 4. logind starts and reads it. A change applies from the next boot. logind is never restarted under a running session. sndstation-lid-apply --print shows what would be written. Removing the package removes the unit, its link and the drop-in. In code: lidPolicy() reads the merged setting, lidPolicyFrom(json) does the same on a file already read, lidPolicyName() gives the string, and logindLidDropIn(policy) gives the drop-in text. Who else reads it The audio socket's input door ( audiodev::readSocket() , see audio-device.md §4 ) does a one-shot scan: open(dir, psDir, HallkeyConfig{}, false) , with no hallkey and no hotplug watch. It takes its answer from the rows. Each row carries has and on (the switches the door carries and which are on, kept current by poll() ) and error (the errno when it couldn't be opened), so a consumer never parses note . Proposal: events, not polling (for discussion) Not built. Today the app calls poll() once a frame, as above. This is how the SDK could tell every program about switches, cards, the battery and the network without anyone polling, and it is what the system volume in audio-levels.md needs to follow a headphone plug while an app runs (today it follows a card change, and an app can call AudioOut::refreshEndpoint() ). The SDK already has the pieces. They are apart, and two of them poll: What Today lid, headphone and line-out sockets, other switches DeviceEvents::poll() , called by the program once a frame. No program calls it yet battery sysfacts::readBattery() , re-read by each program on a timer of its own (the launcher: every 30 s) the card played to (a USB interface coming or going) AudioOut::service() , which reads /proc/asound every half second network the launcher's alone Every one of these has a kernel source that wakes a reader when, and only when, something changes: What Woken by switches the evdev nodes DeviceEvents already holds open, and inotify for nodes coming and going (already there) a socket whose only detect is an ALSA control ALSA control events ( snd_ctl_subscribe_events on /dev/snd/controlC* , alsa-lib, already linked for MIDI). No amixer, no polling cards coming and going the kernel's uevent netlink socket ( sound subsystem) battery: charging, full, plugged the same uevent socket ( power_supply change events) network rtnetlink: links and addresses So: One SDK thread sleeps in epoll_wait on all of them. While nothing happens it costs nothing: it does not run, and it has no timer. What it learns arrives as an SDL event , in the event loop every app already has, beside a gamepad being plugged in. That is one event type, registered once ( SDL_RegisterEvents ), with the kind in user.code : Switch, Battery, Audio, Network. An app that ignores the type is unchanged. It is an event, not a callback, so the app decides when to act: it handles the event where its loop gets to it, on its own thread. A callback would run app code on the SDK's thread, and every app would need locks for it. It does the platform's part first : on a headphone plug, the profile's route and mute (one amixer fork, on a plug only, and never on the audio thread); on a card coming or going, AudioOut 's move to it; in both cases, the switch of endpoint and volume. Then it posts the event. An app that leaves the volume to the SDK needs to do nothing at all. An app that shows the volume, or wants to fade rather than jump, looks at the Audio event. - DeviceEvents::poll() and AudioOut::service() stay , as no-ops once the thread runs, so a program that calls them today keeps working. The one thing the kernel does not announce everywhere is the battery percentage: some fuel gauges send a change event as it drops, others only on charging and full. On those, \"low\" can only be found by reading capacity , and the question is how rarely (question 2 below). Open questions The thread in each SDK program, or one platform process that sleeps on the same sources for every program? Either costs nothing while nothing happens. The process also covers ports and programs outside the SDK, but it is one more thing beside a real-time app, and it still has to tell the app. Battery percentage on a gauge that sends no change event : read capacity how rarely, or only report charging, full and plugged? Other operating systems Evdev and inotify are Linux's ( src/deviceevents_linux.cpp ). Any other build gets src/deviceevents_null.cpp , the way MIDI has midi_null.cpp . There, only the hallkey door can answer, and scanned() says no evdev on this OS . Testing without a unit tests/test_deviceevents.cpp needs no hardware: Pipes stand in for evdev nodes, through addEvdevFd(fd, has, onNow, name) . The test writes struct input_event records into them. setEvdevOps({probe, state}) swaps out the ioctls, so a FIFO in a temp directory passes for a node. That's how hotplug add and remove run in a temp dir standing in for /dev/input , and how SYN_DROPPED gets its re-read. A fake power_supply directory drives the hallkey door, configured and not, through open(..., HallkeyConfig) and addHallkey(path, closedValue) .","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/gamepad-layout.html","t":"Gamepad names, prints and layout","s":"Subsystems","h":["Gamepad names, prints and layout","Names: one standard, by position","Prints: what is on the button","Layout: where things are","Which body this is","CLAP","Open"],"x":"Gamepad names, prints and layout Three things about every control on a pad: the one name the project uses for it, what is printed on it, and where it is. Built in the SDK, apart from choosing among several bodies that share one ID (below, \"Which body this is\"). In the SDK ( pad.h , buttons.h ): pad::standardLabel(b) the standard name: \"A\" for East, always pad::label(b) what the pad in use prints there; the standard name when it prints nothing known pad::layout() the pad in use's layout: its name, frontW / frontH , and controls by key pad::control(b) , pad::control(\"LSTICK\") one control's print, printAt , panel , and, when placed , x , y , w , h , rotation , shape tests/test_layout.cpp checks all of it on virtual pads. Names: one standard, by position Docs, code and config call the face buttons by the Nintendo/handheld letters, and each letter means a position, whatever is printed there : name position pad:: config key old bit name A East East EAST JOY_BUTTON1 B South South SOUTH JOY_BUTTON2 X North North NORTH JOY_BUTTON3 Y West West WEST JOY_BUTTON4 The rest are named as they are: Up, Down, Left, Right (the d-pad), L1, L2, R1, R2, L3, R3 (the stick clicks), Start, Select, Menu, Vol+, Vol−, and the sticks LX, LY, RX, RY. So \"A\" in a doc is the East button on every pad, including an Xbox pad that prints A on the South one and a unit that prints something else entirely. A doc that tells a person what to press names the position the first time: \"A (East)\". This is a name, not a print. What the screen shows is the print. Prints: what is on the button Each control in a layout (below) can say what is printed on or next to it, exactly as written, in UTF-8: \"A\" , \"✕\" , \"△\" , \"SELECT\" , \"−\" , \"☰\" , \"TL\" . It cannot give the print's color: Control has no field for one (see Open). A control with nothing printed has \"\" . pad::label(b) returns the print when the unit's layout is known, and the standard name otherwise. The launcher and every app that asks the SDK show the right print with no change of their own. An app that writes \"A\" itself has chosen the standard name; that is its call. A pad with no layout in the database still gets its prints when SDL knows its family: an Xbox pad (360, One, Series) prints A at the bottom, B, X and Y round it, and a PlayStation pad ✕ ○ □ △. That is prints only, with no geometry, and a layout in the database always wins. A Nintendo pad prints the standard names already. Layout: where things are A new table, \"layouts\" , beside \"maps\" in gamepads.json , named like the maps. A device entry points to one with \"layout\": \"<name>\" . A map says which SDL button is which position; a layout says what that position looks like on this body. They are separate because one map serves several bodies. The XU-10 (MagicX/COOPREME, RK3326) is the case that shows why. It is Xbox-lettered, so every face button prints something other than its standard name. Its Menu button is a \"G\" on the right-hand edge, under the headphone socket, and its shoulders are on the back, not the top. Measured from photos of each side, scaled to its 130 mm height: \"layouts\": { \"xu10\": { \"front_mm\": [82, 130], \"controls\": { \"NORTH\": { \"print\": \"Y\", \"at\": [64.5, 74.8], \"size\": [8, 8], \"shape\": \"round\" }, \"WEST\": { \"print\": \"X\", \"at\": [56.7, 82.8], \"size\": [8, 8], \"shape\": \"round\" }, \"EAST\": { \"print\": \"B\", \"at\": [72.5, 82.8], \"size\": [8, 8], \"shape\": \"round\" }, \"SOUTH\": { \"print\": \"A\", \"at\": [64.7, 90.7], \"size\": [8, 8], \"shape\": \"round\" }, \"UP\": { \"at\": [17.5, 76.5], \"size\": [8, 10.5] }, \"LEFT\": { \"at\": [10.9, 83.2], \"size\": [10.5, 8] }, \"RIGHT\": { \"at\": [24.2, 83.0], \"size\": [10.5, 8] }, \"DOWN\": { \"at\": [17.5, 89.6], \"size\": [8, 10.5] }, \"SELECT\": { \"print\": \"SELECT\", \"print_at\": \"below\", \"at\": [36.7, 73.2], \"size\": [8, 6], \"shape\": \"pill\" }, \"START\": { \"print\": \"START\", \"print_at\": \"below\", \"at\": [45.8, 73.2], \"size\": [8, 6], \"shape\": \"pill\" }, \"LSTICK\": { \"at\": [24.4, 111.6], \"size\": [17, 17], \"shape\": \"round\" }, \"RSTICK\": { \"at\": [57.2, 111.3], \"size\": [17, 17], \"shape\": \"round\" }, \"L1\": { \"print\": \"L1\", \"print_at\": \"below\", \"panel\": \"back\", \"at\": [5.8, 45], \"size\": [16, 14] }, \"L2\": { \"print\": \"L2\", \"print_at\": \"below\", \"panel\": \"back\", \"at\": [23.4, 45], \"size\": [18, 14] }, \"R2\": { \"print\": \"R2\", \"print_at\": \"below\", \"panel\": \"back\", \"at\": [57.7, 45], \"size\": [18, 14] }, \"R1\": { \"print\": \"R1\", \"print_at\": \"below\", \"panel\": \"back\", \"at\": [75.3, 45], \"size\": [15, 14] }, \"MENU\": { \"print\": \"G\", \"panel\": \"right\", \"at\": [10.1, 28.9], \"size\": [4, 9.5], \"shape\": \"pill\" }, \"VOLUP\": { \"print\": \"+\", \"panel\": \"left\", \"at\": [9.9, 11.9], \"size\": [4, 8] }, \"VOLDOWN\": { \"print\": \"−\", \"panel\": \"left\", \"at\": [9.9, 23.9], \"size\": [4, 8] }, \"HEADPHONES\": { \"panel\": \"right\", \"at\": [8.9, 14.0], \"size\": [4, 4], \"shape\": \"round\" } } } }, \"devices\": [ { \"device\": \"XU10 Gamepad\", \"vid\": \"0xC3B0\", \"pid\": \"0x0200\", \"map\": \"xu10\", \"layout\": \"xu10\" } ] What an app learns from it: A doc's \"A (East)\" is the button printed B, and the screen says B. Start's center is 14.5 mm from X's; Select's is 22 mm from X and 16 mm from the d-pad. Start is the one a thumb on the face buttons finds. The shoulders are on the back, 45 mm down, under the fingers that hold it, not on the top edge. G is on the right edge, 29 mm down: reached by moving the right hand, not by a thumb. The rules: Millimeters , on the front as the user holds it: origin at the top left, x to the right, y down. A control is placed by its center ( at ) and its bounding size , with shape one of round , rect or pill , and an optional clockwise rotation in degrees. Photos of known size are good enough; ±2 mm does not change an ergonomic answer. front_mm is the size of the front. The sticks are LSTICK and RSTICK ; their clicks, L3 and R3, are where the stick is and need no place of their own. Other panels: \"panel\" is back , top , bottom , left or right (the front is the default). Every panel uses the front's x and y as seen from the front, so L1 is on the left wherever it is: the back is seen through the unit, not from behind. An edge has no extent in one of them and uses the depth from the front face instead: the top and bottom are [x, depth] , the sides [depth, y] . print_at says where a print is when it is beside the control rather than on it: above , below , left or right . The headphone socket may be placed as well ( \"HEADPHONES\" ), with no print: a cable coming out of an edge is part of how the unit is held. A control the layout leaves out has no position, and an app must not assume one. A device with no layout has no geometry: its prints are its SDL family's, as above, or the standard names. A user's own gamepads.json (the one the CONTROLS mapper writes) replaces the shipped file, and one saved before layouts existed has none. So the shipped file's layouts are always read too: a device whose entry has no \"layout\" takes the shipped entry's, and a layout name is looked up in the user's file first, then the shipped one. An app can then ask what it needs. For example, a music app can ask whether Select is close enough to the face buttons that it gets pressed by accident, and leave it out of a modifier on such a body. Which body this is An external pad is its device entry: vid, pid and name. The built-in controls belong to the unit, and one ID does not always mean one body: clones share USB IDs and a device tree, and differ in prints and placement. Where that is known, the device entry lists the candidates, \"layout\": [\"r36s-a\", \"r36s-b\"] . CONTROLS asks once, with a drawing of each, and keeps the choice for this unit ( unit-identity.md ), as the audio profile is kept. Not built yet: the list is read, and its first layout is used (the log says so). The CONTROLS question is still to come. CLAP The fields are those of an element in the embedded-hardware draft ( clap_extensions/embedded-hardware.h , clap.embedded-hardware/1 ), so a host fills the draft straight from the layout (not done yet: the host still reports one panel with no geometry): layout draft element / panel print label nothing: a layout has no print color label_color , left unset at x_mm , y_mm size width_mm , height_mm rotation rotation_deg shape shape print_at label_position panel panel_id (front, back, an edge) front_mm the front panel's width_mm , height_mm no layout NO_GEOMETRY The draft places panels on \"the device's front\" only. It has no word for the back or an edge, and the XU-10 has controls on both: a point to raise with the draft. The headphone socket is the draft's two-position SWITCH. Open Which unit families need the per-unit choice, and the CONTROLS question that makes it. The CLAP host describing the device from the layout. Layouts for the other units: so far the XU-10 (measured), the PlayStation Classic controller (✕ ○ □ △, placed from its size), and an Xbox pad's prints (the PowerA). A print's color (the PlayStation symbols' green, red, blue and pink): not in the format. Control has no color field, so there is nowhere for one to go yet.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/getting-started/images.html","t":"Getting an image, and going back","s":"Getting started","h":["Getting an image, and going back","Where images come from","Which image each unit runs","Before you flash: keep the stock card","Flashing a card","Going back to the stock firmware","Problems"],"x":"Getting an image, and going back Draft for #168. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. A private-beta tester runs sndstation from an SD card image. This page says where the image for each unit comes from, how to put it on a card, and how to go back to the unit's stock firmware. Where images come from Images are built in sndstation/distribution , a private repository. How an invitee gets one (read access to that repository, or an image handed over) is TODO(#236/#124). Whether the repository later opens is TODO(#236/#120). Do not look for a public download: none is published. Which image each unit runs Each unit runs the sndstation rootfs, ROCKNIX or its stock image. The choice per unit is TODO(#236/#124). What the docs record: Slot Unit Image 1 Anbernic RG351V TODO(#236/#124) 2 R36S Blue and R40XX TODO(#236/#124) 3 XU10 TODO(#236/#124) 4 Powkiddy RGB30 TODO(#236/#124) 5 Anbernic RG40XX H TODO(#236/#124). Its LED rings were verified under ROCKNIX ( stick-leds.md ) 6 TRIMUI Smart Pro TODO(#236/#124) 7 Anbernic RG35XX SP needs two : the sndstation rootfs (the lid is evdev) and a stock BSP image (where hallkey can be read). Which BSP image: TODO(#236/#124) 8 Raspberry Pi 4, OS Lite TODO(#236/#124) The sndstation rootfs is a Debian system. Where one is not offered for a unit, you can install the platform onto a Debian or Ubuntu image the unit already runs: see Installation . Before you flash: keep the stock card Flashing overwrites the card. To be able to go back: Keep the original card and flash a second one. This is the safest. Or image the stock card first , with the card in a reader. On Linux, after finding the device as below: sh sudo dd if=/dev/sdX of=stock-backup.img bs=4M status=progress Keep stock-backup.img somewhere safe. It is as large as the card. Whether any of the eight boots from internal storage rather than the card is TODO(#236/#124). This page assumes the card. Flashing a card Check the device name twice. dd writes to whatever it is told to, and a wrong name overwrites a disk you want. Look at the size and the label. If you would rather not use a terminal, a graphical tool such as balenaEtcher writes an image to a card and checks it. Pick the image, pick the card, flash. With dd : # Linux: find the card; it is the one that matches your card's size lsblk sudo dd if=the-image.img of=/dev/sdX bs=4M conv=fsync status=progress # macOS diskutil list diskutil unmountDisk /dev/diskN sudo dd if=the-image.img of=/dev/rdiskN bs=4m If the image is compressed, decompress it first. The image's format is TODO(#236/#124). When it finishes, eject the card, put it in the unit and switch on. A working install boots to the launcher: see Installation, Verifying . Going back to the stock firmware Put the unit's original card back, or reflash the vendor's stock image onto a card: Get the stock image from the vendor's own firmware page for that model. Do not use a copy of unknown origin. Flash it as above, or write back your stock-backup.img . Boot. The unit is as it was. This assumes the unit boots from the card. If it boots from internal storage, going back differs: TODO(#236/#124). Slot 7 (RG35XX SP) needs a stock BSP image anyway, for the hallkey reading; keep it. Problems If a unit does not boot after flashing, reflash the card, and try a second card if it still fails. A hardware result, good or bad, goes to reporting.md . Known problems are in known-issues.md .","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/getting-started/installation.html","t":"Installation","s":"Getting started","h":["Installation","From flashing a card to the launcher","Building from source","Installing on a handheld","Verifying"],"x":"Installation Draft for #169. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. From flashing a card to the launcher For private-beta testers. You need a supported unit ( supported.md ) and an SD card. Get the image for your unit and keep the stock card or image it first: images.md . Flash the card and put it in the unit: images.md . Switch on. The unit boots into the launcher on its own screen: the install sets tty1 to log the user in and run sndstation-session ( launcher.md , \"Booting into it\"). Check it as in Verifying . On the first run, the launcher asks to map the pad if it does not know it, and to set up the sound by ear. Both can be skipped and done later from Controls and Audio. Report what you see , good or bad: reporting.md . If the image already carries sndstation, you are done. If you started from a plain Debian or Ubuntu image, install the package as in Installing on a handheld . What the install does as root is listed in deb-root.md . Going back to the stock firmware is in images.md . Whether a given image carries sndstation already is TODO(#236/#124). Building from source For developers. The third-party libraries in third_party/ are git submodules, so clone with them, or fetch them into a checkout you already have (and again after a pull that moves one): git clone --recurse-submodules <url> git submodule update --init --recursive # in an existing checkout Debian or Ubuntu, on the handheld itself or a desktop: sudo apt install build-essential cmake pkg-config libsdl2-dev libsdl2-ttf-dev libsdl2-image-dev libasound2-dev cmake -S . -B build cmake --build build -j4 ./build/sndstation # the launcher, in a 640x480 window on a desktop Tested with SDL 2.0.20 (Ubuntu 22.04, the oldest it is built on) and SDL 2.30 (Ubuntu 24.04). alsa-lib ( libasound2-dev , which libsdl2-dev pulls in anyway) gives the audio stream, the mixer, and MIDI their ALSA side; built without it, sound is SDL's and MIDI has no devices. On macOS, for development: brew install pkg-config sdl2 sdl2_ttf sdl2_image , then the same cmake lines. A desktop build is fully usable for development. The keyboard stands in for the gamepad (arrows, Z/X, Return, Backspace), and --fake-audio rk817 answers as a handheld's sound card. --fake-net gives SETTINGS → Network an in-memory unit: networks to join, Bluetooth devices to pair, and USB networking and SSH to turn on. ./build/sndstation --shots DIR writes every screen as a BMP without a display. Tests, git hooks, and the pre-push checks are in CONTRIBUTING . Installing on a handheld A tagged release ( v0.12.0 , …) carries two packages for arm64 units, built on Ubuntu 22.04. They install on Ubuntu 22.04 and later and Debian bookworm and later. Releases up to 0.15.0 were built on Debian trixie and need trixie or later: sndstation_<version>_arm64.deb , the platform; sndstation-vendors_<version>_all.deb , the unit's vendors. How an invitee downloads them is TODO(#236/#120/#122). Copy both to the unit and install them together: sudo apt install ./sndstation_*_arm64.deb ./sndstation-vendors_*_all.deb To package a checkout instead, run packaging/make-deb.sh on the unit. It builds for the system it runs on and writes both packages to dist/ : packaging/make-deb.sh # builds, then packages both into dist/ sudo apt install ./dist/sndstation_*.deb ./dist/sndstation-vendors_*.deb The sndstation package recommends sndstation-vendors but does not need it. That package holds the vendors SETTINGS → Software packages lists, and installing it adds each one's apt source ( updates.md , \"Vendors\"). Without it the launcher works, and that page lists no vendors. On a recognized handheld the install also sets the unit up to boot into the launcher: - a realtime allowance for the audio thread; - a udev rule for the stick lights; - tty1 autologin into the session; - a sudo rule that lets SETTINGS → Network run its one network helper as root. Anywhere else it changes nothing outside /usr/share/sndstation . docs/launcher.md has the details and the switches ( SNDSTATION_BOOT=no , SNDSTATION_USER=… ). Verifying A working install on a handheld: It boots to the launcher on tty1 , with no login prompt, showing the APPS page. L1 and R1 change the page: APPS, PLUGINS, SETTINGS, ABOUT. ABOUT shows the unit. It lists the platform's version, the board, the SOC ID, the kernel, CPU, memory, SDL, the display, and the input device. The SOC ID is how the unit is told from another of its model ( unit-identity.md ). The pad works. A unit whose pad is not in the gamepad database opens Controls on a first-run card. With no pad found at all the launcher says \"No gamepad found\" and leaves to a shell after five seconds. Exit to shell ends the session and leaves a prompt. It is the last item of the power menu: press MENU (the unit's FN, Home, or MENU key), or hold SELECT and press START ( launcher.md , \"The power menu\"). Logging out logs in again, and the launcher returns. From a shell on the unit (ssh will do), after logging in again so that pam_limits has run: dpkg -s sndstation | head -3 # the package is installed, and its version groups # audio, video, input (and dialout for the stick lights) ulimit -r # 95: the audio thread may run realtime The install script says which user it set up and warns if that user is missing from audio , video or input ( launcher.md , \"Booting into it\"). A realtime refusal is said on stderr and shown on AUDIO / DIAGNOSTICS, row Thread ( rt-bench.md , \"Allowing it\"). On a unit with stick lights, sndstation --led-test lights the rings in turn ( stick-leds.md ). On a desktop, ./build/sndstation opens a 640x480 window, and ctest --test-dir build runs the tests ( CONTRIBUTING ). Nothing else is installed, and nothing boots into it. If something is wrong, see known-issues.md .","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/getting-started/quickstart.html","t":"Quickstart","s":"Getting started","h":["Quickstart","1. Run the launcher on a desktop","2. Write an app","3. Next steps"],"x":"Quickstart Draft for #169. This page is for people writing an app. To put sndstation on a unit and try it, start with Installation . 1. Run the launcher on a desktop After building : ./build/sndstation The keyboard stands in for the pad (arrows, Z/X, Return, Backspace). --fake-audio rk817 pretends to be a handheld's sound card. 2. Write an app Start from examples/hello : a complete app in one file. It opens the screen, reads the pad, plays a sound and quits back to the launcher, and its README walks through it. An app is an ordinary program. It is listed by the launcher when its package installs a manifest, /usr/share/sndstation/apps/<id>.json : { \"name\": \"Example\", \"description\": \"What it is, in a line\", \"exec\": [\"/opt/example/example\"], \"order\": 10 } When the user picks it, the launcher shuts itself down and execs it, so the app has the display, the audio device and every byte of memory to itself. When it exits, the session brings the launcher back. To use the SDK, add this tree to your build and link the library: add_subdirectory(path/to/sndstation ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/sndstation) target_link_libraries(myapp PRIVATE sndstation::sndstation) The SDK is a library, not a framework: your program owns its main loop and calls in. Its headers are included as <sndstation/…> , and <sndstation/sndstation.h> includes them all. Everything in them is in namespace sndstation ( sndstation::pad , sndstation::gfx , sndstation::AudioOut , …); qualify it, or write using namespace sndstation; in your own files. The headers are listed in the API reference . The platform's settings are shared by every app on the unit: the gamepad database, stick calibration, and the unit's audio. For audio, that is where it plays, the routes, each output's volume and maximum, and the rate and buffer size. The launcher writes them; an app reads them through the SDK and keeps its own settings in ~/.config/<YourApp> . 3. Next steps Concepts overview API reference","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/hardware/known-issues.html","t":"Known issues","s":"Hardware","h":["Known issues","For developers","A pull request gets no CI run until someone asks for one","On the units","RG40XX H and RG35XX SP: no button map of their own","RGB30 and TRIMUI Smart Pro: no routes for the built-in card","RGB30: realtime audio refused under CONFIG_RT_GROUP_SCHED","RG35XX SP: the lid file ships empty","RG35XX SP: no Bluetooth on about half of boots","H700: SDL's ALSA stream panics the kernel under PREEMPT_RT"],"x":"Known issues Each item gives the symptom, what it affects, a workaround where there is one, and the issue that tracks it. Per-unit status is in supported.md . For developers A pull request gets no CI run until someone asks for one Symptom: a pull request shows no build or test run. Why: CI runs nothing on its own: the account has a small allowance of Actions minutes for this private repository, and macOS bills at 10×. Workaround: add the label ci: build , ci: linux , ci: macos , or ci: aarch64 to the pull request, or use Run workflow under Actions ( CONTRIBUTING ). The five tests that used to fail on macOS ( test_socid , test_about , test_audioin , test_mdfourier and test_audioout ) pass there since #181. On macOS, test_mdfourier reports a 192 kHz difference rather than failing, because the reference checksums come from glibc's math library. Measured on an Apple-silicon Mac (macOS 27.3): 40 of 40 tests pass. On the units RG40XX H and RG35XX SP: no button map of their own Symptom: neither unit has a map made for it. Both share the speculative RG28xx (more?) entry in config/gamepads.json . RG35XX SP: that entry works. On two SP units the launcher matched it to the common17 map (17 buttons), measured 2026-11-16 (#132). RG40XX H: not tried yet. If it does not match, the unit may not get past the first screen. Affects: Anbernic RG40XX H (#130), Anbernic RG35XX SP (#132). Workaround: none recorded for the RG40XX H. Nobody has tried Map this controller, the wizard at SETTINGS → Controls, on it ( launcher.md , Controls). A unit with no pad found at all leaves to a shell after five seconds, where a keyboard or ssh works ( launcher.md , \"No gamepad at all\"); that is a way in, not a fix. Tracking: #130 and #132. Both are marked as needed before the developer beta. RGB30 and TRIMUI Smart Pro: no routes for the built-in card Symptom: there is no codec table for the built-in card, so sound is not routed as on the other units. On the RGB30 there is a card choice (the built-in card is taken over HDMI's card 0) but no routes. For the A133P there is nothing. Affects: Powkiddy RGB30 (#129), TRIMUI Smart Pro (#131). Workaround: none recorded. Give these two units to testers, not to someone who needs sound to build an app. A USB audio interface is picked automatically when plugged in ( launcher.md , Audio), but that has not been tried on these two units. Tracking: #129 and #131. RGB30: realtime audio refused under CONFIG_RT_GROUP_SCHED Symptom: the audio thread stays an ordinary thread, and stderr says the kernel is refusing SCHED_FIFO although ulimit -r allows it. Affects: kernels built with CONFIG_RT_GROUP_SCHED , found on the RGB30 (kernel 7.0.2). Workaround: boot with rt_group_sched=0 on the kernel command line. The install script checks for this kernel and says so, but does not change the boot line ( rt-bench.md , \"Allowing it\"). Tracking: #129 (\"reproduces or is closed\"). RG35XX SP: the lid file ships empty Symptom: config/deviceevents.json has no entry for the SP, so on a stock BSP image the lid's hallkey attribute is never read. Affects: the RG35XX SP on the stock BSP image. On the sndstation rootfs the lid is evdev gpio-keys-lid and needs no entry ( device-events.md ). Workaround: use the sndstation rootfs. To add the entry, read the attribute with the lid open and shut and report it ( reporting.md ). Tracking: #132. RG35XX SP: no Bluetooth on about half of boots Symptom: no hci0 appears, so bluetoothd never starts and paired controllers stay away. The Realtek RTL8821CS on the SP's UART never answers the link's SYNC, and the driver logs nothing more. Affects: the RG35XX SP. Measured on an SP (Debian 13, kernel 7.2): 4 boots of 8. Workaround: none needed with the package installed. Its handheld half enables sndstation-bt-h5.service . At boot it rebinds the driver when no hci device appears within six seconds, up to three times. Measured on an SP with the service installed: 6 reboots in a row, hci0 and a paired BLE-MIDI controller back every time, one of them by a rebind ( 6be33dc ). What it did is in journalctl -u sndstation-bt-h5 . The same rebind by hand is two commands run as root ( launcher.md , \"Bluetooth at boot\"). Tracking: none needed, because the boot service is the fix. The SP's slot is #132. H700: SDL's ALSA stream panics the kernel under PREEMPT_RT Symptom: a kernel panic in sun6i_dma_interrupt , racing a stop of the card's DMA. It follows SDL's ALSA stream, which starts with one frame queued and so underruns, and restarts, at a short period ( audio-device.md , §3.0). Affects: H700 units (RG40XX H, RG35XX SP, RG CubeXX) on a PREEMPT_RT kernel. Workaround: none needed by default. sndstation plays through its own stream on alsa-lib, which fills the buffer before it starts. Setting SNDSTATION_AUDIO_STREAM=sdl (or sndstation-audiotest --sdl ) brings SDL's stream back for comparison. Do not set it on an H700 under PREEMPT_RT unless you accept a panic. A program that does not go through the SDK uses SDL's stream and is exposed. Tracking: #130 (\"SDL's ALSA stream stays off this card\").","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/hardware/reporting.html","t":"Reporting a hardware result","s":"Hardware","h":["Reporting a hardware result","What to collect","How to get each","The unit id and version","--rt-bench","Sound routes (rk817 and h616 units)","Buttons and layout","Stick lights","The lid (RG35XX SP)","File the report","Where results land"],"x":"Reporting a hardware result Draft for #172. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. A private-beta slot holder measures what the unit's issue says it \"must show\" and reports it. This page says what to collect, how to get it, and where it goes. The slots and their \"must show\" lines are in supported.md . What to collect Item Where it comes from Unit the model on the case, and the slot number from supported.md SoC the CPU row on the launcher's ABOUT page, for example \"Rockchip RK3326\" Image which image the unit runs: the sndstation rootfs, ROCKNIX, or a stock BSP image ( images.md ), and its name or date. TODO(#236/#124): how an image states its own version Version the platform's version, on ABOUT Unit id the SOC ID row on ABOUT, for example RK3326-URKC08010-4FE23CAAD163F02F --rt-bench output the RESULT line and the step lines above it, or why you could not run it What the slot's \"must show\" says one line each: shown, not shown, or could not be tried Config files ~/.config/sndstation/gamepads.json once Map this controller saves it, and the rings entry --led-slots prints Copy the SOC ID exactly. It is the key under which audio.json , leds.json , and deviceevents.json file a unit ( unit-identity.md ). Two units of one model have different ids. How to get each The unit id and version Open the launcher. L1 and R1 (or left and right) change the page: APPS, PLUGINS, SETTINGS, ABOUT. ABOUT lists the platform's version, the board, the SOC ID, the kernel, the CPU, memory, uptime, SDL, the display, the input device, and the audio settings. It is read-only; B leaves ( launcher.md , About). The launcher also prints the id on a unit: line and the chip on a soc: line. Under sndstation-session , those lines go to ~/.local/state/sndstation/session.log , and the session before this one is in session.log.1 . With SNDSTATION_SESSION_LOG=- , or with the launcher run by hand, they print on the terminal. grep -E '^(unit|soc):' ~/.local/state/sndstation/session.log --rt-bench The bench belongs to the SDK, and an app runs it: the app opens its AudioOut , calls rtbench::start() , and parses the --rt-* flags ( rt-bench.md , \"Running it\"). The example app does: hello --rt-bench (#194, examples/hello ). The .deb does not install hello , so build it or copy it to the unit. The launcher does not take the flag. hello --rt-bench --rt-noise 4 Run it on the unit itself, not on a desktop, and report the whole output: the RESULT line carries the unit, kernel, driver, PCM, rate, frames, and buffer. Several runs that agree are a result; one is an anecdote. If the audio thread is refused realtime, the bench says so on stderr, with the cause ( rt-bench.md , \"Allowing it\"); include that. TODO(#236/#125): which app testers run it from. Sound routes (rk817 and h616 units) Open the Audio page and use an output's Set up by ear . Answers are kept when the DONE screen says SAVE, into ~/.config/sndstation/audio.json under the unit's id ( launcher.md , Audio). Report what each route did, and whether it survived a headphone plug. Buttons and layout SETTINGS → Controls has Test buttons, which shows the map end to end with the raw SDL source, and Map this controller, the wizard. The wizard saves into ~/.config/sndstation/gamepads.json ( launcher.md , Controls). Send that file. For a layout, the rules for placing controls are in gamepad-layout.md . Stick lights sndstation --led-slots # measure: every wire slot, one at a time sndstation --led-test # check: each logical LED in turn --led-slots asks for a clock position per slot and prints the rings entry to paste. --led-test walks both rings clockwise: N, NE, E, and so on ( stick-leds.md , \"Checking a unit\"). The user has to be in dialout . The lid (RG35XX SP) On the sndstation rootfs, close the lid, open it again, and say whether the unit reacts as the lid policy says. On a stock BSP image, read the hallkey attribute under /sys/class/power_supply (the node is axp2202-battery ) with the lid open and then shut, and report both values ( device-events.md , \"hallkey\"). File the report Open a \"Hardware report\" issue: the template is .github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/hardware-report.yml . Fill in what is asked, and attach the output rather than describing it. The unit's own issue (#126 to #133) ticks its gap boxes as results land: TODO(#236/#125) whether the reporter or a maintainer links the report there. Where results land #125 sets where each result ends up, so that each \"must show\" changes the file it concerns: Result File --rt-bench RESULT rt-bench.md unit id, same-lot collisions unit-identity.md button maps, layouts config/gamepads.json stick-light rings stick-leds.md , config/leds.json the lid's hallkey config/deviceevents.json Who makes that change, and how a report becomes a commit, is TODO(#236/#125). How invitees send changes at all is TODO(#236/#122).","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/hardware/supported.html","t":"Supported hardware","s":"Hardware","h":["Supported hardware","Shorthand","The eight slots","What every slot has to show","Unit by unit","1. Anbernic RG351V (RK3326), #126","2. R36S Blue and R40XX (RK3326), #127","3. XU10 (RK3326), #128","4. Powkiddy RGB30 (RK3566), #129","5. Anbernic RG40XX H (H700), #130","6. TRIMUI Smart Pro (A133P), #131","7. Anbernic RG35XX SP (H700), #132","8. Raspberry Pi 4, OS Lite (BCM2711), #133","Probably supported","H700 units","Not in the beta","Reporting a result"],"x":"Supported hardware Draft for #170. Placeholders marked TODO(#236/#…) wait on a decision. #236 tracks each one, and the second number is the decision's closed issue. The private beta has eight unit slots (#116). Each slot is a unit that has to show something specific on real hardware before sndstation calls it supported. None has done that yet. Until a slot's issue closes, read every unit below as private beta, not yet verified : the platform is written for it, parts of it are measured, and the rest is a gap listed here. Two units have no sound routes for the built-in card: the Powkiddy RGB30 (slot 4) and the TRIMUI Smart Pro (slot 6). Give each of them to a tester, not to an app author who needs sound to build an app. The .deb sets a unit up as an sndstation system on six kinds of chip: RK3326, RK3566, H700, A133P, MT8167, and a Raspberry Pi with no desktop ( packaging/make-deb.sh , handheld() ). On any other machine it installs but changes nothing outside its own directory. SNDSTATION_SYSTEM=yes or no overrides the check. The eight slots are the units checked on real hardware. MT8167, the PlayStation Classic, is on the install list but has no slot (#185). Whether the public beta covers every unit on those chips, or only the eight once they pass, is not decided yet (#116). The page says \"the beta\" for the eight. Shorthand Term Meaning F3, F0 Device families from the survey in #100 §1. F3 is a gamepad handheld, with a d-pad and sticks. F0 is a headless board, with no panel. OTP One-time-programmable memory on the SoC, written at manufacture. An RK3326 unit's SOC ID is read from it ( unit-identity.md ). pad::layout() The SDK call that says where each control is on the body, in millimeters. A pad with no layout prints the standard names ( gamepad-layout.md ). pixels/1 The display API every host offers a plugin: the plugin draws XRGB8888 pixels into the host's buffer ( embedded-hardware.md ). gles/1 , vulkan/1 Display APIs for a GPU, which #100 §1 proposes after pixels/1 . The extension header does not define them yet. The eight slots # Unit SoC Family Status Issue 1 Anbernic RG351V RK3326 F3 gamepad handheld private beta, not yet verified #126 2 R36S Blue and R40XX RK3326 F3 gamepad handheld private beta, not yet verified #127 3 XU10 RK3326 F3 gamepad handheld private beta, not yet verified #128 4 Powkiddy RGB30 RK3566 F3 gamepad handheld private beta, not yet verified #129 5 Anbernic RG40XX H (also the RG CubeXX) H700 F3 gamepad handheld private beta, not yet verified; LED rings verified #130 6 TRIMUI Smart Pro (also the Brick) A133P F3 gamepad handheld private beta, not yet verified #131 7 Anbernic RG35XX SP H700 F3 gamepad handheld private beta, not yet verified #132 8 Raspberry Pi 4, OS Lite BCM2711 F0 headless board private beta, not yet verified #133 \"Also\" means the same slot is expected to speak for a sibling unit. It does not mean the sibling has been tried. Which image each unit runs (the sndstation rootfs, ROCKNIX, or the stock firmware) is TODO(#236/#124). images.md has what is known. What every slot has to show Each unit's \"must show\" is its exit criterion. It is what a holder measures and reports ( reporting.md ). A unit with an open gap box has not yet shown it. Unit by unit 1. Anbernic RG351V (RK3326), #126 Must show: --rt-bench reports a RESULT; the SOC ID row reads from OTP; the rk817 routes are learned by ear and survive a headphone plug; 48 kHz / 256 frames runs clean on hw , where plug underran. Works, per the docs: the realtime audio thread. On this unit at 48 kHz / 512 frames with four competing threads, SCHED_FIFO 70 measured 90% headroom ( rt-bench.md ). Opening the card as hw:0,0 was clean at 48 kHz / 256 frames, where alsa-lib's plug over MMAP underran ( audio-device.md , §3.3). A gamepad map exists ( RG351V (ArkOS) , with inverted analog axes in config/gamepads.json ). Open gap: no body layout ( pad::layout() ), so the pad prints the standard names. The slot's report is also owed. Known not to work: nothing recorded. 2. R36S Blue and R40XX (RK3326), #127 One slot, two boxes. Must show: the two units get different SOC IDs and separate audio.json entries; the R36S body's controls and layout are right. Works, per the docs: the two units already have different ids on the ABOUT page, read from OTP though their device trees are alike: RK3326-URKC08010-A599D7394C5B58B6 (R40XX) and RK3326-URKC08010-4FE23CAAD163F02F (R36S Blue) ( unit-identity.md ). A gamepad map for the R36S and R36S Plus exists in config/gamepads.json . Open gaps: no body layout; no rt-bench result; the slot's report. Known not to work: nothing recorded. 3. XU10 (RK3326), #128 Must show: pad::layout() matches the body within the stated 2 mm; rear shoulders and the edge Menu are placed correctly (the face gap of #100 §1, on real hardware). Works, per the docs: a gamepad map and a layout, measured from photos of every side and scaled to the unit's 130 mm height. Its shoulders are on the back and its Menu is the G on the right edge ( gamepad-layout.md , config/gamepads.json ). Open gaps: no rt-bench result; the slot's report. Known not to work: nothing recorded. 4. Powkiddy RGB30 (RK3566), #129 Must show: the built-in card is picked over HDMI's card 0; the RK3566 rt-bench ( CONFIG_RT_GROUP_SCHED ) finding reproduces or is closed. Works, per the docs: the card choice. Card 0 is HDMI and the speaker codec is card 1, so sndstation takes the first card that is not a display output ( audio-device.md , §3.3). A bench run on kernel 7.0.2, booted with rt_group_sched=0 , was clean through 90% with four competing threads and failed at 95% ( rt-bench.md ). A gamepad map exists. Open gaps: no body layout; no routes for the built-in card (there is a card choice but no codec table); the slot's report. Known not to work: realtime scheduling is refused for a login session on a kernel with CONFIG_RT_GROUP_SCHED unless it is booted with rt_group_sched=0 ( rt-bench.md , \"Allowing it\"). Its /proc/asound has no per-stream status, so the bench reports access=unknown buffer=unknown . See known-issues.md . 5. Anbernic RG40XX H (H700), #130 Also stands for the RG CubeXX. Must show: the rings light in the shipped slot order without root; the h616 route table and the speaker amp switch play where test_h616_route says; SDL's ALSA stream stays off this card (the sun6i_dma_interrupt panic). Works, per the docs: the stick-light rings are verified . On an RG40XX H under a ROCKNIX kernel, slots 0-7 are the right ring and 8-15 the left. Each slot took its own color, checked by eye ( stick-leds.md ). The h616 codec reports the jack and leaves the speaker on, so its speaker switch is its whole routing; this is tested headless ( --fake-audio h616 , tests/test_h616_route.cpp ), not on the unit ( launcher.md , Audio). Open gaps: a button map of its own; a body layout; the slot's report. The button map has to land before the developer beta starts. Today there is only the speculative RG28xx (more?) entry, so the unit cannot get past the first screen. Known not to work: the button map above. SDL's ALSA stream under PREEMPT_RT panics the kernel; sndstation uses its own stream, so this only shows if SNDSTATION_AUDIO_STREAM=sdl is set. See known-issues.md . 6. TRIMUI Smart Pro (A133P), #131 Also stands for the Brick. Must show: the install recognizes the unit and sets it up to boot into the launcher; the layout's shoulders (on top) are placed; a plugin drawing through pixels/1 alone is usable. Works, per the docs: the install's handheld allowlist includes the sun50i-a100 device tree, and this is the only unit that uses it ( packaging/make-deb.sh ). Two gamepad maps exist (one for Knulli, one for the unit's own image) and a layout, taken from the maker's front-on photo scaled to 80 mm, accurate to about 2-3 mm. The photo does not show the top edge, so the shoulders are printed, not placed ( gamepad-layout.md , config/gamepads.json ). Open gaps: no routes for the built-in card (nothing for the A133P yet); no rt-bench result; the slot's report. Known not to work: sound from the built-in card, until routes exist. See known-issues.md . 7. Anbernic RG35XX SP (H700), #132 The only clamshell. Must show: on the sndstation rootfs, the lid closes and opens through evdev gpio-keys-lid , and the lid policy acts on it. On the stock BSP image, the hallkey attribute is read open and shut, and becomes the first entry in config/deviceevents.json . Works, per the docs: on the sndstation rootfs (a mainline kernel) the lid is evdev gpio-keys-lid , value 1 for closed, and the lid policy ( suspend by default) is applied to logind at boot ( device-events.md ). The Realtek Bluetooth chip fails to answer on about half of boots; a boot service rebinds it ( launcher.md , \"Bluetooth at boot\"). Open gaps: a button map of its own (needed before the developer beta, as for slot 5); a body layout; no rt-bench result; a lid entry; the slot's report. config/deviceevents.json ships empty, so the beta is the lid's measurement. Known not to work: the button map above. The lid is not read through hallkey on any unit, because no polarity has been measured. See known-issues.md . Two images: the sndstation rootfs, where the lid is evdev, and a stock BSP image, where hallkey can be read: TODO(#236/#124). 8. Raspberry Pi 4, OS Lite (BCM2711), #133 The headless case, with no pad and no backlight. Must show: the install takes the no-desktop branch; with no pad and no backlight, nothing claims a panel or geometry; vulkan/1 can be offered beside pixels/1 and gles/1 . Works, per the docs: the install treats a Pi, like any board with a device tree, as a handheld only when no desktop is installed ( packaging/make-deb.sh ). The unit id comes from the firmware's serial in the device tree, for example BCM2711-100000001A2B3C4D ( unit-identity.md ). Open gaps: no rt-bench result; the slot's report. Known not to work: nothing recorded. Probably supported The eight slots are the units someone has agreed to verify. They are not the limit of what runs. sndstation gates on the SoC , not the model, and on whether a desktop is installed: packaging/make-deb.sh sets a machine up as a unit when the device tree's compatible matches one of compatible Slot that covers it rockchip,rk3326 1, 2, 3 rockchip,rk3566 4 allwinner,sun50i-h700 5, 7 allwinner,sun50i-a100 6 (the A133P's device tree calls itself sun50i-a100) mediatek,mt8167 none any other, with no desktop installed 8 (any Pi, not only the 4); an SBC or a TV box on Armbian's Minimal or Server images, with no slot behind it An S905X TV box on Armbian ( amlogic,meson-gxl , HDMI, a USB gamepad) installs this way; it has no slot and has not been verified. So a unit this page never names probably works, if it is on one of those SoCs. RK3326 is the broadest case: src/rk3326_otp.h comes from reading \"18+ retail RK3326 handhelds from multiple manufacturers (Anbernic, Powkiddy, ...)\", and the launcher's identity, audio, and realtime paths key off the SoC rather than the model. mediatek,mt8167 installs with no slot behind it at all. What \"probably\" is doing in that sentence is the gamepad . The database in config/gamepads.json holds four layouts— ps_classic , trimui_smart_pro , xbox , xu10 . A unit whose pad matches none of them gets the fallback: it runs, and some buttons are in the wrong place. That is the expected failure for an unnamed unit, and it is a config fix, not a port. H700 units These are the H700 units with a device tree, 19 in all. Every one of them matches allwinner,sun50i-h700 , so the .deb sets each up as an sndstation system. Only the units in slots 5 and 7 have a holder; the rest are not verified. Unit Device tree Slot Anbernic RG28XX sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg28xx.dtb none Anbernic RG34XX sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg34xx.dtb none Anbernic RG34XX SP sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg34xx-sp.dtb none Anbernic RG34XX SP, v2 panel sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg34xx-sp-v2-panel.dtb none Anbernic RG35XX 2024 sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-2024.dtb none Anbernic RG35XX 2024, rev6 panel sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-2024-rev6-panel.dtb none Anbernic RG35XX H sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-h.dtb none Anbernic RG35XX H, rev6 panel sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-h-rev6-panel.dtb none Anbernic RG35XX Plus sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-plus.dtb none Anbernic RG35XX Plus, rev6 panel sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-plus-rev6-panel.dtb none Anbernic RG35XX Pro sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-pro.dtb none Anbernic RG35XX SP sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-sp.dtb 7 Anbernic RG35XX SP, v2 panel sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg35xx-sp-v2-panel.dtb 7 Anbernic RG40XX H sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg40xx-h.dtb 5 Anbernic RG40XX H, v2 panel sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg40xx-h-v2-panel.dtb 5 Anbernic RG40XX V sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg40xx-v.dtb none Anbernic RG40XX V, v2 panel sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg40xx-v-v2-panel.dtb none Anbernic RG CubeXX sun50i-h700-anbernic-rgcubexx.dtb 5, as a sibling Anbernic RG SP sun50i-h700-anbernic-rg-sp.dtb none A panel variant is the same unit with a different screen. Which panel a slot's holder has is part of the report ( reporting.md ). For what the beta holds back rather than leaves untested, see the next section. Those are decisions, not gaps. If you run an unnamed unit, reporting.md says what to send. A result on a unit with no slot is still worth having. Not in the beta Each of these is decided in its own issue. Until it is, treat the hardware as out. Hardware Issue F4 touch workstation #134 GPU classes V- and X #135 Adreno and RK3568 #136 The XF40H stick lights, which ship disabled as unverified #137 Units with an entry in the tree but no slot, such as the RG40XX V and the XF40H (both in config/leds.json ), are not verified. Reporting a result See reporting.md . Known problems are in known-issues.md .","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/launcher.html","t":"The sndstation launcher and session","s":"Subsystems","h":["The sndstation launcher and session","How an app is started","Listing an app: the manifest","The session","Booting into it","Bluetooth at boot","Controls","No gamepad at all","First run","Audio","First run","MIDI","MIDI monitor","About","NOIZG8","Screensaver","Network: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, USB networking and SSH","Bluetooth","USB networking","SSH: off unless turned on here","How it works","Storage: SD cards, USB drives and files","How it works","Software packages","Display","Brightness","Skin","The on-screen keyboard","The file browser","Plugins","Playing a plugin","The power menu: sleep, restart, shut down, exit to shell","Options","Keys","Not yet"],"x":"The sndstation launcher and session sndstation is the first thing on screen once the OS is up. It lists the installed apps and starts the one you pick. sndstation-session runs it in a loop: when the app exits, the launcher comes back. \"Exit to shell\" ends the loop. Both ship in the sndstation package and are built from sndstation/launcher/ and sndstation/session/ on the public SDK alone. How an app is started The launcher replaces itself with the app. It shuts SDL down (display, joystick, audio) and then exec s the app's program. So: the app gets all of the memory and every device; an app that crashes leaves no launcher half-alive behind it; the session loop, not the launcher, is what brings the list back. The app runs with SNDSTATION_SESSION=1 in its environment. An app may use it to say \"back\" rather than \"quit\". Which platform is installed is in a file, not the environment: /usr/share/sndstation/version holds its version on one line ( 0.17.1 ). The package ships it, so any program can read it, started by the launcher or not, and it is never out of date after an upgrade. Listing an app: the manifest One file per app, <id>.json , in any of these directories (later ones override earlier ones with the same id): directory who puts it there /usr/share/sndstation/apps the app's package ~/.local/share/sndstation/apps you: your own entries, or overrides $SNDSTATION_APPS (colon-separated), --apps DIR development { \"name\": \"Example\", // required \"description\": \"What it is, in a line\", \"exec\": [\"/opt/example/example\"], // required: argv, no shell \"order\": 10, // optional, low first \"skin\": \"system\" // optional: wear the unit's skin } skin : \"system\" starts the app in the skin chosen in Display (below), a skin's id in that one. The launcher hands it over as SNDSTATION_SKIN ; the app's own style goes over it. Without it, an app has its own look ( sdk-style.md ). exec runs as given: no shell, and an absolute path is not looked up on $PATH . A program that is not there (or not executable) is still listed, grayed out with the reason. A broken install says so instead of vanishing. Only apps with a manifest are listed. A manifest without name or exec is skipped, with a line on stderr. The list is read when the launcher starts , and the launcher is a new process after every app, so coming back from one always reads it afresh. For something installed or removed while the launcher sat on the list (a port added over SSH, say), X (North) rescans. Nothing reads it on a timer: the unit is an audio device, and an idle launcher does nothing but keep its sound stream, which the screensaver lets go of. Starting an app reads the list once more first, so one removed a moment ago is not started, and neither is whatever the cursor lands on in its place. An app's package installs its manifest into /usr/share/sndstation/apps . The session sndstation-session loops on the launcher. What each run returns says how it ended: exit means the session 87 \"Exit to shell\" ends; the shell that ran it carries on anything else the app, or the launcher, ended starts the launcher again Three failing runs in a row, each under five seconds, also end the session. That catches a launcher that cannot start (no display, a broken install), which would otherwise flicker forever. The session says so and leaves you a shell. Arguments are passed to every launcher run. SNDSTATION_LAUNCHER overrides which program it runs. Booting into it On a handheld, the sndstation package sets this up when it installs , as part of the handheld half it already links into /etc (the realtime allowance, the LED rule). The unit's own screen then boots into the launcher: /etc/profile.d/sndstation-session.sh is linked to sndstation/session/profile.sh . A login on tty1 runs sndstation-session , once. SSH, a serial console or any other terminal is an ordinary shell. Because it is a real login, pam_limits applies, so the audio thread gets its realtime allowance. /etc/systemd/system/getty@tty1.service.d/sndstation-autologin.conf is written to log tty1 in automatically. It names a user, so it is written rather than shipped, and it carries a marker line so the package only ever removes its own. /etc/systemd/system/sndstation-lid.service applies the lid policy, system.json 's \"system\": {\"lid\"} , to logind at boot. It writes /etc/systemd/logind.conf.d/sndstation-lid.conf from the tty1 user's setting, so a change takes effect from the next boot. See device-events.md . \"Exit to shell\" ends the session, and that login carries on to a normal prompt. Logging out of the prompt logs in again, and the launcher comes back. Whose login it is: SNDSTATION_USER if given at install time, else whoever ran sudo , else the first ordinary account (uid 1000 and up, with a shell). The script says which, and says if that user is missing from audio , video or input . Nothing changes until the next boot: the install never restarts tty1 under whoever is using it. Nobody's ~/.profile is edited. at install time SNDSTATION_BOOT=no leave tty1 alone, and remove what an earlier install set up. Remembered ( /etc/sndstation/boot ) through upgrades, until… SNDSTATION_BOOT=yes …this turns it back on SNDSTATION_USER=<name> log this user in SNDSTATION_SYSTEM=no no handheld half at all (this included) An autologin drop-in of the same name that the package did not write is left alone, and the script says so. Without systemd, the profile link is still made; logging the user in on tty1 is then up to the image. Removing the package removes both. Purging it also forgets the yes/no. Bluetooth at boot The RG35XX SP and the other H700 units with a Realtek RTL8821CS have their Bluetooth on a UART, spoken to over the Three-wire (H5) protocol. On about half of all boots the chip never answers the link's SYNC. The driver binds and logs nothing more; no hci0 appears, so bluetoothd never starts, and paired controllers (a BLE-MIDI keyboard, say) stay away until a lucky boot. Measured on an SP (Debian 13, kernel 7.2): 4 boots of 8 came up this way. Rebinding the driver powers the chip down and up through its enable GPIO, and it then answers at once. The handheld half does that for you: /etc/systemd/system/sndstation-bt-h5.service is linked to sndstation/system/sndstation-bt-h5.service and enabled. At boot it runs /usr/share/sndstation/system/sndstation-bt-h5 , which waits for each device the hci_uart_h5 driver has bound and, if no hci device appears within six seconds, rebinds it, up to three times. When hci0 registers, udev starts bluetoothd as usual. A unit without an H5 controller finds none and the service exits. What it did is in journalctl -u sndstation-bt-h5 . By hand, as root, the same thing is: echo serial0-0 > /sys/bus/serial/drivers/hci_uart_h5/unbind echo serial0-0 > /sys/bus/serial/drivers/hci_uart_h5/bind On a desktop none of this happens. To try the session there, run sndstation-session by hand. Controls SETTINGS → Controls sets up the unit's gamepad for every app ( launcher/controls.cpp ): Test buttons press anything: its pill lights. The map, end to end, with the raw SDL source and every axis live. START three times leaves. Map this controller the wizard. Each control is haloed in turn; press its button twice to confirm, hold any button 1.5 s to skip it (or keep what it was), and a hat d-pad is taken whole. It edits the pad's existing entry rather than relearning it, and saves into ~/.config/sndstation/gamepads.json , live at once. Calibrate sticks ranges (swirl both sticks), swapping the two sticks (units of one model wire them either way round), and per-stick inversion and X/Y swap, into calibration.json The wizard reads raw button numbers, not the pad's logical bits: an unknown pad runs on the generic fallback map, whose bits are guesses. One pad is in use at a time: the first one SDL reports. Another pad plugged in alongside is ignored entirely, buttons, hat and sticks, until the one in use is unplugged; then the next one still connected takes over, on its own map and calibration. (Its presses used to be read through the first pad's map.) Key A or START pause the log, or resume it. Lights and counts carry on while it is paused X clear the log and the counts Y hide or show timing clock and active sensing. Hidden by default UP / DOWN, L1 / R1 scroll back a line or ten (pauses), and forward again B back to the Controls menu On a desktop's keyboard: A is Z or A, START is Return, X is Space or C, Y is V, L1 and R1 are 1 and 4, and B is X or D. No gamepad at all Before anything else, after the boot logo (the session's first launcher) or on a handheld's screen: with no gamepad half a second in, the launcher says \"No gamepad found\" and counts five seconds down, then leaves to the shell (exit 87, as Exit to shell), where a keyboard or ssh can find out why: a driver that failed, a device tree without the pad. A pad that arrives meanwhile, or any key on a keyboard, ends it and the launcher carries on, the pad's own first run next. A launcher started by hand on a desktop has the keyboard, and never shows it. First run When the unit's pad is not in the gamepad database , Controls opens by itself before the list, on a card: press any button twice to map it (the wizard, then the tester); hold any button for three seconds to skip it. It still runs on the fallback map, and it is not asked about again ( ~/.config/sndstation/setup.json , pads_skipped ). Controls maps it any time later. A pad already in the database is never asked about. Audio SETTINGS → Audio sets up the unit's sound for every app ( launcher/audiosetup.cpp ). Everything is set and tested here once, and every app follows it: the page edits what the unit keeps ( audio.json , version 3, per unit and per endpoint) and its own sound output applies it, by the same SDK functions every app opens its sound through. audio-page.md has the model and why. Device Automatic (a USB interface when plugged in; else the headphones when in and the unit can tell; else the speaker; HDMI only when chosen), or one output: speaker, headphones, speaker + headphones where the board can, a USB interface, HDMI. Something always plays: a chosen output that goes away falls back to Automatic Sample rate, Buffer size the playing card's, live, offering only what the device answered when asked; kept per card. The buffer size is a slider, with its length in ms User level the playing output's system volume, a gain on the samples in software, in dB below its system levels, shared by every program ( audio-levels.md ) System levels the chip's controls; its playback levels are the playing output's ceiling, kept as they move; nothing else sets it. PLAYBACK and ROUTING shown; switches, and capture and the rest, each behind a row A opens Test sound pings peaking at full scale, at the volume (as loud as an app can play at this volume), or a steady 440 Hz at -12 dBFS OUTPUTS one row per output this unit has; A opens it: its user level, Play on it, and for the built-in card Set up by ear and Corrections (plug detection, swap, speaker switch) TOOLS USB interface (while one is plugged in: audio-page.md ), Diagnostics, MDFourier ( audio-device.md §2.4 ) System levels with the test sound on plays pings at full scale (0 dBFS, past the user level; at it on headphones), over the card's playback levels: raise them until it distorts, rattles or is too loud, and back off. Each move is kept as that output's maximum, its 0 dB. Every change is kept as it is made , into ~/.config/sndstation/audio.json , and \"✓ Saved\" shows in the title bar for a moment. The by-ear set-up is the exception: its answers are kept when its DONE screen says SAVE, and a run abandoned part way leaves the unit as it was. A unit nobody has set up has nothing routed: only Automatic, Built in and any other card are offered. The page plays into the launcher's stream ( launcher/audio.h ), which the launcher opens when it starts and keeps: the page's test sounds join it when the page opens and fade out when it closes, and the page is the one place that reopens it (a card, a rate, a buffer size). The stream is let go of for the hand-over to an app, the screensaver and sleep ( audio-stack.md ). The launcher also applies the unit's kept levels and its output's route and maximum each time it starts ( audiodev::applyStartupMixer ), for programs that do not open their sound through the SDK. Vol+ and Vol- step the system volume on every launcher page but CONTROLS, which tests the keys: the volume of the output a program would play on now, 1.5 dB a press, saved at once so the next program starts there. An overlay says which output and how loud. AUDIO and the game step their own stream's, the same volume. The detail — the survey behind the wizard, the profile schema, why the test signal holds no state: audio-device.md . First run After the gamepad's first run, a handheld whose unit is not set up gets a card before the list: A sets it up by ear (the wizard, then the page), B skips it. A skip is remembered for the unit ( setup.json , audio_skipped ); Audio sets it up any time later. A handheld is a device tree and a card with a speaker/headphone route to choose — a desktop is never asked, and its system decides where sound goes. A card with no mux but a switch named for the speaker counts too, as a two-item route, on and off, when the socket gives a signal to follow: the H616 codec in the RG40XX H reports the jack and leaves the speaker on, so that switch is its whole routing ( --fake-audio h616 ). MIDI Under Settings, MIDI chooses which MIDI ports every app uses ( launcher/midisettings.cpp ): MIDI in all ports, or none; a list of named ports set in system.json is kept as a third choice MIDI out none, or all ports MIDI controllers › pairs a BLE-MIDI keyboard or pad over Bluetooth; right or A opens Network's MIDI controllers list , B comes back here MIDI monitor › every MIDI port, and what arrives on each input; right or A opens it, B comes back (below) Up and down choose a row, left and right change it. Each change is saved at once into ~/.config/sndstation/system.json , only the key it changes, and apps read it when they next start. MIDI monitor MIDI monitor , on the MIDI page, shows the unit's MIDI devices and everything they send ( sndstation/launcher/midimon.cpp , on midi.h ): Ports: every port midi::ports() lists, inputs and outputs, with in/out, MIDI 1.0 or 2.0, and the tier the backend is on. The list is read again when a device comes or goes: on Sink::portsChanged , or every two seconds while no input is open yet, because the backend tells only a sink that already has a port open. Activity: a light for each input. It flashes on anything that arrives, whether or not the log shows it. The log: newest at the bottom. Each line has the port, the channel, the message ( Note On C4 vel 100 , CC 20 = 73 , Pitch bend +2048 , Sysex 6 bytes ) and its bytes in hex, from UmpToBytes . MIDI 2.0 arrives scaled to MIDI 1.0. A sysex longer than 512 bytes is logged by its size. The last 256 lines are kept. Counts: messages received, hidden by the filter, and dropped . Each input's sink only copies the packet into a lock-free ring of 1024 packets, which the screen empties every frame. A burst bigger than that between two frames is dropped and counted, never waited on. Every input is opened with midi::open() when the screen opens, and closed when it is left. The launcher's own MIDI ( launcher/launchermidi.h ), which listens to the unit's inputs from its start, is paused meanwhile, and the ports are the monitor's: one port has one sink. It listens again when the monitor closes. The backend is the launcher's, and stopped only when the launcher hands over to an app, sleeps or shows the screensaver: it holds no ports, and no sequencer client, while an app runs. On the sequencer tiers other programs keep their ports; on the raw MIDI tier a device has one owner, the launcher while it runs. About About says what the unit is and what it runs: the platform's version, the board and which physical unit it is (the SOC ID that audio.json is keyed on), the kernel, CPU, memory, uptime, SDL, the display, the input device, and the audio settings every app starts from. It is the same list an app's own ABOUT page can show, from the SDK's sysfacts::about() . Read-only; B leaves. NOIZG8 START pressed three times on About opens a game. sndy flies out over a city at night to meet the noise coming in, and everything in it is music: his shots play a line on the chord on the sixteenths, kills strike the chord in time and let go of notes that fly back to him and climb the scale as they are caught, the noise comes in on the bar and fires on the beat, and the song fills out as the kills come without a hit. A run is five takes, each its own song with its own tempo, groove, chords and sounds (the shots, the kills and the notes caught too), its own part of town, noise and boss, each faster and harder than the last: take the boss 1 a cold night, slow and dark; things up out of the deep THE HUM, an anglerfish 2 the city in neon; static DISTORTION, a one-eyed squid 3 sundown; things that fly FLUTTER, a moth 4 a green night; blocks BITCRUSHER, a skull of them 5 everything on fire; steel BRICKWALL, a wall that walks A take's song plays its parts with its boss last; the boss down, the next take begins. The fifth down is ALL CLEAR. The best score is kept in game.json . The bigger noise now and then, a gold formation wiped out, the outro, and each boss at a third and two thirds of its life let go of upgrades, which one at random, each an effect: upgrade CHORUS three shots in a fan (the band plays three notes) ECHO two fainter sndys on your path, firing with you (your shots' note comes back a dotted eighth later, and again) OVERDRIVE shots twice as hard (and louder, with grit) PHASER shots go through the noise, five before they stop AUTOTUNE shots bend toward the nearest noise ahead of them LIMITER a shield: it takes a hit for you, and goes You have one at a time: a new one takes the place of the one you have, and the same one again fills its meter (the FX meter, bottom right, with its name), which runs dry over twelve bars. A hit, or the meter run dry, takes it: DRY. control D-pad, left stick fly A or Y, held fire B or X the drop, when its meter is full: everything on screen takes a hit, their shots turn to notes L1 or R1, held fly slow, and show the one pixel that can be hit Start pause; B there goes back to the launcher Volume keys the sound card's own playback levels, for the card the game plays to: a step a press, repeating while held, each step moving the first of Master, PCM, Line Out, Headphone, Speaker and DAC with room to go that way; the band's own level where the card has none It is drawn at half the panel's pixels or a third ( launcher/gameart.* ): every sprite, the city and the pixel fonts in code, the logo cut from JetBrains Mono. The band is a synth of its own ( launcher/gamesound.* ) that keeps the time the game plays to; it plays into the launcher's stream from the game's start to its close, and the stream is closed before an app starts, so the app finds the card free. Screensaver After a minute with no button pressed on the launcher's pages, the game's city goes by with no one in it ( launcher/saver.* ), drawing thirty frames a second. Any button brings the launcher back and does nothing else. It does not start over Controls, Audio, MIDI, Network or the game, nor while a message box is up, and a message box arriving puts it away. While it is up the launcher's sound stream is let go of, faded out and closed, so nothing holds the card; it is opened again, faded in, when the screensaver ends. Sleep does the same. Network: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, USB networking and SSH Network , a row on the SETTINGS page after MIDI, puts the unit on Wi-Fi, pairs Bluetooth devices, lets a computer reach it over a USB cable, and lets it be logged in to over SSH, without taking the card out to edit files on it. A opens it; B goes back a screen, and from its menu back to SETTINGS. The row's note says how things stand: the network and address it is on, a connected Bluetooth device, USB networking's address, or, while SSH is on, SSH is ON and the command to connect with, in the warning color. Network opens on a menu of five, each with how it stands under its name, each opening its own page: page Interfaces every network interface but lo , built in or a USB adapter ( wlx0ccf89f495ea , enx606d3cbcf661 ), Wi-Fi or wired: its state and IPv4 address. To read; NetworkManager brings a wired one up by itself Wi-Fi connected or not, the network, its IPv4 address. Choose a network, forget the one joined, Wi-Fi off or on · networks what a scan finds, strongest first: name, WPA2 / WPA3 / open, saved, connected, signal. A joins, X scans again, Y forgets a saved one, B goes back · passphrase the on-screen keyboard , masked: D-pad moves, A types, B deletes (B on an empty field goes back), X shift, Y space, SELECT shows or hides it, START joins. It is checked as you type: 8 to 63 characters, or 64 hex digits Bluetooth on or off, and the paired devices, connected or not. Devices (while it is on), Bluetooth off or on · devices what a scan of a few seconds finds, paired ones too: name, address, paired, connected. A connects (pairing first) or disconnects, X scans again, Y forgets a paired one (after a card), B goes back · MIDI controllers the same list from an LE scan for BLE-MIDI gear alone, with the same buttons. Settings → MIDI → MIDI controllers opens this very screen, since that is where someone looking for a keyboard looks first; from there B goes back to the MIDI page, and where Bluetooth is off or absent the Bluetooth page opens instead and says so USB networking the mode and the unit's address on it, and how to log in over it. Connected to my PC, Connected to a hub, As a host (each where the port can do it), off while one is on, and Remember network settings SSH on or off, ssh sst@192.168.1.23 while it is on, and who could then log in. Make host keys, turn SSH on or off Nothing is hidden for want of hardware. Every page is on every unit, and one with nothing to drive says No … device present and, where the helper can tell, why, in this order: a hardware switch (rfkill), firmware the kernel failed to load (named, from the kernel log), hardware on the SDIO bus (or a USB Bluetooth interface) with no driver bound, or no hardware at all. On these kernels a radio with no driver or no firmware is more common than no radio. A unit with no built-in Wi-Fi joins through a USB Wi-Fi adapter the same way: NetworkManager drives either. Joining takes up to 45 seconds; a card says so, and the buttons wait. A saved network or an open one is joined without asking. A passphrase the network turns down is not kept: the keyboard comes back with it still typed, to correct. WEP and 802.1X (eduroam, company networks) are listed but not joined. Hidden networks (no name) are not listed, but the list says how many there are. Power saving (on the Wi-Fi page) turns the radio's power saving off or on ( iw , and NetworkManager's wifi.powersave for later connections), and with it the driver's deep power save where it has a switch (rtw88's disable_lps_deep , behind the \"firmware failed to leave lps state\" errors on an RG35XX SP). Off is kept across restarts in the helper's /etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/sndstation-wifi-powersave.conf , /etc/modprobe.d/sndstation-rtw88.conf (where rtw88 is a module) and /etc/tmpfiles.d/sndstation-rtw88.conf (where it is built in, as on the SP's kernel, so modprobe.d never applies); on removes them. A driver without the switch is said so on the row. Bluetooth Pairing is done with no PIN (BlueZ's NoInputNoOutput agent): a pad cannot type one back, and the devices a music unit pairs with (headphones, MIDI keyboards and controllers) do not ask for one. A paired device is trusted, so it reconnects on its own. A device that wants a PIN, or one not in pairing mode, fails to pair and the page says so. USB networking A network over a USB cable, with no Wi-Fi, in one of three modes. Picking one turns off whichever was on first. Connected to my PC. The unit's USB port becomes a USB Ethernet device (a configfs gadget with one CDC NCM function, which Windows 10 and later, macOS and Linux drive with no extra driver; CDC ECM, which Windows cannot, on a kernel without NCM), and the unit takes 10.55.0.1/24 on it. With dnsmasq in the image, NetworkManager hands the computer an address too; without it, give the computer 10.55.0.2/24 by hand. Then ssh sst@10.55.0.1 reaches the unit, once SSH is on. The usual choice, so it is listed first. Connected to a hub. The same USB Ethernet device, but the unit asks for an address rather than giving one, and keeps asking until a unit in host mode answers. Plugged into that unit, or into a hub on it, it gets a 10.56.0.x address; until then the page says it is waiting. Its MAC addresses come from the unit's machine-id, so units on one hub differ. As a host. The port is a USB host, and a bridge ( br-usb ) takes in every USB Ethernet link on it and hands out 10.56.0.10 – 254 , with the unit at 10.56.0.1 . Units connected to a hub join it, and so does any other USB Ethernet plugged in: a USB Ethernet adapter on this unit joins the bridge rather than working on its own while host mode is on. The page says how many have joined. No mode hands out a route or DNS: a computer or another unit reaches this one, but its own traffic never goes through it (a wired route outranks Wi-Fi, so one would take over a laptop's internet). The device modes need a port with a device mode (a UDC under /sys/class/udc , or a USB role switch under /sys/class/usb_role that can make one) and the kernel's libcomposite . Host mode needs a host port of its own beside the UDC's, or a role switch. A mode the unit cannot do is not listed, and with none the page says what is missing. On a role switch (RK3326) the port is told to be a device in the device modes, and so is no host: a USB Wi-Fi or Ethernet adapter on that port stops working, and the page says so. Host mode on such a port refuses while another USB gadget holds it. Turning a mode off, or a failure, puts the port back as it was. Every mode is off again at the next restart unless Remember network settings is on (it is off until turned on). Then the mode in use comes back at boot: sndstation-usbnet.service runs the helper's usb-boot , which reads /etc/sndstation/usb-network . A remembered mode the unit can no longer do is left off, and the reason goes to the journal. How it all works, and what to check when a unit does not come up: usb-networking.md . SSH: off unless turned on here Turning SSH on asks first, on a card with CANCEL under the cursor, saying who could then log in. It stays on through restarts until it is turned off here. The page refuses to turn it on, and says what to do instead, when: the unit has no host keys yet. Release images make them on first boot; if they are missing, Make SSH host keys runs ssh-keygen -A . sshd takes passwords and the user's is empty , or still the image's default ( sst , which is public, or the user's own name). Exit to shell and run passwd , then turn SSH on. A key in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys does not make the default password safe while sshd still takes passwords; with PasswordAuthentication no , the key is the only way in, and that is allowed. The page never sets a password, never adds a key and never turns SSH on by itself. Note that the sndstation release images come with openssh-server enabled by Debian's package and the login sst / sst ; the page shows that as SSH is ON , and turning it off here disables it. How it works The launcher never runs as root. It runs one helper, /usr/lib/sndstation/sndstation-netctl ( tools/sndstation-netctl ), through sudo -n , and the package's install script lets the sudo group do that without a password, on a handheld only ( /etc/sudoers.d/sndstation-netctl , checked with visudo , removed with the package; the same rule lets Storage mount and eject cards, below). The helper uses what the image already has: Wi-Fi : NetworkManager ( nmcli ), which the release images ship with wpa_supplicant. A network is joined by writing a connection into NetworkManager's own store, /etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/ (0600, root), loading it and bringing it up. The passphrase reaches the helper on stdin, never on a command line ( sudo logs those, and /proc shows them to everyone); it is never printed or logged, and the launcher keeps no copy. A connection that does not come up is deleted. Forgetting deletes every saved connection for that network. SSH : OpenSSH's ssh.service ( sshd.service elsewhere), systemctl enable --now and disable --now (its socket too). Whether the password is the default is checked as root, against /etc/shadow , with the entry's own salt. Bluetooth : BlueZ's bluetoothctl ( show , power , scan , pair , trust , connect , disconnect , remove ). An address is checked to be one before it is passed on. BlueZ keeps the pairings, in /var/lib/bluetooth . USB networking : configfs ( /sys/kernel/config/usb_gadget/sndstation , after modprobe libcomposite ) for the gadget, and NetworkManager connections made with save no ( sndstation-usb ; in host mode the bridge sndstation-usbhost and its port profile sndstation-usbhost-port ), so none outlives a restart. A role switch is set to device (or host ) first, and back afterward (its old role is kept in /run , with the mode in use). Remembering is /etc/sndstation/usb-network , put back at boot by sndstation-usbnet.service . Interfaces : /sys/class/net , and ip -4 addr for the address, so an interface NetworkManager does not manage is listed too. Why nothing is there : /sys/class/rfkill , the kernel log ( dmesg ) for firmware that failed to load, and /sys/bus/sdio and /sys/bus/usb for hardware with no driver bound. Needs the image. The release images have NetworkManager and OpenSSH, but not yet BlueZ ( bluez ) or a USB gadget set up, and dnsmasq-base is needed for USB networking to hand out an address. Until the distribution adds them, Bluetooth says it is not installed and USB networking says what is missing; Wi-Fi and SSH work as they are. A call that runs too long is given up on: 40 s, or 75 s for joining a network or pairing a device. The helper and anything it started are killed, and the page says the helper did not answer in time; its own calls to bluetoothctl and systemctl stop at 30 s. The status is read again every 5 s on the page and every 30 s while it is closed (for the launcher's row), without a card; a button pressed meanwhile is not held up by it, its work goes next behind its own card. That read is one helper call ( status ), so one sudo , which the sudo rule leaves out of the logs since it changes nothing. The helper keeps its verdict on the password (a yescrypt per candidate, slow on these CPUs) in /run , root's only, until the hash changes; Bluetooth is two bluetoothctl devices listings (BlueZ 5.65 or later), not one call per device; and USB networking's status looks for libcomposite without loading it. It answers in lines the launcher reads ( netctl 1 , then key=value ), so it can be tried from a shell: sudo -n /usr/lib/sndstation/sndstation-netctl wifi-status . Without the helper, on a desktop say, the page says so; with --fake-net it runs on an in-memory unit with five networks around it ( correct horse joins any of them), Bluetooth off with three devices nearby, and a USB port that can do USB networking, for development and --shots (18–41, with the no-device and no-driver pages). Storage: SD cards, USB drives and files SETTINGS → Storage lists Home and every SD card and USB drive in the unit that is not the one the system runs from: its name (its label, or \"SD card\", \"USB drive\"), its file system, its size, and where it is mounted, with what is free on it, or that it is not mounted. A opens it in the file browser, mounting it first if it is not. Every file is offered; A on one shows it as it is, the way a license is shown (an image as an image, text as text, anything else as its first bytes in hex). B goes up a folder, and from where it opened back to the list. Y ejects a mounted one (written out, then unmounted: safe to take out), or mounts one that is not. X lists them again: nothing polls, so a card put in while the page is open shows up on X. The system's card has a row of its own after Home when the system can grow into what the card has unused (\"System card · +25.0 GB\"). An image written to a card is only as big as the image, and the rest of the card sits unused until it is grown into. The grow is the image's own /usr/local/bin/grow-rootfs (sndstation/distribution): it extends the root partition to the end of the card and grows its ext4, early in a boot, with its progress on screen, and nothing grows by itself. Without that script, or with no room, there is no row. A asks first (\"Grow by 25.0 GB in the next restart? Do not power off while it runs.\"); A again requests it ( grow-rootfs request ), B or any other key is a no. Then the row says it grows on the next restart, and A opens the power menu on Restart (below). Y , while it is pending, cancels it ( grow-rootfs cancel ). A card is mounted at /media/sndstation/<its label> (a label that is not a safe path is made one; no label, sd-mmcblk1p1 ), nosuid and nodev . FAT, exFAT and NTFS keep no owners, so they are mounted as the user's; the others keep their own. A card the image mounts by itself (ArkOS puts a second card at /roms2 ) is listed where it is, and the file browser offers it as a place. A file system it cannot mount (HFS+, say) is listed, and says so. How it works The listing is /usr/lib/sndstation/sndstation-storage list ( tools/sndstation-storage ), run as the user: lsblk needs no root. The disk the system runs from is the one any of the system's mount points ( / , /boot , /flash , /storage , /userdata ...) are on, and nothing on it is listed; with no such disk found, nothing is. Mounting and ejecting run the same helper through sudo -n , by the same sudo rule as Network's, and it mounts or unmounts only what it lists, and only at a new or empty directory. It answers in lines ( storage 1 , then vol records, a grow record for the system's card, and key=value ), so it can be tried from a shell: /usr/lib/sndstation/sndstation-storage list . With --fake-storage the page runs on a made-up SD card, a USB stick and a disk it cannot read, for development and --shots (21–21g). Software packages SETTINGS → Software packages is everything the unit installs, through apt. Opening it asks every source first, and the page stays grey until it has compared what each has with what is installed; X asks again. Offline (as NETWORK knows it), it asks nothing, shows what is installed, and removes; nothing says up to date until every source has answered a check. Leaving it after anything was installed, removed or upgraded walks the CLAP plugin folders, as at start, and reads APPS again. Updates , whose row names the sources its updates come from, installs the unit's updates: apt-get update , then what apt upgrade does, with every update listed by where it comes from (Security, each supplemental source, the base system, Kept back), its versions, size and changelog, and apt's own progress and log while it installs. The kernel and boot loader are held; an install refuses a battery under 30% that is not charging; nothing is ever installed unasked. A background check, at most daily, lets the row say how many there are. sndstation lists the sndstation source's apps, plugins, sounds and skins to get, update (A) or remove (Y), its row saying how many are installed, how many more are available, and whether any has an update; Vendors , one row that opens the list of every instrument maker the unit has, however many there are, with their demos and free products, any of them removed from the unit with Y and added back with A, where START gets every one not installed yet, after one card, and a vendor that did not answer is in red; Install a .deb file shows a full version's .deb , from a card or a stick, and installs it (a .deb opened in Storage's files comes here too). All of it is in updates.md . With --fake-updates the page runs on made-up updates and packages ( --shots 21h–21u, 21o0 the page while it asks, 21o1 and 21o2 offline, 21o3 before NETWORK has read the unit's status, 21o4 the vendors' list, 21o5 and 21o6 the page and that list with thirty vendors more). Display SETTINGS → Display has two rows, chosen with UP and DOWN: the screen's brightness, and the launcher's skin. Both show at once and are saved when B goes back. Brightness LEFT and RIGHT take the brightness down and up 5% at a time, held to keep going, and it shows at once. B goes back, and the level is saved in the platform's system.json (its user copy, \"display\": {\"brightness\"} ) and set again whenever an sndstation program opens its window (the SDK's Window does it), so every app has it, started from the launcher or on its own, booted into or not. Apps have nothing to do with it: it is the unit's setting. It drives the kernel's backlight, /sys/class/backlight/<name> (the one system.json's display.backlight names, else the kernel's preferred one), through sndstation/display.h . The level is as the eye sees it: the duty is level ^ brightness_gamma (2.2) over brightness_floor (5% of the maximum), so equal steps look equal and the lowest one still lights the screen. No root: the package's udev rule ( /etc/udev/rules.d/70-sndstation-backlight.rules , handhelds only) hands the backlight's brightness to the video group, which the session's user has to be in. A unit with no backlight there (vendor sunxi kernels take it through /dev/disp ) says so, and is left as it is. No brightness keys yet. Skin LEFT and RIGHT put on the next skin: the launcher's colors, all of them, changed from the next frame. A row of swatches shows the skin's colors. It is saved in the platform's system.json as \"display\": {\"skin\": ID} and put on when the launcher starts. Skin Dark the SDK's own look, and the default Light paper and ink, for daylight; the accents darkened to keep their contrast High contrast white on black, yellow and cyan accents, the quiet text no longer quiet Stage red dim reds on black, for a dark stage: it keeps the eyes' night vision The skins are config/skins.json , each a style block over the SDK's look, keyed by id. A unit's own ~/.config/sndstation/skins.json changes one or adds one without copying the rest ( \"order\" places it; Dark stays first). A skin sets colors only: fonts and sizes would need the window opened again. An id that names no skin leaves the SDK's look on and says so. An app wears the skin only if it asks: \"skin\": \"system\" in its manifest (above), or in its own style block. --skin ID runs the launcher in a skin for once, without saving it. With --shots , every screen is drawn in it. The shots always include each skin on APPS and SETTINGS (22e on) and the Skin row (22d). The on-screen keyboard Text typed with the pad, for the Wi-Fi passphrase and anything else that needs it. It is in the library ( sndstation/keyboard.h , Keyboard ), so an app can use it too. Four rows of ten keys, then five wide ones: case, #+= (symbols), SPACE, DEL and an OK key the page names (JOIN on Wi-Fi). The d-pad moves and wraps on every edge, A types the key under the cursor, B deletes (and on an empty field goes back), X is shift, Y a space, START is OK from anywhere, and SELECT shows or hides a masked field. A masked keyboard overwrites its text before letting it go. The Name layout, for a short label, has capitals, digits and - + / . only. The file browser A file picked with the pad, for a plugin's license and anything else that needs one. It is in the library beside the keyboard ( sndstation/filebrowser.h , FileBrowser ), and looks and moves like it: a field saying where it is, a tile for each folder and then each file it offers, and a row of wide keys, PLACES, UP and an OK key the page names (REGISTER for a license). It starts at the places: home, anything mounted under /media , /run/media or /mnt , any FAT or exFAT volume that is not /boot , and anything mounted from a disk the system is not on, wherever the image mounts it. A page that lists the places itself (Storage) opens it in a folder instead, and up from there is back to that page. Hidden files are left out, and only files with the extensions the caller names are offered. Up and Down move, Down from the last row reaches the wide row; A opens a folder or picks a file, B goes up a folder (from the places, back), Y goes to the places, START picks the file under the cursor. Plugins The launcher keeps the unit's CLAP plugin cache, ~/.config/sndstation/clap_plugins.json , the one file every app reads its plugin list from ( clapdb::platformDatabasePath() ). Every time it starts, which is at boot and each time an app exits, once the first-run Controls and Audio are out of the way: It walks the plugin folders: plugins.scan_paths from the platform's system.json , else /usr/lib/clap , /usr/local/lib/clap , ~/.clap and every directory in $CLAP_PATH . A file whose size and modification time are what the cache last saw is not read again, so a walk that finds nothing new costs next to nothing. Each plugin that is new, or whose binary changed, is loaded in a child process and checked and rated by clapprobe at its conformant level ( clap.md ), at the unit's own sample rate and buffer size. A screen counts them off meanwhile. One that crashes or hangs costs a child process and a line in the cache, never the launcher. Indexing preset files is what can take long, so the first half second of it is timed: when the rate says the rest will take two seconds or more, a second bar counts the files, and B (South) stops the indexing. A quick library, and presets inside the plugin (one call, nothing to show between), show nothing. Stopped, the presets found so far are kept, the plugin is checked to the end, and the next start indexes its presets again. A library of thousands of files takes as long as it needs; only one file that takes over 10 s stops it, and its presets are then left out. If anything is new, or gone, a message box says so: each new plugin with its rating out of 100, or that it crashes, hangs or will not load and is not listed. A file of many plugins is one line: how many it holds, how many play, and their ratings ( effects-pack: 12 plugins, 11 play, 71-100/100 ); the log has each one. A plugin whose presets were stopped says presets stopped , and one whose presets crashed or hung says presets left out . Any button closes it. The same goes to the log ( plugins: … ). Twenty packaged files, 195 plugins in all, take about fourteen seconds the first time on a desktop and nothing after that. The PLUGINS page lists every plugin the cache says plays. A on one shows it close up: its check and rating out of 100, which of the strict checks it missed, and when it was checked. There, A plays it (below), X (North) checks it again, Y (West) is its license's (REGISTER or SHOW LICENCE, when it has one), and B goes back to the list. The same buttons mean the same everywhere in the launcher: A does the thing, B goes back, X rescans or checks again, Y is the item's own second thing, START is A. X (North) on the list walks the folders again in the same way, for a plugin installed while the launcher was up; a walk that finds nothing new shows nothing. It also asks every plugin that says what its license is what it says now (below), since a license installed or removed does not change the plugin's file; one that changed is in the message. With --no-plugin-scan it only reads the cache again. A plugin that says what its license is (the license extension, clap.md ) has a tag on the list: FREE, FOSS, DEMO, EXPIRED or REGISTERED, with DEMO and EXPIRED the ones that stand out. Its close up says the same, with the license's SPDX name and URL for one that is open source, and what the plugin tells the user for one that is not registered. A plugin that says nothing has no tag and no license rows. DEMO, EXPIRED: Y (REGISTER) registers it the way it takes: a file, a key, or, when it takes both, a choice between them. A key is typed on the on-screen keyboard (B deletes, and cancels on an empty field), and START or REGISTER hands it to the plugin; a key it refuses stays there, to be corrected. A file is picked in a file browser. It starts at the places a file can come from: home, anything mounted under /media , /run/media or /mnt , and any FAT or exFAT volume that is not /boot . It offers only the files the plugin says a license ends in (all of them, when it does not say). A on a file hands it to the plugin. The plugin installs it itself, wherever it keeps its licenses, and says where. The launcher writes nothing but the plugin's new answer, into the cache. The browser is the library's (below, \"The file browser\"). The message after says the plugin accepted the file, and nothing of what is in it. REGISTERED: a license can carry a name, an email, an address, so nothing of it is on the close up: not who it is registered to, not the file. Y (SHOW LICENCE) shows it: the file the plugin names, a PNG as the picture it is (never stretched: at the most whole times it fits, or scaled down only when it is bigger than the screen), text as text, anything else as its first 512 bytes in hex, under who it is registered to. The first time in a run it asks first, with CANCEL under the cursor. Up and Down scroll; A or B close it. Playing a plugin A on a plugin's close up plays it in the launcher itself: no app is started, nothing is unloaded, and B or the plugin's own quit brings back the list. The host is the platform's ( ClapPlayer , clapplayer.h ), and it plays into the launcher's sound stream, a source on its mixer, with the launcher's MIDI (every input the unit listens to) pointed at it: it opens no device of its own ( audio-stack.md ). A plugin built for the hardware gets clap.embedded-hardware ( embedded-hardware.md ). A plugin built for the hardware (it has the extension's plugin side and asked for its event space) does its whole thing: the panel is its screen, its canvas scaled by a whole number and centered when it asks for a smaller one, and every control is its own but MENU and the volume keys, which stay the unit's. MENU opens the power menu with Close its name on top; the plugin's request_quit() closes it too. Sleep tells it ( suspend() , then resume() and a RESET). Any other plugin gets a test bench: the card, rate and period it plays at, its work as a share of each block (the worst in the last second), dropouts, the level of each side, MIDI in, the notes held, and a chord: A plays it while held, LEFT and RIGHT move its root a semitone, UP and DOWN an octave, Y changes its kind (major, minor, seventh, sus4). B leaves. Its state ( clap.state ) is saved when it closes, however it closes (the power menu's restart, shut down and exit included), and loaded the next time it plays: ~/.local/share/sndstation/plugins/ID.state . It runs in the launcher's process , so a plugin can take the launcher down with it. A crash ends the launcher, and the session starts it again on its main page ( sndstation-session ). A hang ends nothing, so a watchdog does: the main thread not back for 8 seconds, or the plugin's process() for 5 while the launcher's stream plays, ends the launcher the same way. Before loading, ~/.local/state/sndstation/plugin-running says which plugin it is, and a clean close removes it. A launcher that finds it says in the footer that the plugin stopped the last one, records when ( plugins-stopped , beside it), shows that on its close up (LAST PLAYED), and plays it again only on a second A. A plugin that did not pass its check asks the same. The power menu: sleep, restart, shut down, exit to shell MENU (the unit's FN, Home or MENU key), or SELECT held, then START , opens the power menu on any of the launcher's pages, over whatever is showing, except CONTROLS, which tests those buttons: Sleep Restart Shut down ───────── Exit to shell While a plugin plays (PLUGINS, A close up), Close its name is above them, the cursor on it. Both ways work on every unit: someone used to SELECT+START on another handheld finds it, and the SDK reports that chord as MENU to every app ( sndstation/pad.h , \"the Menu chord\"), so no app can give it another meaning. Every footer on the launcher's pages says how to reach it. Opening it is the deliberate step, so an item acts at once, with no second question. The cursor starts on Sleep , the harmless one, so a stray A right after opening never powers anything off. B, or MENU again, closes it. While packages are installing (SETTINGS → Software packages), Restart and Shut down are dimmed and the menu says why. Restarting and shutting down say so over everything until the unit goes; refused, the launcher stays and says why. Storage's \"restart now\", after asking for the system's partition to grow, opens this same menu on Restart. Shut down runs system.shutdown_cmd from the platform's system.json through sh . By default that is systemctl --no-ask-password poweroff , which logind lets the user at the unit's own screen run without root where polkit is installed, and failing that sudo -n /usr/sbin/poweroff , which the package's sudo rule ( /etc/sudoers.d/sndstation-netctl , handhelds only) allows the sudo group without a password: that command, with no arguments. --no-ask-password because otherwise, where polkit says no (a session logind does not count as at the screen, such as one started from a serial console), systemctl asks for a password on the launcher's terminal and waits there, and the sudo fallback never runs. Not a bare poweroff : it lives in /usr/sbin , which an ordinary user's PATH does not have on Debian, so sh never finds it. An image without systemd names its own command there. If the command fails the launcher stays up and says so, and sudo's own words are on stderr. Powering off is the platform's, not an app's: apps leave to the launcher (an app may offer sleep and quit, but not shut down). The goodbye. On a handheld, every power-off (Shut down, the power button, poweroff over SSH) ends with the boot splash's dude going to bed, at the splash's size: he hops in, pulls the covers up to his chin, shuts his eyes and says さようなら in a word balloon, then the lights go out, about three and a half seconds in all. sndstation-goodbye.service , wanted by poweroff.target (so not on a restart), runs sndstation --goodbye as the session user once tty1's session has gone and the display is free. The launcher tells systemd when it has played ( Type=notify , so the power-off waits that long) and keeps the screen dark to the end, so the console's shutdown messages never show. Exit to shell ends the session (exit 87, above). Restart (Storage's, once the system's partition is to grow) runs system.restart_cmd the same way: systemctl --no-ask-password reboot , else sudo -n /usr/sbin/reboot , which the same sudo rule allows with no arguments. All three are in the SDK, sndstation/power.h : power::off() is SHUT DOWN's, power::restart() Storage's, and power::sleep() an app's SLEEP. Sleep runs system.sleep_cmd , which has to block until the unit wakes: by default mem written to the kernel's /sys/power/state , through sudo -n tee , which the same sudo rule allows for that one file and nothing else. The stick lights are put away before and brought back after. On a desktop without the rule it is refused, and nothing happens. Options sndstation run it sndstation --launch ID start that app straight away, no list (e.g. booting into one app: the session's first run) sndstation --apps DIR also list the manifests in DIR (repeatable) sndstation --res WxH force the panel size sndstation --scale N a window N times the layout's size, on a desk sndstation --hdpi a desk's retina display at its full pixels, not stretched from 1x sndstation --rot N turn the frame N degrees onto the panel (0, 90, 180, 270; -1 = auto), over system.json's display.rotate sndstation --fake-display WxH dev: what the display is said to be, so a sideways panel can be looked at on a desk sndstation --shots DIR headless: write the screens as BMPs, then exit sndstation --skin ID this run in that skin (config/skins.json), not the saved one; with --shots, every screen in it sndstation --battery N dev: pretend the battery is at N% sndstation --fake-audio NAME dev: a handheld's sound card on a desk (rk817, rk817ext, h700, h616); it counts as a handheld for the first run sndstation --fake-net dev: Network's pages (Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, USB, SSH) on an in-memory unit, not the system's sndstation --fake-storage dev: Storage on a made-up SD card and USB stick, not the system's sndstation --fake-updates dev: Software packages on made-up updates and packages, installed slowly sndstation --tone-wav SIGNAL PATH dev: three seconds of the test sound as a WAV (pings, pings-gap, sine), or the whole MDFourier run (mdfourier[:RATE][:numpy2]) sndstation --audio-preset this unit's audio as an entry for config/audiopresets.json: its set-up, its speaker's maximum and its card, for a board the team has calibrated ([audio-page.md](audio-page.md)) sndstation --rescan-plugins check and rate every CLAP plugin again, not only the new ones (after a checker or dependency change) sndstation --no-plugin-scan leave the plugin cache alone this time sndstation --logind-lid print logind's drop-in for system.json's lid policy, then exit (sndstation-lid.service uses it) sndstation --goodbye play the goodbye, then keep the screen dark until the power-off ends it (sndstation-goodbye.service) On a handheld (a display of 1280×800 or less) it takes the panel whole. On a desktop it opens a 640×480 window, the size everything is designed at. Keys control L1 / R1, or Left / Right the page: APPS, PLUGINS, SETTINGS, ABOUT (every footer says so) MENU (FN), or SELECT held then START the power menu, on any page but CONTROLS (above) Up / Down choose (wraps) A (East), Start do the thing: open the app or the row; on PLUGINS, the plugin close up, and there play it, in the launcher (above) X (North) rescan or check again: on APPS and PLUGINS, what was installed or removed since the launcher started; in a plugin close up, check that plugin again Y (West) the item's own second thing: in a plugin close up, register a demo or show a registered plugin's license B (South) on PLUGINS, back from a plugin close up Up / Down, A, B in the power menu: choose, do, close Left / Right, A, B on Network's cards: choose, confirm, cancel They go through the SDK's pad layer, so a unit's gamepad map and the desktop keyboard (arrows, Z/A, Return) work as they do in any app. Not yet Hold Menu to quit an app back to the launcher. Icons. The manifest has no icon key until the launcher can draw one. Storage: mounting a card as it goes in, copying and deleting files, and formatting a card. Display: brightness keys, the screen dimming and going off when idle, and units whose brightness goes through /dev/disp (vendor sunxi kernels). Network: joining a hidden network, WEP and 802.1X, a static address, and showing the unit's host key fingerprint, so a first SSH login can be checked. Bluetooth devices that need a PIN.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/midi.html","t":"MIDI devices","s":"Subsystems","h":["MIDI devices","The usual app","The unit's choice of ports","Tiers","macOS and Windows"],"x":"MIDI devices <sndstation/midi.h> gives an app the unit's MIDI devices, in and out. Every message crosses the API as UMP, the Universal MIDI Packet of MIDI 2.0: a MIDI 1.0 channel message is one 32-bit word (message type 2), sysex is a run of two-word packets (type 3), so MIDI 1.0 gear loses nothing; a MIDI 2.0 device's channel voice messages (type 4: 16-bit velocity, 32-bit controllers) arrive at full resolution where the unit can carry them. An app that wants bytes asks UmpToBytes , which also scales MIDI 2.0 down by the specification's rules. CLAP carries both: CLAP_EVENT_MIDI2 is one UMP, for a plugin whose note port prefers the MIDI 2.0 dialect. The usual app struct Synth : sndstation::midi::Sink { void received(const sndstation::midi::Port&, const uint32_t* ump, size_t words, uint64_t timeNs) override { /* queue it for the audio thread */ } }; Synth synth; sndstation::midi::listen(&synth); // the unit's inputs, now and as plugged in ... sndstation::midi::stop(); received() runs on the SDK's MIDI thread, one packet per call: neither the audio thread nor the UI thread. Copy the packet somewhere lock-free and return. To pair a BLE-MIDI keyboard or pad, the launcher's Settings → MIDI → MIDI controllers scans for BLE-MIDI gear over Bluetooth; it is the same screen as Settings → Network → Bluetooth → MIDI controllers . Once connected, BlueZ gives the device an ALSA sequencer port, which midi::open() picks up as it appears, exactly as it would a USB keyboard. To see what a device sends, the launcher's Settings → MIDI → MIDI monitor lists every port and logs every input, decoded and in hex ( launcher.md ). Its source, launcher/midimon.cpp , is also a worked example of a Sink that hands packets to a UI thread through a lock-free ring. The unit's choice of ports system.json : \"midi\": { \"in\": [\"*\"], \"out\": [] } Port names or ids, or \"*\" for every port there is (the sequencer's own loopback, Midi Through, excepted). listen() opens every input named, and any that is plugged in later. outputs() lists the outputs named, for apps that send. An app may also list ports() and open() what it likes. Tiers The handhelds' kernels differ in what they build. Across ROCKNIX's device families today, one has the sequencer with UMP and USB MIDI 2.0, several have the sequencer without UMP, and several have no sequencer at all, only the raw MIDI devices. So the backend decides when it starts, from what it finds, never from the build alone, and logs the tier it is on: Tier When What you get sequencer (UMP) the kernel's sequencer has UMP, and alsa-lib is 1.2.10 or later MIDI 2.0 gear native; MIDI 1.0 gear converted by the kernel; ports shared with other programs sequencer (legacy) a sequencer without UMP, or an older alsa-lib MIDI 1.0, converted here; ports shared raw MIDI no sequencer MIDI 1.0, converted here; /dev/snd/midiC*D* directly, so one program per device none no MIDI, or another OS an empty port list alsa-lib's UMP calls are looked up at run time, not linked by name, so one binary built against a new alsa-lib still starts on a unit with an old one, on the legacy tier. A build against headers older than 1.2.10 leaves the UMP tier out. SNDSTATION_MIDI_TIER=legacy or =raw starts lower down, to try a tier on a unit that has a better one. Timestamps are CLOCK_MONOTONIC , taken as each packet is read. macOS and Windows No devices yet: the build there uses a backend with an empty port list, and the UMP helpers work as everywhere. Both platforms' current APIs are UMP-native (CoreMIDI's MIDIEventList , Windows MIDI Services), so each is a backend of the same shape when it is wanted.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/rt-bench.html","t":"--rt-bench: measuring the audio thread's headroom","s":"Subsystems","h":["--rt-bench: measuring the audio thread's headroom","Running it","What it found, and what ships","Pinning, and the helpers: measured, and left off","Why 95% fails, and why that is fine","Allowing it","Watching it day to day: the CPU meter","What a step is","Where \"underran\" comes from","Reading the output","Making a comparison mean something"],"x":"--rt-bench: measuring the audio thread's headroom Scheduling changes — a realtime audio thread, nicer helper threads, a reserved core — are easy to make and hard to judge by ear. --rt-bench turns \"it seems better\" into one number that can be compared across configurations, units and kernels: headroom , the share of each audio period the callback can spend before the card underruns. Running it The bench is the SDK's ( <sndstation/rtbench.h> ), and an app runs it: it hands rtbench::start() its open AudioOut and an rtbench::Instrument (a chord to hold, and a name for it), takes the --rt-* flags with rt::parseArg() , and calls rtbench::frame() every frame. examples/hello does, with its test tone as the instrument ( hello --rt-bench ); it is the wiring to copy. With app standing for an app that does: app --rt-bench --rt-noise 4 # as shipped: SCHED_FIFO 70 app --rt-bench --rt-noise 4 --rt-fifo 0 # baseline: an ordinary thread app --rt-bench --rt-noise 4 --rt-cpu 2 # audio thread pinned to core 2 app --rt-bench --rt-noise 4 --rt-helpers-nice 10 # helpers lowered The audio thread's scheduling comes from the platform's system.json — audio.realtime_priority (70 as shipped) and audio.cpu (-1, no pin) — and the flags override it for one run. A baseline has to say --rt-fifo 0 explicitly. It runs the real program — window, UI drawing, the instrument that is loaded — so what it measures is what the app does on that unit, not what the hardware could do in isolation. After a 3 s warm-up it holds a chord (struck again every 2 s) and steps the callback's load up; at each step it prints a line, and at the end one RESULT line, then quits. Flag Default --rt-bench run the sweep, print, quit --rt-loads a,b,… 0,50,70,80,85,90,95 percent of the period, in order --rt-step S 20 seconds per step --rt-fifo P audio.realtime_priority audio thread to SCHED_FIFO P; 0 = ordinary --rt-cpu N audio.cpu audio thread pinned to core N; -1 = any --rt-helpers-nice N off helper threads ( ss-amixer , ss-leds ) to nice N --rt-noise N 0 N competing threads on every core --rt-proc 0\\|1 0 when alsa-lib's count is available /proc status reads, for the buffer margin (below) The --rt-fifo , --rt-helpers-nice and --rt-noise switches work without --rt-bench as well, so a configuration that measured better can be listened to. What it found, and what ships On an RG351V (RK3326, kernel 7.1.2, 48 kHz / 512 frames, built-in synth), with --rt-noise 4 : audio thread headroom worst late, no added load ordinary, nice 0 0% — 115 underruns at 50% 12.8 ms ordinary, nice -20 (as root) 0% — 6 underruns at 50% 5.7 ms SCHED_FIFO 70 90% 1.7 ms Without the noise, an ordinary thread still came 4-4.8 ms late 36 times a minute, at irregular spacing (the UI, Mesa, kernel workers); at FIFO 70, never more than 1 ms. A high nice is not enough: an ordinary thread at any nice still shares the CPU fairly, and only a realtime policy goes first. A Powkiddy RGB30 (RK3566, kernel 7.0.2, 48 kHz / 256 frames, built-in synth, booted rt_group_sched=0 — see \"Allowing it\") agrees: with no added load an ordinary thread came 18 ms late at worst and underran twice in a minute; at FIFO 70, 1.12 ms and clean. With --rt-noise 4 , clean through 90%, failing at 95%. So the audio thread runs SCHED_FIFO 70 by default , raised by the thread itself on its first callback ( rt::audioThreadEnter ) rather than through SDL's priority hints, which pick their own number and say nothing when refused. Pinning, and the helpers: measured, and left off Same unit and noise, FIFO 70 in each: headroom failed at worst late, no added load no pin ( audio.cpu -1, shipped) 90% 95% 1.56 ms pinned to core 2 ( --rt-cpu 2 ) 85% 90% 1.68 ms helpers at nice 10 ( --rt-helpers-nice 10 ) 90% 95% 1.55 ms Pinning failed a step earlier, on the same once-a-second ~50 ms stalls as below. That fits how the kernel's reserve for ordinary threads works: it is paid per core, and an unpinned realtime thread can move to another core while one is paying it; a pinned one has to wait it out. One step is within run-to-run noise, but nothing points the other way, so the audio thread is not pinned. Lowering the helpers changed nothing, as it should not: once the audio thread is realtime, no ordinary thread gets ahead of it at any nice. Both stay as flags, for measuring. Why 95% fails, and why that is fine At 95% the FIFO thread underran once a second, ~50 ms each time, like clockwork ( median spacing 1.01 s ). That is the kernel keeping 50 ms of every second, on each CPU, for ordinary threads (RT throttling; on newer kernels the \"fair server\"): the noise threads starved by a thread that would take 95% of a core get their 50 ms at once. It is the same rule that keeps a runaway plugin from freezing the box — it stalls its own audio instead — and it puts the practical ceiling near 90%. At a short buffer the failure is not a few clicks but a cascade: the RGB30 at 256 frames underran 1639 times in 11 s at 95%. After an underrun the stream restarts nearly empty, and a callback that takes 95% of every period gains only 5% of one back per period — never enough to refill before the next stall. (The console says each underrun at most once a second, with a count of the rest.) Allowing it A user may raise a thread to realtime only up to its RLIMIT_RTPRIO ( ulimit -r ), which is 0 by default. Without an allowance the raise is refused, said once on stderr — rt: audio thread: SCHED_FIFO 70 refused (Operation not permitted; ulimit -r is 0) — it stays ordinary. — and shown on the launcher's AUDIO / DIAGNOSTICS, row Thread , in the warning color. On a recognized handheld (an RK3326, RK3566 or H700 device tree, or a Raspberry Pi with no desktop installed) the package's install script links /etc/security/limits.d/90-sndstation.conf into place — on a desktop it does not, and says so — granting the audio group 95: what PipeWire's own 25-pw-rlimits.conf grants @pipewire , so on a machine with both it can only raise a limit, never lower PipeWire's below the 88 it asks for. pam_limits applies it at login : log out and in again, then ulimit -r should say 95. The user must be in audio ( groups ; sudo usermod -aG audio <user> ). Refused although ulimit -r allows it — the program says so on a second line: rt: the limit allows 70, so the kernel is refusing it: a kernel built with CONFIG_RT_GROUP_SCHED gives a login session no realtime budget. Boot it with rt_group_sched=0 on the kernel command line. With CONFIG_RT_GROUP_SCHED=y and cgroup v2, every cgroup but the root gets a realtime budget of zero, and a login session ( /user.slice/…/session-N.scope ) is never the root: realtime is refused there for everyone, root and sudo chrt included. Found on a Powkiddy RGB30 (kernel 7.0.2). Kernels with the option also take a boot parameter that switches it off, rt_group_sched=0 : append it to the kernel command line — on most handheld images the APPEND line of /boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf — and reboot. cat /proc/cmdline shows it took; chrt -f 70 true from the session then succeeds. The install script checks for this kernel and says so, but leaves the boot line alone. Where the login does not go through pam_limits , the binary can carry the capability itself: sudo setcap cap_sys_nice+ep <path to the app> . Two catches — a binary with a capability ignores LD_LIBRARY_PATH , and a rebuild drops it. Watching it day to day: the CPU meter The bench answers \"how much more fits\"; the status bar answers \"how much is this using\". CPU 34% , beside the battery, is the share of one core the audio thread used over the last second — the instrument or plugin, plus SDL's conversion and the ALSA write — from the kernel's clock for that thread ( CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID ), which counts what the thread ran and nothing it waited for. It is the figure to watch while optimizing: a function that took the meter from 56% to 34% did exactly that. The color follows the worst period of that second, not the average: amber once one render took 70% of its period, red at 90%. An average of 34% can hide the one block that clicks. The same two figures are on the launcher's AUDIO / DIAGNOSTICS (row CPU ) and on the --stats line ( audio cpu 34% (peak 61%) ). ui.cpu_meter hides the status-bar one. To see the same thing in btop, pin the thread ( \"audio\": { \"cpu\": 2 } ): core 2 then carries the audio thread, and its graph reads about what the meter does — a little more, since btop also counts whatever else the kernel runs on that core. Pinning measured a step worse under heavy competition (below), which is why it is not the default; for watching, that is a fair trade. Measured against a known load (the bench's busy-loop, SDL's dummy driver): expected 20% and 33%, the meter read 21% and 33%. What a step is The callback renders as usual, then spins until it has used the step's share of the period — standing in for a heavier plugin. At 70% on a 5.33 ms period, every callback takes 3.7 ms. The thread is still paced by the card (SDL's ALSA backend blocks in snd_pcm_writei ), so a sustained load below 100% only underruns when the thread is woken late — which is exactly what scheduling changes are meant to fix. The sweep stops at the first step that underran. Headroom is the last load that did not. When no step underran — the list ran out, or the run was cut short — it is printed as a floor, headroom=>=90% : nothing higher was tried. Stopping a run early (Ctrl-C, or quitting) is fine, and on a long step the normal way to end one: the step in progress is measured as far as it got, printed under (step stopped after N s) , and counted. A configuration that moves it from 60% to 85% is better by an amount that means something: that much more plugin fits. Where \"underran\" comes from Two accounts, neither of them this program's own timing: alsa-lib. The stream (audio-device.md §3.0, or SDL's ALSA backend) recovers every underrun with snd_pcm_recover(…, silent = 0) , and alsa-lib reports it — ALSA lib pcm.c:…:(snd_pcm_recover) underrun occurred . The bench installs alsa-lib's error handler (through the copy SDL already loaded) and counts those, still printing every message as alsa-lib would. xruns in the step line. The card's buffer. /proc/asound/cardN/pcmNp/subN/status carries avail_max , how empty the buffer got; buffer_size - avail_max is the margin that was left. The kernel resets avail_max on every read of status , so with --rt-proc 1 the bench reads it four times a second and keeps the worst. margin min in the step line, in frames; 0 or less is an underrun. Off by default whenever alsa-lib's count is there, because reading status is not free of consequences: it makes the kernel ask the driver where the DMA is, and acts on the answer. On an RG CubeXX (H700, kernel 7.2) the bench counted single, sudden underruns — about one a minute, with every callback on time (worst 0.6 ms late of a 21 ms buffer) — while the same stream played for minutes without one when nothing read status . The reads are the difference, so they are off unless asked for. On the RG351V (RK3326) they never caused one. Some kernels have no such file at all: the per-stream directories are CONFIG_SND_VERBOSE_PROCFS , which the RGB30's 7.0.2 leaves off (its card1/ holds only id ). There the bench says so on its first line, the RESULT line has access=unknown buffer=unknown , DIAGNOSTICS shows no buffer or margin, and alsa-lib's count is the only one. A step is clean only when both say so. On a backend that is not ALSA (PipeWire, PulseAudio, CoreAudio on a Mac) neither exists for this stream, and a wakeup of the device more than one of its cycles late is the stand-in; the header line says which applies. A device that takes several periods at a wakeup. Not every backend calls once a period. CoreAudio on a Mac, its device cycle 512 frames under a 256-frame stream, calls twice back to back every 11.6 ms: gaps of 0.01 and 11.6 ms, never 5.8 (measured with plain SDL, so it is the backend, not this program; asking SDL for 256-frame device buffers changed nothing). Measured against one period, every other callback looked 5.8 ms late, and a sweep \"underran\" at 0%. So the warm-up counts the callbacks per wakeup — burst in the RESULT line, with a line saying so when it is more than 1 — and a callback is late only by how far it trails the schedule of every one of the last burst callbacks; the stand-in above is a wakeup more than burst periods late. With a burst of 1, which ALSA's is, that is a callback's gap less one period, exactly as before, so results from before the change compare. On that Mac, with --rt-noise 4 : lateness about 0.06 ms at every step, clean through 95%. Nothing else may read the PCM's status file during a run. AUDIO / DIAGNOSTICS reads it twice a second, which is why it was once the thing to close first; it is the launcher's now and never open while an app runs, but a shell loop on /proc/asound would do the same: it resets avail_max under the bench, and on the H700 it can cause the very underrun above. Reading the output rtbench: load 70% calls 3750 late p99 0.21 p99.9 0.64 max 1.90 ms render max 0.31 ms (cpu 0.29) margin min 180 f xruns 0 clean rtbench: worst: +3.12s 0.58 ms late (render before 0.28, cpu 0.27) +8.11s 1.90 ms late (render before 0.25, cpu 0.24) ... rtbench: RESULT headroom=85% unit=\"Anbernic RG351V\" kernel=6.12.x driver=alsa pcm=hw:0,0 access=RW_INTERLEAVED rate=48000 frames=256 burst=1 buffer=512 audio=\"SCHED_OTHER, nice 0, cpus 2\" fifo=0 helpers_nice=0 noise=0 instrument=TESTSYNTH base_late_p999=0.64ms base_late_max=1.90ms late — how far past the period each callback arrived, as percentiles; per wakeup of the device where it takes several callbacks at once ( burst , above). The 0% step's figures ( base_late_* in the result) are the scheduling noise floor with no load at all. render max … (cpu …) — rendering alone, before the spin, as wall time and as the CPU time the thread actually got for it. worst — the four latest callbacks of the step, in time order: when (seconds into the step), how late, and the callback before it — its render in wall time and in CPU time. Those two say whose fault it was: cpu about equal to render, both long : the instrument's own work — a voice starting, a sample streamed. Scheduling cannot fix it. render long, cpu short : the thread was preempted in the middle of rendering. Wall time alone would blame the instrument for this; it is scheduling, the same as — both short : the thread was not run at all until late. Evenly spaced times point at a periodic cause: SDL's ALSA backend re-lists the devices every 5 s from a thread of its own ( SDLHotplugALSA ). - over 2 ms late — every callback that late, by time (the first 32), and the median spacing between them. Four worst cases are too few to show a period; this is where a steady spacing shows up. - instrument — what was actually sounding, as the app names it: an instrument that failed to load and left a fallback playing is a different load, and the report says which. - audio — the audio thread as the kernel reports it, read back rather than assumed: policy, priority, nice, the cores it may run on. With --rt-fifo and no rtprio allowance ( ulimit -r is 0) it says the raise was refused, and the run measured an ordinary thread. Making a comparison mean something Same unit, same instrument, same page, same --rt-noise . The background is part of the measurement. Several runs per configuration. A single run's worst moment is partly luck. Three runs that agree are a result; one is an anecdote. Change one thing at a time against a baseline run made the same day.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/stick-leds.html","t":"The lights around the analog sticks","s":"Subsystems","h":["The lights around the analog sticks","Why it is its own layer","Isolated by unit","Nothing is guessed at","The two protocols","ch32-frame — Anbernic H700 (RG40XX H/V, RG CubeXX)","aislpc-preset — XiFan XF40H and kin (RK3326, RK3562)","Permissions","Checking a unit"],"x":"The lights around the analog sticks Part of the sndstation design. Built: include/sndstation/leds.h , src/leds.cpp , config/leds.json , system/70-sndstation-leds.rules , sndstation --led-test and --led-slots , and test_leds . Nothing draws on the rings yet: this is the layer that makes them a thing the rest of the program can draw on without knowing which handheld it is running on. Why it is its own layer SDL cannot reach these lights. Its LED call speaks only to controllers it has a HID driver for (DualShock, Switch Pro); these are a microcontroller on the board, on a serial port, behind a power switch, and every family does it differently. Worse, the same board does it differently under different kernels: an RG40XX H's chip is ttyS5 with a battery-driver power file on Anbernic's kernel, and ttyS2 with an LED-device power switch on ROCKNIX's mainline one. So the program sees two rings of logical LEDs, LEFT and RIGHT, each with a count , and everything else is a fact about the unit, in leds.json : leds::fill(leds::Ring::Left, {0xFF, 0x6A, 0x00}); leds::set(leds::Ring::Right, 3, {0, 0xD4, 0xC8}); leds::commit(); // one write for both rings, from a worker thread count() , addressable() and describe() are all a caller ever needs to ask. A device that only has a few preset colors reports one LED per ring and shows the nearest preset to whatever is drawn, so drawing code written for the full-color rings degrades instead of branching. Isolated by unit leds.json is keyed the way audio.json is: the unit's id (the SOC ID on the ABOUT page), the board model ( /proc/device-tree/model ), then \"*\" . Unlike audio.json the matches are merged, widest first . Wiring is a fact about a model, shared by every unit of it, so the shipped entries are per model; what one unit needs differently is all its own entry says, in ~/.config/sndstation/leds.json : \"H700-…\": { \"rings\": { \"right\": [4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2, 3] } } \"H700-…\": { \"enabled\": false } The rings map is logical index → wire slot, and the convention it maps onto is index 0 = North, clockwise, seen from the front (0 N, 1 NE, 2 E … 7 NW), so drawing code can point at a direction. A map is measured, never extrapolated. The first maps shipped for the RG40XX H and RG CubeXX were built from two or three positions per ring, on the assumption that the rest carry on evenly round it; on the box they were wrong — the right ring skipped a position — so the slots do not simply run round in order, and both entries went back to the wire order, marked unmeasured. Compass names and \"clockwise\" were also read two ways in the reports that went into them. So --led-slots asks about every slot, one at a time and at the holder's pace, as a clock position seen from the front : $ sndstation --led-slots slot 0> R 9 slot 1> R 7:30 … \"rings\": { \"right\": [6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0, 7], \"left\": [8, 15, 14, 13, 12, 11, 10, 9] } It computes the map from the answers and prints the entry to paste; a position given to two slots, or to none, is reported and the map refused rather than filled in. --led-test then lights the rings in logical order and prints the compass point each step should be: a correct map walks both rings N, NE, E… clockwise. Whether two units of one model can differ — a ring board that fits more than one way round — is not known. That is what a unit entry is for: the model's map is the first unit measured, and a unit that differs overrides rings alone under its own unit id. Nothing is guessed at Nothing is touched until the first commit() . A unit with lights and a program that has not drawn on them leaves them as the system left them. The serial port is found by the UART it is wired to ( node:5001400.serial , matched against /sys/class/tty/*/device ), never by a ttyS number alone, which comes from device-tree aliases. A port that matches neither the node nor an explicit path in the entry is not opened: on another board the same number can be the Bluetooth chip. The power switch is whichever listed form is present — led:<name> , file:<path> , gpio:<chip>:<line> (the character device, not the sysfs numbers mainline now allocates from 512) or none . A commit that changes nothing sends nothing, so drawing code can commit every frame; changes are capped at 30 a second and a burst coalesces into its last state. Around a system sleep the rings go dark and the chip unpowered before the sleep command runs, and the last frame is sent again after: the chip forgets everything when the board sleeps. The two protocols ch32-frame — Anbernic H700 (RG40XX H/V, RG CubeXX) A CH32V003 on the H616/H700's UART5, 115200 8N1 raw: [mode, brightness, 16 × (R, G, B), checksum] checksum = sum & 0xFF Verified on an RG40XX H under a ROCKNIX kernel : slots 0-7 are the right ring and 8-15 the left, each taking its own color (one LED lit at a time, by eye). Mode 1 is plain color and is all this sends; the chip's own effects (2-4 breathing, 5-6 rainbows) are animations the program can draw itself, in time with the music, which the chip's cannot be. Dark is mode 1 at brightness 0. power port mainline (ROCKNIX) /sys/class/leds/rgb:kbd_backlight/brightness ttyS2 vendor (Anbernic, Knulli) /sys/class/power_supply/axp2202-battery/mcu_pwr ttyS5 Sources: ROCKNIX's quirks/platforms/H700/bin/analog_sticks_ledcontrol and its uart5 device-tree patch; Knulli's allwinner/h700/.../knulli-rgb-led . aislpc-preset — XiFan XF40H and kin (RK3326, RK3562) One byte per command at 9600 baud, each sent several times: 1 =G 2 =B 3 =R 4 =G+B 5 =R+G 6 =B+R 7 =white (rainbow on some boards) 8 =flow 9 =off, 17-24 breathing; some boards ignore everything until an init byte 0x20 + leds/4 − 1 . No arbitrary color, no per-LED control. Every number that differs between boards is in the entry ( presets , off , init , repeat , gap_ms ), and a color drawn is shown as the nearest preset by hue, so a dim orange is still orange. Not verified here , so the shipped XF40H entry is disabled: the protocol is bmdhacks' reverse-engineering on the RG52 Mini (dArkOS) as ported in AveyondFly's ROCKNIX fork, and the XF40H's port ( ff178000.serial ), power line (GPIO2_A1, assumed gpiochip2:1 ), init byte and repeat count are all unconfirmed. gpioinfo and /sys/class/tty/*/device on the unit settle them. Permissions sndstation does not run as root, and should not — it hosts third-party plugins. The serial port is the dialout group's on Debian already; the power switch is a sysfs attribute no group can write, so on a handheld the package's install script links in a udev rule handing exactly that file to dialout , for both the mainline and the vendor form. A rule only applies when its device appears, which is at boot, so the package's install script replays the \"add\" for those two devices alone; without that, installing on a running box changed nothing until a reboot. The user sndstation runs as has to be in dialout . The XF40H's GPIO chip is not handed over yet: a GPIO bank holds more than one LED's power, and that rule waits until the line is confirmed. Checking a unit sndstation --led-slots # MEASURE: every wire slot, asked about one at a # time; prints the \"rings\" entry sndstation --led-test # CHECK: each logical LED in turn, with the # compass point it should be, then both rings in # their stick colours, then dark The first line says what was found — driver, port, power switch, rings, and which entries it came from — or exactly why nothing was.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/unit-identity.html","t":"Which unit this is","s":"Subsystems","h":["Which unit this is","The problem","The SoC can answer it","One row on the panel, and nothing beside it","Raspberry Pi, Amlogic, MediaTek: the chip's own number","This is a fingerprint, not a proof of uniqueness"],"x":"Which unit this is Part of the sndstation design. Built and shipping: include/sndstation/sysinfo.h , src/sysinfo.cpp ( sysfacts::unitId() , sysfacts::socName() ), shown on the launcher's ABOUT, and the key audio.json is written under. The problem The device tree cannot answer \"which console is this\". Of seventeen units surveyed, seven report the same board model and the same compatible — and they do not behave the same: five route audio cleanly, two play out of both outputs at once. A profile keyed on the DTB is shared between machines that disagree about the very thing being profiled. The SoC can answer it It was burned at manufacture and cannot change. RK3326 carries a package lot and a per-die block in OTP ( /sys/bus/nvmem/devices/rockchip-otp0/nvmem ), reverse-engineered across 18+ retail handhelds — see sndstation/src/rk3326_otp.h , which is plgDavid's work and carries its own notes. It is a scan , in the shape the socket detection already uses on this project: try every door, report what each said, take the first that answered. The RK3326 decoder, so a family we understand yields a readable id. Then every /sys/bus/nvmem/devices/*/nvmem in turn, hashed — so a family nobody has written a decoder for (an Allwinner SID, the next clone) still produces a stable fingerprint with no new code . SOC ID RK3326-URKC08010-4FE23CAAD163F02F A readable prefix and a hash of all 64 bytes . Deciding which fields matter is exactly the decision that cannot be made from a datasheet that documents none of this region, and a fingerprint ignoring the undecoded bytes cannot separate two chips differing only in them. The full hex goes into audio.json as unit_raw , beside the conclusion drawn from it, so the fingerprint can be recomputed differently later without the console. One row on the panel, and nothing beside it The line above the SOC ID already says \"Rockchip RK3326\", and the lot and the die bytes are both inside the hash — printing them as well is the same fact three times. Two consoles differ here visibly, which is the only thing a person needs from this line, and it is verbatim the key audio.json is written under, so it is also how you find this machine's entry in the file: R40XX RK3326-URKC08010-A599D7394C5B58B6 R36S Blue RK3326-URKC08010-4FE23CAAD163F02F Same lot, different unit. The decode — package, revision, die block, leakage, performance, and any non-zero byte outside those — goes to the log , and the bytes themselves to unit_raw . Neither belongs on a panel where 48 of the 64 bytes are zero on every chip seen so far. Raspberry Pi, Amlogic, MediaTek: the chip's own number These three families publish a serial, or a block the serial comes from, where a decoder can find it. Which family a unit is comes from the device tree's compatible , and its decoder runs after the RK3326 one and before the generic nvmem scan. The id is the chip and that number, both readable, nothing hashed when the number is already the chip's own: Family Doors, in order Id Raspberry Pi the firmware's system/linux,serial in the device tree (8 bytes, from OTP); the device tree's serial-number ; /proc/cpuinfo Serial BCM2711-100000001A2B3C4D Amlogic the secure monitor's chip ID ( /sys/bus/platform/drivers/meson-sm/*/serial ); the soc bus's serial_number ; the device tree's serial-number ; a vendor kernel's /proc/cpuinfo Serial S922X-0A0B0C0D0E0F101112131415 MediaTek the efuse (an nvmem device whose node is a mediatek,…efuse ), hashed; LK's chosen/atag,devinfo , hashed; the soc bus's serial_number ; the device tree's serial-number ; androidboot.serialno on the command line MT8167-075C8ED50C0E3A03 A serial that is too short, one digit repeated or a bootloader's placeholder ( 0123456789ABCDEF ) is refused, and the next door tried. A MediaTek kernel has to publish the efuse for the first door to find it: CONFIG_NVMEM=y and CONFIG_NVMEM_MTK_EFUSE=y . The PlayStation Classic's device tree has its efuse ( 10009000.efuse ), but a kernel built without those has no driver bound to it and no /sys/bus/nvmem at all, and nothing else of the chip's own either: no soc bus, no devinfo, no serial on the command line. Its log then says \"no identity source\", door by door. The chip in the id is named from the device tree too: the last compatible is the SoC ( mediatek,mt8167 → MT8167; Allwinner's sun50i-h700 → H700). Amlogic lists its package before the family ( amlogic,s922x , amlogic,g12b → S922X, in G12B), and the kernel's soc bus, where a driver fills it in ( /sys/devices/soc0/soc_id : \"G12B (S922X)\"), outranks the device tree. A Raspberry Pi's board revision code names its chip better than its device tree does, and is decoded into the log: revision c03114: Pi 4B 1.4, 4 GB, Sony UK, BCM2711 . ABOUT's CPU row carries the same name, \"MediaTek MT8167 · 4 cores\", with the soc bus's revision when there is one: a mainline arm64 kernel has no \"Hardware\" line in /proc/cpuinfo , and left that row saying only how many cores. After the cores come the clock the CPU runs at now and the cpufreq governor choosing it (\"1.25 GHz · performance\"), the fastest cluster's on a chip with two. A unit of these families that had an id from the generic scan before has a new one now, and starts from nothing under it: what was saved under the old key is not looked for. The log lists every door, answered or not, after a soc: line: soc: Broadcom BCM2711 — revision c03114: Pi 4B 1.4, 4 GB, Sony UK, BCM2711 unit: BCM2711-100000001A2B3C4D (rpi-serial) unit: [no ] /sys/bus/nvmem/devices/rockchip-otp0/nvmem cannot open (root only?) unit: [yes] /proc/device-tree/system/linux,serial serial 100000001a2b3c4d tests/test_socid.cpp builds each family's /proc and /sys in a directory and scans it ( sysfacts::scanUnit(root) ). This is a fingerprint, not a proof of uniqueness Eighteen units gave one collision on the lot alone, resolved by the die bytes. That is evidence the combination discriminates better than anything else available, not that it cannot collide. Treated as one, the worst case is two consoles sharing a profile — which is where every one of them is today. --fake-otp PATH reads the OTP from a file, so the page can be looked at and the decoder exercised on a machine with no RK3326 in it.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/updates.html","t":"Software packages","s":"Subsystems","h":["Software packages","Where packages come from","Vendors","Packages, for vendors and for sndstation","A .deb file","Publishing a source","The updates","How others do it, and why this follows the command line","The page","Groups","On the system side","What is held, and why","The background check","Supplemental sources","Not here","Development"],"x":"Software packages SETTINGS → Software packages is everything the unit installs, through apt: UPDATES the unit's updates, the system's, sndstation's and the vendors': apt-get update , then what apt upgrade does, shown whole (below). Its row names the sources they come from: Includes: Debian, Debian-Security, sndstation, Sound Works . SNDSTATION the sndstation source's packages: apps, plugins, sounds, skins. Its row says how many are installed, how many more are available, and UPDATES AVAILABLE when one of those installed has a newer version. A gets or updates one; Y removes one. VENDORS one row, however many vendors the unit has: their names, how many of their packages are installed and how many more are available, UPDATES AVAILABLE when one of them has a newer version of something installed, and in red any that did not answer. A opens their list, a page of its own: each vendor, with a tile of its own color, its row as sndstation's, and in red with apt's reason when it did not answer. There, A lists a vendor's packages: demos and free ones. Nothing is asked: a vendor is trusted by being in the sndstation-vendors package (below). Y removes a vendor from the unit, after a card that says what stays; its row then says removed , and A on it adds it back. B on a vendor's packages goes back to the list. A list longer than the page follows the selection, and says which of how many it is on. FROM FILES Install a .deb file : a full version, bought from a vendor, from a card or a stick; shown first, installed on A. And the packages installed that way, to remove. A .deb opened in STORAGE's files comes here too. Opening the page asks every source before it shows a count: apt-get update for every source apt has, whether each vendor whose source is not written yet answers ( ping , below), then what each source has against what is installed, and what an upgrade would take. Until then the page is grey and only B works, with how many sources have answered and for how long. X ( rescan all ) asks again. That look is the UPDATES page's too, so opening it does not ask every source a second time. Offline, nothing is asked. Whether the unit is online is NETWORK's answer (Wi-Fi joined, or a wired link up with an address; USB to a PC is not one), read every 30 seconds. Offline, the page shows what is installed, from the unit's own lists, and is not gray: Y removes a package. Getting, updating, Get all and setting up a vendor whose source is not written yet say \"Offline: connect in NETWORK first\". The background check and the UPDATES page's own check are skipped too. Not known yet is not online. Just after the launcher starts, NETWORK has not read the unit's status yet. Until it has, the page is as it is offline: what is installed, removable, and nothing asked. Once NETWORK says the unit is online, the sources are asked, and they are too when the unit comes online while the page is open, without X. The background check waits for NETWORK to say the unit is online. \"Up to date\" only after every source answered. SETTINGS' row, the UPDATES row here and the UPDATES page say up to date only when the last check reached every source apt has. Otherwise they say how many updates the unit's lists know of (\"· offline\" after it while offline), offline , or how many sources did not answer. In a source's list, an installed package is in the style's ok color and one not installed is gray; one with an update shows both versions, the installed one and then its source's, in note (docs/sdk-style.md, state colors). A gets a package or updates that one alone; Y removes an installed one, with an update pending or not. A never removes. Leaving the page after anything was installed, removed or upgraded walks the plugin folders, as the launcher does at start: a CLAP plugin a package brought is checked and reported, and one it took is gone from PLUGINS, and from what every app reads. APPS is read again too, as it is the moment each install ends. Every update is listed with where it comes from, from which version to which, and how big it is; while anything installs, apt's own progress and log are on screen. Nothing is installed unless someone asks. Where packages come from Debian (or the unit's base system): its updates only, in UPDATES. sndstation , https://sndstation.s3.us-west-1.amazonaws.com , suite stable : the platform's own updates, and its extra packages, free. The sndstation package ships the source ( /etc/apt/sources.list.d/sndstation.sources , a conffile) and the key it is signed with ( /usr/share/keyrings/sndstation-archive-keyring.asc ); a package built without that key ( packaging/sndstation-archive-keyring.asc ) ships no source at all, as an unsigned one would fail every check. Vendors : instrument and effect makers, each with its own signed source, which carries demos and free products only . A vendor in the sndstation-vendors package is trusted: its source is written when that package is installed, and the unit does not ask. Files : a full, registered product is never in a source. It is a .deb the buyer gets from the vendor, and installs from wherever they put it. Vendors A unit's vendors are files in /usr/share/sndstation/vendors , put there by a package (the platform names none itself): # /usr/share/sndstation/vendors/soundworks.vendor Name: Sound Works About: Instruments and effects: demos and free ones URIs: https://apt.soundworks.example/debian Suites: stable Components: main A flat repository (one directory with no dists/ , as on a GitHub release) names the suite ./ and no Components . Beside it is the public key its source is signed with, soundworks.asc (armored) or soundworks.gpg . Turning one on ( sndstation-updates vendor-on soundworks ) writes its source, /etc/apt/sources.list.d/sndstation-vendor-soundworks.sources , signed by that key, and pins it below the system's ( /etc/apt/preferences.d/sndstation-vendor-soundworks.pref , priority 100, by its host): a package only the vendor has installs and updates from it as from any source; a package the system or sndstation also has never comes from it, at any version: a vendor's source adds packages, it never replaces one. vendor-off removes both files, and notes the vendor's id in /etc/sndstation/vendors-off . What the vendor installed stays, and gets no more updates; removing it is the package list's. Being in the package is what makes a vendor trusted. The project vets a vendor before its definition goes in, and the package's install script turns on every vendor it holds. The page has no trust step. What this means for a unit: every vendor's source is asked on each check for updates, so a vendor whose server is down shows as did not answer , in red, until it is back; nothing of a vendor's is installed until a user gets it from its list: a source adds packages to choose from, and updates what was chosen; the pin (above) still holds: a vendor never replaces a package of the system's or sndstation's. Removing a vendor is the owner's. Y on a vendor's row asks first, then runs vendor-off : its packages are no longer listed or updated, what it installed stays, and its server is no longer asked, not even whether it answers. Y works on a vendor that is not set up yet too, so the package does not turn it on later. The vendor stays removed through every upgrade of sndstation-vendors , until A on its row ( vendor-on ) adds it back, with no question. Removing works offline; adding back fetches the vendor's lists, so it waits for a network. The definitions reach a unit with their package , sndstation-vendors , and so do their changes, with nobody editing a file. The platform builds it: packaging/make-vendors-deb.sh , which packaging/make-deb.sh runs, from packaging/vendors/ (its README says what a definition holds) and from any directory a build passes with --vendors DIR , for vendors an app, an image or a distribution offers that the platform does not. An id defined twice stops the build; a vendor with no source or no key yet is left out, and said. its version moves with the vendors: the platform's revision, or the last change to a vendors directory when that is later, so apt takes a new definition (a vendor's beta ) as an upgrade; installing or upgrading it writes the source of every vendor it holds from the definition it brings (its postinst runs sndstation-updates vendor-on ID for each), and removes the source of a vendor it no longer holds, whose key went with its definition, when the helper wrote that source. A file under that name that someone wrote by hand, for a vendor the package never held, is left alone. A vendor the owner removed (its id is in /etc/sndstation/vendors-off ) is left removed. A source edited by hand is replaced then, and only then; removing it removes the source and pin of every vendor it held (its postrm ), and nothing it did not write. What the vendors installed stays, and so does the list of vendors the owner removed, unless the package is purged; a unit whose sndstation-vendors is 0.16.1 or earlier has sources only for the vendors that were trusted on it. There, A on a vendor's row writes its source, asking nothing, and then lists its packages; its row says not set up yet until then; a source the package wrote during an upgrade has no lists yet. Back from the UPDATES page, the page sees a vendor that is on and was not at its last look, and asks every source again before it shows a count. Packages, for vendors and for sndstation Everything the sndstation source and each vendor's has is listed, in every component its source file names (a vendor's beta with its main ), apart from the platform itself ( sndstation , sndstation-vendors ), which updates with the system. A package can say what it is, in a field of its own, which the page shows: XB-Sndstation-Kind: clap-plugin (in debian/control; app, clap-plugin, sounds or skin) which is Sndstation-Kind: clap-plugin in the .deb and in the source's index; one without it is listed as a package . Installing one through the page installs the version its source has ( apt-get install NAME=VERSION ), never the system's package of the same name, and removing one never takes a package of the system's or the platform itself with it. A .deb file is installed through the page only with the field (below). Get all : START on a source's list gets every package of it not installed yet (a demo whose full version is installed is not one), after one card that names them and what they download. They install one after the other, each its own helper call, checked against its source as one installed alone is; the first that fails stops it there, and the page says which and how many were got before it. One install after another, at once : after every install the page reads what updates are pending again, from apt's state with no lock, and an install started meanwhile runs beside it rather than waiting; the list is read once more after it. Only a check of the sources, an upgrade or a repair holds an install back, and the page says so. A demo and its full version are two packages, so that an update of the demo can never take the full one's place: in the vendor's source as a file, for the buyer name soundworks-piano soundworks-piano-full control Sndstation-Kind: clap-plugin Sndstation-Kind: clap-plugin , Provides: soundworks-piano , Conflicts: soundworks-piano , Replaces: soundworks-piano Installing the full one removes the demo (it conflicts with it, and replaces its files). Its Provides keeps everything that depended on the demo satisfied, and the page shows the demo as full version installed , with nothing to get. The full version's own updates come as new files, from the vendor. Removing one never takes anything of the system's with it: the helper refuses when apt would also remove a package that is not one of the platform's, and says which. A package's dependencies stay installed after it is removed ( apt autoremove , from a shell, clears them). A .deb file Install a .deb file opens the file browser on .deb files. Picking one shows what it is before anything installs ( dpkg-deb -f ): its name, version, kind, maker, what it replaces, its size, what it needs, and its description; and, when it is already installed, which version. Then: refused : not one of the platform's (no Sndstation-Kind ), or built for another architecture; otherwise A installs it, with apt-get install /path/to/file.deb , so what it needs comes from the sources. A package's scripts run as root, like the system's own: the card says so, and says to install only a file from a vendor you trust. Publishing a source tools/apt-repo.sh , on the publisher's own machine (the signing key stays there; nothing publishes from CI): export REPO_KEY=you@example.org # the gpg key that signs it tools/apt-repo.sh add ~/apt/sndstation dist/*.deb tools/apt-repo.sh upload ~/apt/sndstation s3://sndstation tools/apt-repo.sh key ~/apt/sndstation > packaging/sndstation-archive-keyring.asc add puts packages in the pool (a version is never replaced, only added) and writes and signs the index ( InRelease , Release.gpg ) for arm64, armhf and amd64; upload copies the pool, then the index, with the index never cached. REPO_LABEL (default sndstation ) is the label UPDATES groups by. A vendor runs the same with its own key, label and bucket. On S3 , a unit reads the source over plain HTTPS, so dists/* and pool/* must be readable by anyone. A new bucket blocks that ( Block public access ): turn off the two settings about bucket policies (the ACL ones can stay on), then give it a policy that allows reading those two folders and nothing else: { \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\", \"Statement\": [{ \"Sid\": \"AptRead\", \"Effect\": \"Allow\", \"Principal\": \"*\", \"Action\": \"s3:GetObject\", \"Resource\": [\"arn:aws:s3:::sndstation/dists/*\", \"arn:aws:s3:::sndstation/pool/*\"] }] } curl -I https://sndstation.s3.us-west-1.amazonaws.com/dists/stable/InRelease then answers 200 (or 404 before the first upload), not 403. On GitHub , a source can be served from GitHub Pages the same way (the directory apt-repo.sh writes, pushed to the Pages branch). A release's assets cannot: apt needs the dists/ and pool/ paths as they are. The updates How others do it, and why this follows the command line What it shows Ubuntu (Software Updater) a total and an Install button; packages, versions and changelogs behind \"Details\". Security updates also install in the background by themselves ( unattended-upgrades ), and some updates are phased , reaching a unit days after others. The least shown. Debian desktops (GNOME Software, KDE Discover) one \"OS updates\" block, downloaded in the background and installed on the next restart. Synaptic and apt for the detail. Linux Mint (Update Manager) every package: its type (security, kernel), old and new version, size, source, description and changelog; packages can be ignored for good; a snapshot is suggested first; nothing automatic unless asked. The most shown. Raspberry Pi OS a list of packages from a panel icon, then an install. Handheld images often their own update scripts, and a warning against apt upgrade : it can replace the kernel, the device tree and the GPU's blobs the image depends on. This page takes Mint's openness on the command line's own path: it is apt-get underneath, with nothing in between that decides on the user's behalf. Its safeguards are the handheld's (below), not hidden choices. The page the list the updates, grouped by where they come from (below), each with its versions, size, one-line description and tags: security , new (another update needs it), held , restart . The header says when it was checked and how many sources answered. A: a package; START: install; Y: hold or release it; X: check again; SELECT: the sources. a package its versions, source, sizes, what its tags mean, and its changelog ( apt-get changelog , fetched from its source). sources each apt source and whether the last check reached it, apt's warnings, and its whole output. confirm how many packages, what they fetch, from which sources; what stays (held, kept back); Y to take the kept-back ones too, which is a full upgrade and names everything it would remove . Refused on a battery under 30% that is not charging. installing one bar (downloading its first half, installing its second), the step at hand (\"Unpacking openssl\"), and as many of apt's latest lines as the screen has room for; Y shows apt's log whole. It cannot be left or stopped: dpkg cut off halfway is how a system stops starting. done what it came to: a restart needed, a configuration file someone changed whose new version was set aside, a package dpkg could not finish (A repairs it), the log. Groups Security : anything from a -security suite or a source labeled so. Each supplemental source , under its own name, in the order config/updates.json gives ( sources ): sndstation's, an instrument maker's, a trusted developer's. Any other source , by the label apt gives it. System : the base system's sources ( system in updates.json ). Kept back : what apt-get upgrade leaves, because it needs a package added or removed (a full upgrade takes it), or because it is held. A source is named by the Label of its Release file, as apt-get -s upgrade prints it before the colon ( Debian-Security:12/stable-security ). On the system side /usr/lib/sndstation/sndstation-updates ( tools/sndstation-updates ), run through sudo -n by the same sudo rule as Network's and Storage's: Verb Does As refresh apt-get update , each source and how it went, and what each that answered calls itself (its Release file's Label , else Origin ) root list apt-get -s upgrade --with-new-pkgs and -s dist-upgrade (simulations), apt-mark showhold , apt-cache show for sizes and descriptions the user upgrade [full] apt-get upgrade --with-new-pkgs (what apt upgrade does: may install new packages, never removes one), or with full apt-get dist-upgrade root repair dpkg --configure -a , then apt-get -f install root changelog PKG apt-get changelog PKG the user hold PKG , unhold PKG apt-mark hold / unhold root holds-default holds the kernel, the device tree and the boot loader, once root available the sndstation source and each vendor (on, off, or removed by the owner), and every package of those that are on, in every component (read from apt's lists), each with its kind ( Sndstation-Kind , or none), its version, the one installed, and the installed package standing in for it (a full version's Provides ); then those installed from files the user install PKG , remove PKG apt-get install NAME=VERSION of a package one of those sources has, at its version there, or of one with a Sndstation-Kind ; apt-get remove of one of those, never taking the system's or the platform with it root inspect FILE dpkg-deb -f on a .deb : its fields, and whether it may be installed the user install-file FILE apt-get install FILE , when it is one of the platform's root vendor-on ID , vendor-off ID write a vendor's source, pinned below the system's, or remove it. The sndstation-vendors package runs vendor-on for every vendor it holds; the page runs it for one whose source is not written yet, or to add one back. vendor-off first notes the id in /etc/sndstation/vendors-off , so the package leaves that vendor removed, and vendor-on takes it out again; it refuses an id that has neither a definition nor a source root ping whether each vendor whose source is not written yet answers: its InRelease (else Release ) fetched with apt-helper download-file and thrown away, with the same 10-second limit; refresh asks the others, and one the owner removed is not asked at all the user A check says what it is doing while it runs, on the page and on the launcher's console: which step (holding the kernel, contacting the sources, reading what is installable), for how long, and each source as it answers. A source that does not answer gives up after 10 seconds, not retried ( Acquire::http(s)::Timeout , Acquire::Retries ), rather than apt's minutes: the next check tries it again. On the console: updates: refresh (as root) updates: ok http://deb.debian.org/debian trixie updates: FAIL https://apt.example.com stable updates: refresh: exit 4 in 11.3 s It answers in lines ( updates 1 , then tab-separated records and key=value ), so it can be run from a shell: /usr/lib/sndstation/sndstation-updates list . An install reports apt's progress through APT::Status-Fd as it comes, one progress record a step, and apt's log as log records; the page reads both live. Installing never asks a question, which on a handheld would hang it: DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive , and for a configuration file someone changed, --force-confdef --force-confold : theirs is kept and the package's new version is set aside as .dpkg-dist , which the page names. Nothing is installed for being recommended: every apt-get it runs has APT::Install-Recommends=false , list 's simulations too. A package's Recommends are mostly a desktop's (a file dialog's zenity , and the GTK under it); what a unit needs is a Depends . Security: what the launcher passes in is a package name or a vendor's id, checked against Debian's rules for a name before it reaches any command, or a .deb file's whole path, which dpkg-deb must read as a package that says it is one of the platform's. Installing a file is installing its scripts as root, which is the unit's owner's call: the sudo rule is a handheld's, whose user is its owner. Under sudo the environment is reset, so its test override is not honored there. What is held, and why The first check holds the installed packages matching linux-image-* , linux-dtb-* , linux-u-boot-* and u-boot* , once (a mark in /var/lib/sndstation ), so a release later is not undone. On these units the kernel and device tree carry the audio routing and the display; a new one from the base system can lose either. A unit's own list, one glob a line, can be put at /etc/sndstation/update-holds . Holds are apt's own ( apt-mark ), so apt upgrade from a shell keeps them too. Y releases one. The background check While the launcher is on its own pages (nothing playing, no game), it checks at most every check_hours (24): apt-get update , then the list. SETTINGS then says \"6 updates, 2 security\". What it found is kept in ~/.config/sndstation/updates-state.json , whether every source answered included, so the next start says it without checking again. Offline, it does not check. What is pending follows what is installed, without the network: the list ( apt-get -s upgrade , from the lists apt already has) is read again after anything installed or removed here, at the first idle moment of each run, and whenever dpkg's database ( /var/lib/dpkg/status ) changes, an apt upgrade at a shell included. An update installed any way stops being offered at once, rather than at the next check. A check that fails (offline) is tried again after an hour. Opening the page checks, unless this run checked within the hour. check_hours: 0 checks only when the page is opened. Supplemental sources The way Debian adds a source, unchanged: /etc/apt/sources.list.d/<name>.sources , with Signed-By: naming its key. sndstation's is shipped by its package; a vendor's is written when its package is installed (above). Each one's updates are named by the label its Release file gives, or by the name updates.json gives that label. Not here Installing anything unasked , security updates included. A unit mid-performance must not change under its player. Restarting. The page says when an update needs one. Phased updates : the page shows what apt would install, phasing and all; a phased update is not listed until apt offers it. Snapshots before an install (as Mint suggests): most units' file systems cannot take one. Development sndstation --fake-updates runs the page on made-up updates (a security update, a supplemental source's, a new package, a held kernel, one that only a full upgrade takes), installed slowly enough to watch; and on made-up packages: the sndstation source's, a vendor with its own, one whose source is not written and does not answer, a full version installed from a file, and .deb files (a path ending in -full.deb is one to install, any other is refused). The shots ( --shots , 21h–21u) use it. The tests: tests/test_updates.sh runs the helper against stub apt-get , apt-cache , apt-mark , dpkg , dpkg-query and dpkg-deb , over a fake root with apt's lists and a vendor's files; tests/test_updates.cpp reads its output and drives both pages on the fake.","stub":false},{"u":"sndstation/docs/usb-networking.html","t":"USB networking","s":"Subsystems","h":["USB networking","What it is for","The three modes","Connected to my PC","Connected to a hub","As a host","Remembering","Which units can do which mode","Underneath","When it does not come up","Not done"],"x":"USB networking Part of the sndstation design. Built and shipping: tools/sndstation-netctl ( usb-* commands), the launcher's SETTINGS → Network → USB networking page ( launcher/settings.cpp , launcher/netctl.cpp ), and system/sndstation-usbnet.service . The page's own behavior is in launcher.md ; this is how the whole thing works. What it is for A network over a USB cable, with no Wi-Fi: a unit and the computer it is plugged into, or several units on one USB hub. On a hub, one unit is the host and the others plug into it, directly or through the hub. Units on it reach each other at fixed or handed-out addresses, so an app on one can talk to an app on another (ssh, a game's TCP connection, anything IP). The three modes Mode The unit is Its address Hands out addresses Connected to my PC a USB Ethernet device 10.55.0.1 to the computer Connected to a hub a USB Ethernet device from the host, 10.56.0.x no, it asks As a host a USB host with a bridge 10.56.0.1 10.56.0.10 – 254 Picking a mode turns off whichever was on first. Every unit starts off after a power-on unless Remember network settings is on. No mode hands out a route or DNS. Each one reaches the others; nobody's own traffic goes through a unit. A wired default route outranks Wi-Fi, so one handed out here would quietly take over a laptop's internet. Hub mode refuses a route and DNS from whatever answers it, for the same reason. Connected to my PC The port becomes a USB Ethernet gadget with one CDC NCM function (Windows 10 and later, macOS and Linux drive it with no extra driver; CDC ECM on a kernel without NCM). NetworkManager's shared mode runs dnsmasq and gives the computer an address. Without dnsmasq in the image, give the computer 10.55.0.2/24 by hand. ssh sst@10.55.0.1 reaches the unit once SSH is on. Connected to a hub The same gadget, but the unit asks for an address and keeps asking: with no cable in yet, with no host answering, after an unplug, after the host restarts. The page says it is waiting until a host answers. Its gadget MACs come from the unit's /etc/machine-id (host side 02:… , device side 06:… ), the same every time and different on every unit, so several units on one hub never collide. Release images ship an empty machine-id, so each card makes its own at first boot. As a host The port is a USB host. A bridge, br-usb , takes in every USB Ethernet link that appears on it (drivers cdc_ether , cdc_ncm , rndis_host ), including ones already plugged in when host mode starts, and hands out addresses. The page counts the links on the bridge as joined . Any USB Ethernet joins, not only other units: a USB Ethernet adapter, a phone's USB tethering, or a unit left in \"connected to my PC\". While host mode is on, an adapter on this unit is part of the bridge, not a network of its own. Remembering Remember network settings is off until the user turns it on. While it is on, the mode in use comes back at boot: sndstation-usbnet.service (a oneshot after NetworkManager) runs sndstation-netctl usb-boot , which reads /etc/sndstation/usb-network and sets that mode. Changing mode while remembering updates it; turning it on while off remembers off . A remembered mode the unit can no longer do is left off, and the reason goes to the journal. Wi-Fi and SSH are not part of it: NetworkManager already keeps Wi-Fi networks, and SSH stays on through restarts on its own. Which units can do which mode The device modes (PC, hub) need a port with a device mode: a UDC under /sys/class/udc , or a USB role switch under /sys/class/usb_role that can make one, and the kernel's libcomposite . Host mode needs a USB host controller with a port of its own beside the UDC's (H700 units have a separate host-only EHCI port), or a role switch. One UDC takes one gadget. If another gadget already holds it (a boot service from outside sndstation, say), the device modes are not offered and the page says \"The USB port is in use by another USB gadget ( name )\". The page lists only what the unit can do, and says why for the rest. Role switches (RK3326). The port is told to be a device for the device modes and a host for host mode, and put back as it was afterward. A device port is no host: a USB Wi-Fi or Ethernet adapter on it stops working, and the page says so. Host mode refuses on a role-switch port while another gadget holds it. RK3326 cards need a device-tree overlay. The harvested RK3326 device trees (all but the GKD Pixel 2's) leave the USB PHY's OTG port disabled, so the device modes bind but nothing reaches the cable. The fix is an overlay that sets /syscon@ff2c0000/usb2phy@100/otg-port to okay , applied from the card's config.txt ( overlays= ). It belongs to the card image, not to this package. Underneath Everything runs through the root helper, as the launcher is never root: sndstation-netctl usb-status the usb section of `status`, alone sndstation-netctl usb-mode off|pc|hub|host usb-on and usb-off are pc and off sndstation-netctl usb-remember on|off sndstation-netctl usb-boot the remembered mode, at boot usb-status (and the usb section of status ) says: Key Meaning supported , why the device modes, and why not host , hostwhy host mode, and why not mode off , pc , hub , host active , ip on, and the unit's address (empty while a hub waits) joined host mode: links on the bridge remember yes or no role=switch the port has a role switch What it makes: the gadget: configfs /sys/kernel/config/usb_gadget/sndstation NetworkManager connections, all made with save no , so none outlives a restart: sndstation-usb (PC and hub), and for host mode the bridge sndstation-usbhost and its port profile sndstation-usbhost-port ( match.driver , multi-connect multiple ) dnsmasq's drop-in /etc/NetworkManager/dnsmasq-shared.d/sndstation-usb.conf ( dhcp-option=3 , dhcp-option=6 : no router, no DNS) in PC and host mode /run/sndstation-usb-mode (the mode in use) and /run/sndstation-usb-role (a role switch's old role) /etc/sndstation/usb-network while remembering, removed on purge NetworkManager leaves USB gadget interfaces unmanaged (its udev rule expects whoever made the gadget to set it up). PC mode activates its connection explicitly. Hub mode also sets the device managed and makes its connection autoconnect, so it comes up whenever a host answers. When it does not come up Nothing on the host side at all ( lsusb shows nothing, the hub's port reads not attached in /sys/bus/usb/devices/<hub>/<hub>-port*/state ): the cable or the port, or, on RK3326, the overlay above. The link is there but no address: ping every IPv6 node on it, ping -6 ff02::1%br-usb on the host, to see who is on it with no IPv4 at all, then ssh to the answer. Who has joined, by name: ip link show master br-usb , and the leases in /var/lib/NetworkManager/dnsmasq-br-usb.leases . Not done Routing a hub's units out to the internet through the host (shared mode's NAT rules also need nftables expressions these kernels lack). Showing an address on the page for a gadget the launcher did not make.","stub":false}];
